{
  "1676": {
    "id": 1676,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 294,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG, YI DVAGS KYIS CHU KLUNG LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, CHU KLUNG BSKAMS PA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA'I DE'I MIG SHES DANG , 'BRAS BU CAN GYI LJON PA LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, 'BRAS BU MED PA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA'I DE'I MIG SHES TSAD MA YOD PAR THAL, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA'I YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES TSAD MA YOD PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig, yi dvags kyis chu klung la bltas pa de'i tshe, chu klung bskams pa la tshad mar song ba'i de'i mig shes dang , 'bras bu can gyi ljon pa la bltas pa de'i tshe, 'bras bu med pa la tshad mar song ba'i de'i mig shes tshad ma yod par thal, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la bltas pa de'i tshe, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa la tshad mar song ba'i yi dvags kyi mig shes tshad ma yod pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Yet again, another argument might be made: Let's take the case of one of those craving spirits that looks at a river of water, and sees it as a dry riverbed, genuinely so. Or consider one that looks at a tree loaded with fruit, but sees it as nothing but bare limbs, genuinely so. The visual consciousness of both of these beings then must be a valid perception. Why? Remember the case of the three different beings looking at a glass full of something wet and flowing; according to you, the pus and blood was actual pus and blood, and the perception of them by the craving spirit was genuine: his visual consciousness was a valid perception.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG, YI DVAGS KYIS CHU KLUNG LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, CHU KLUNG BSKAMS PA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA'I DE'I MIG SHES DANG , 'BRAS BU CAN GYI LJON PA LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, 'BRAS BU MED PA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA'I DE'I MIG SHES TSAD MA YOD PAR THAL, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA'I YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES TSAD MA YOD PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig, yi dvags kyis chu klung la bltas pa de'i tshe, chu klung bskams pa la tshad mar song ba'i de'i mig shes dang , 'bras bu can gyi ljon pa la bltas pa de'i tshe, 'bras bu med pa la tshad mar song ba'i de'i mig shes tshad ma yod par thal, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la bltas pa de'i tshe, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa la tshad mar song ba'i yi dvags kyi mig shes tshad ma yod pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Yet again, another argument might be made: Let's take the case of one of those craving spirits that looks at a river of water, and sees it as a dry riverbed, genuinely so. Or consider one that looks at a tree loaded with fruit, but sees it as nothing but bare limbs, genuinely so. The visual consciousness of both of these beings then must be a valid perception. Why? Remember the case of the three different beings looking at a glass full of something wet and flowing; according to you, the pus and blood was actual pus and blood, and the perception of them by the craving spirit was genuine: his visual consciousness was a valid perception.\"}"
  },
  "1677": {
    "id": 1677,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 295,
    "acip": "ZER NA MA KHYAB STE, DANG POR YI DVAGS KYIS CHU MA MTHONG NA PHYOGS DER LONGS SPYOD PA'I PHYIR, 'GRO BA YANG MI 'THAD PAS DANG POR CHU MTHONG YANG , PHYIS LAS KYI DBANG GIS CHU MA MTHONG BAR 'OG GZHI GRAM SREG TZAM ZHIG MTHONG ZHING , DE'I RJES SU RTOG PAS CHU BSKAMS PAR ZHEN PA DANG , DE BZHIN DU DANG POR LJON SHING GI 'BRAS BU MTHONG YANG PHYIS LAS KYI DBANG GIS 'BRAS BU MA MTHONG BAR YAL GA TZAM ZHIG MTHONG ZHING , DE'I RJES SU RTOG PAS 'BRAS BU MED PAR ZHEN PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "zer na ma khyab ste, dang por yi dvags kyis chu ma mthong na phyogs der longs spyod pa'i phyir, 'gro ba yang mi 'thad pas dang por chu mthong yang , phyis las kyi dbang gis chu ma mthong bar 'og gzhi gram sreg tsam zhig mthong zhing , de'i rjes su rtog pas chu bskams par zhen pa dang , de bzhin du dang por ljon shing gi 'bras bu mthong yang phyis las kyi dbang gis 'bras bu ma mthong bar yal ga tsam zhig mthong zhing , de'i rjes su rtog pas 'bras bu med par zhen pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Just because we said that about the other case doesn't mean that it's true in every case. If the craving spirit hadn't seen any water in that area in the first place, it wouldn't have made any sense for him to go over in that direction to try to enjoy some of the water. Therefore what happened was that, at first, he saw some water. Later on, he was forced by his karma to stop seeing water and saw only bare, parched earth. Then he had an impression where he thought the water had dried up. The case with the fruit tree is the same. Although at first the craving spirit sees a tree loaded with fruit, later on his karma forces him to stop seeing fruit, and all he sees is bare branches. Then he has an impression where he thinks that the tree has no fruit any more.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"ZER NA MA KHYAB STE, DANG POR YI DVAGS KYIS CHU MA MTHONG NA PHYOGS DER LONGS SPYOD PA'I PHYIR, 'GRO BA YANG MI 'THAD PAS DANG POR CHU MTHONG YANG , PHYIS LAS KYI DBANG GIS CHU MA MTHONG BAR 'OG GZHI GRAM SREG TZAM ZHIG MTHONG ZHING , DE'I RJES SU RTOG PAS CHU BSKAMS PAR ZHEN PA DANG , DE BZHIN DU DANG POR LJON SHING GI 'BRAS BU MTHONG YANG PHYIS LAS KYI DBANG GIS 'BRAS BU MA MTHONG BAR YAL GA TZAM ZHIG MTHONG ZHING , DE'I RJES SU RTOG PAS 'BRAS BU MED PAR ZHEN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"zer na ma khyab ste, dang por yi dvags kyis chu ma mthong na phyogs der longs spyod pa'i phyir, 'gro ba yang mi 'thad pas dang por chu mthong yang , phyis las kyi dbang gis chu ma mthong bar 'og gzhi gram sreg tsam zhig mthong zhing , de'i rjes su rtog pas chu bskams par zhen pa dang , de bzhin du dang por ljon shing gi 'bras bu mthong yang phyis las kyi dbang gis 'bras bu ma mthong bar yal ga tsam zhig mthong zhing , de'i rjes su rtog pas 'bras bu med par zhen pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Just because we said that about the other case doesn't mean that it's true in every case. If the craving spirit hadn't seen any water in that area in the first place, it wouldn't have made any sense for him to go over in that direction to try to enjoy some of the water. Therefore what happened was that, at first, he saw some water. Later on, he was forced by his karma to stop seeing water and saw only bare, parched earth. Then he had an impression where he thought the water had dried up. The case with the fruit tree is the same. Although at first the craving spirit sees a tree loaded with fruit, later on his karma forces him to stop seeing fruit, and all he sees is bare branches. Then he has an impression where he thinks that the tree has no fruit any more.\"}"
  },
  "1678": {
    "id": 1678,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 296,
    "acip": "DE'I TSE NA, YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES KYI SGRIB BYED KYIS CHU KLUNG MA MTHONG BAS, 'OG GZHI GRAM SREG MTHONG BA YIN TE, MI'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG SGRIB BYED KYIS CHU KLUNG MA MTHONG NA, 'OG GZHI GRAM SREG MTHONG DGOS PA LTA BU YIN PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "de'i tshe na, yi dvags kyi mig shes kyi sgrib byed kyis chu klung ma mthong bas, 'og gzhi gram sreg mthong ba yin te, mi'i mig shes kyis kyang sgrib byed kyis chu klung ma mthong na, 'og gzhi gram sreg mthong dgos pa lta bu yin pa'i phyir",
    "english": "When all this is happening, the obstacle in the visual consciousness of the craving spirit prevents him from seeing the river of water, and so he sees a dry, parched riverbed. The same is true for the visual consciousness of a human: if the obstacle were there, it would prevent him from seeing the river of water, and then he would have to see a dry, parched riverbed.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"DE'I TSE NA, YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES KYI SGRIB BYED KYIS CHU KLUNG MA MTHONG BAS, 'OG GZHI GRAM SREG MTHONG BA YIN TE, MI'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG SGRIB BYED KYIS CHU KLUNG MA MTHONG NA, 'OG GZHI GRAM SREG MTHONG DGOS PA LTA BU YIN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"de'i tshe na, yi dvags kyi mig shes kyi sgrib byed kyis chu klung ma mthong bas, 'og gzhi gram sreg mthong ba yin te, mi'i mig shes kyis kyang sgrib byed kyis chu klung ma mthong na, 'og gzhi gram sreg mthong dgos pa lta bu yin pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"When all this is happening, the obstacle in the visual consciousness of the craving spirit prevents him from seeing the river of water, and so he sees a dry, parched riverbed. The same is true for the visual consciousness of a human: if the obstacle were there, it would prevent him from seeing the river of water, and then he would have to see a dry, parched riverbed.\"}"
  },
  "1679": {
    "id": 1679,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 297,
    "acip": "RNAM PA GCIG TU NA, YI DVAGS KYIS CHU KLUNG LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DE MA BSKAMS KYANG , RANG GI BLTA BYAR GYUR PA'I CHU KLUNG DANG , LONGS SPYOD BYAR GYUR PA'I CHU KLUNG BSKAMS PA YOD DE, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, LAS KYI DBANG GIS RNAG KHRAG SOGS DNGOS PO RNAM PA GSUM CIG CAR DU GRUB PA ZHIG YOD PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "rnam pa gcig tu na, yi dvags kyis chu klung la bltas pa de'i tshe na de ma bskams kyang , rang gi blta byar gyur pa'i chu klung dang , longs spyod byar gyur pa'i chu klung bskams pa yod de, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, las kyi dbang gis rnag khrag sogs dngos po rnam pa gsum cig car du grub pa zhig yod pa'i phyir",
    "english": "From one point of view, the river of water hasn't dried up when the craving spirit looks at it; but there is a river which has dried up, if you're talking about a river of water that the spirit can see, or a river of water that the spirit can drink from. This follows because when the three different types of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing, it is true that, due to the force of karma, three different kinds of objects exist there at the same time: the pus and blood, and the other two.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"RNAM PA GCIG TU NA, YI DVAGS KYIS CHU KLUNG LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DE MA BSKAMS KYANG , RANG GI BLTA BYAR GYUR PA'I CHU KLUNG DANG , LONGS SPYOD BYAR GYUR PA'I CHU KLUNG BSKAMS PA YOD DE, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, LAS KYI DBANG GIS RNAG KHRAG SOGS DNGOS PO RNAM PA GSUM CIG CAR DU GRUB PA ZHIG YOD PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"rnam pa gcig tu na, yi dvags kyis chu klung la bltas pa de'i tshe na de ma bskams kyang , rang gi blta byar gyur pa'i chu klung dang , longs spyod byar gyur pa'i chu klung bskams pa yod de, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, las kyi dbang gis rnag khrag sogs dngos po rnam pa gsum cig car du grub pa zhig yod pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"From one point of view, the river of water hasn't dried up when the craving spirit looks at it; but there is a river which has dried up, if you're talking about a river of water that the spirit can see, or a river of water that the spirit can drink from. This follows because when the three different types of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing, it is true that, due to the force of karma, three different kinds of objects exist there at the same time: the pus and blood, and the other two.\"}"
  },
  "1680": {
    "id": 1680,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 298,
    "acip": "DE LTAR BYAS NA, ZAS SKOM NYID LA YOD PA'I SGRIB PA CAN GYI YI DVAGS KYIS LONGS SPYOD PA LA MA BRTZAMS KYI BAR DU BZA' BTUNG DNGOS YIN KYANG , DES LONGS SPYOD PAR BRTZAMS PA NA, ZAS DE'I RIGS 'DRA PHYI MA RNAG KHRAG TU SKYE BA YIN GYI, RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM GYIS BZA' BTUNG GI BYA BA BYED PA MA YIN TE, DE YIN NA LAS 'BRAS LA SKUR BA BTAB PAR 'GYUR BA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DE LTA BU'I YI DVAGS KYIS LAS 'BRAS LA SKUR BA BTAB PAR 'GYUR BA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "de ltar byas na, zas skom nyid la yod pa'i sgrib pa can gyi yi dvags kyis longs spyod pa la ma brtsams kyi bar du bza' btung dngos yin kyang , des longs spyod par brtsams pa na, zas de'i rigs 'dra phyi ma rnag khrag tu skye ba yin gyi, rnag khrag tu snang ba'i snang ba tsam gyis bza' btung gi bya ba byed pa ma yin te, de yin na las 'bras la skur ba btab par 'gyur ba'i phyir, der thal, de lta bu'i yi dvags kyis las 'bras la skur ba btab par 'gyur ba'i phyir",
    "english": "Given all this, consider craving spirits that have obstacles that relate to their food and drink itself. The food and drink there really is food and drink, until such time as the spirit starts trying to eat or drink it. When he does try to do so, then the continuum of the food into the next moment starts becoming pus and blood. It is not though that it is the simple appearance of something as pus and blood that could ever function as something to eat or drink. If this were the case, then the rules of karma and its consequences would have to be less that what they really are. And this is true, for if a craving spirit like this ever existed it would represent a failure of the laws of karma and its consequences.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"DE LTAR BYAS NA, ZAS SKOM NYID LA YOD PA'I SGRIB PA CAN GYI YI DVAGS KYIS LONGS SPYOD PA LA MA BRTZAMS KYI BAR DU BZA' BTUNG DNGOS YIN KYANG , DES LONGS SPYOD PAR BRTZAMS PA NA, ZAS DE'I RIGS 'DRA PHYI MA RNAG KHRAG TU SKYE BA YIN GYI, RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM GYIS BZA' BTUNG GI BYA BA BYED PA MA YIN TE, DE YIN NA LAS 'BRAS LA SKUR BA BTAB PAR 'GYUR BA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DE LTA BU'I YI DVAGS KYIS LAS 'BRAS LA SKUR BA BTAB PAR 'GYUR BA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"de ltar byas na, zas skom nyid la yod pa'i sgrib pa can gyi yi dvags kyis longs spyod pa la ma brtsams kyi bar du bza' btung dngos yin kyang , des longs spyod par brtsams pa na, zas de'i rigs 'dra phyi ma rnag khrag tu skye ba yin gyi, rnag khrag tu snang ba'i snang ba tsam gyis bza' btung gi bya ba byed pa ma yin te, de yin na las 'bras la skur ba btab par 'gyur ba'i phyir, der thal, de lta bu'i yi dvags kyis las 'bras la skur ba btab par 'gyur ba'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Given all this, consider craving spirits that have obstacles that relate to their food and drink itself. The food and drink there really is food and drink, until such time as the spirit starts trying to eat or drink it. When he does try to do so, then the continuum of the food into the next moment starts becoming pus and blood. It is not though that it is the simple appearance of something as pus and blood that could ever function as something to eat or drink. If this were the case, then the rules of karma and its consequences would have to be less that what they really are. And this is true, for if a craving spirit like this ever existed it would represent a failure of the laws of karma and its consequences.\"}"
  },
  "1681": {
    "id": 1681,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 299,
    "acip": "GZHAN YANG , SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG MED CING DER SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM GYIS BZA' BTUNG GI BYA BA BYED NA, RAB RIB CAN LA BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD SNANG BAS 'CHING BA DANG , DE LA SNANG BA'I SBRANG BUS LUS ZA BA DANG , SMIG RGYU'I CHUS CHU'I BYA BA BYED NUS PA SOGS YIN PAR THAL, SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG MED KYANG , RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM GYIS BZA' BTUNG GI BYA BA BYED NUS PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "gzhan yang , snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag med cing der snang ba'i snang ba tsam gyis bza' btung gi bya ba byed na, rab rib can la bse ru'i nang du skra shad snang bas 'ching ba dang , de la snang ba'i sbrang bus lus za ba dang , smig rgyu'i chus chu'i bya ba byed nus pa sogs yin par thal, snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag med kyang , rnag khrag tu snang ba'i snang ba tsam gyis bza' btung gi bya ba byed nus pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Suppose moreover that there were no pus and blood out there to appear as the pus and blood, and suppose that the mere appearance of something looking like pus and blood could ever function as something to eat or drink. Well then, you would also have to be able to use a comb on the hair that appears on a porcelain sink to a person with cataracts. And a horsefly that appeared to the same person would have to be able to give him a bite. And the water of a mirage would have to provide all the normal functions of water, and so on. Why so? Well because, according to you, there is no pus and blood out there to appear as pus and blood; according to you, the mere appearance of something looking like pus and blood can provide all the functions of things that you eat and drink.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"GZHAN YANG , SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG MED CING DER SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM GYIS BZA' BTUNG GI BYA BA BYED NA, RAB RIB CAN LA BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD SNANG BAS 'CHING BA DANG , DE LA SNANG BA'I SBRANG BUS LUS ZA BA DANG , SMIG RGYU'I CHUS CHU'I BYA BA BYED NUS PA SOGS YIN PAR THAL, SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG MED KYANG , RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM GYIS BZA' BTUNG GI BYA BA BYED NUS PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"gzhan yang , snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag med cing der snang ba'i snang ba tsam gyis bza' btung gi bya ba byed na, rab rib can la bse ru'i nang du skra shad snang bas 'ching ba dang , de la snang ba'i sbrang bus lus za ba dang , smig rgyu'i chus chu'i bya ba byed nus pa sogs yin par thal, snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag med kyang , rnag khrag tu snang ba'i snang ba tsam gyis bza' btung gi bya ba byed nus pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Suppose moreover that there were no pus and blood out there to appear as the pus and blood, and suppose that the mere appearance of something looking like pus and blood could ever function as something to eat or drink. Well then, you would also have to be able to use a comb on the hair that appears on a porcelain sink to a person with cataracts. And a horsefly that appeared to the same person would have to be able to give him a bite. And the water of a mirage would have to provide all the normal functions of water, and so on. Why so? Well because, according to you, there is no pus and blood out there to appear as pus and blood; according to you, the mere appearance of something looking like pus and blood can provide all the functions of things that you eat and drink.\"}"
  },
  "1682": {
    "id": 1682,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 300,
    "acip": "BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA LHA MI YI DVAGS DUD 'GRO SOGS KYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, LHA YI BDUD RTZI SNANG BA'I SNANG BA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA DANG , YI DVAGS LA RNAG KHRAG SNANG BA'I SNANG BA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA YOD KYANG , SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG SOGS MED NA, SEMS CAN DMYAL BA'I MIG SHES DES, DMYAL BA'I LCAGS SREG DANG , RAL GRI'I NAGS TSAL DANG , ME TSOGS LA SOGS PA SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM LA TSAD MAR SONG BA YOD KYI, DE DAG DNGOS GNAS PA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA MED PAR THAL, KHYOD KYI DAM BCA' 'THAD PA GANG ZHIG, RGYU MTSAN MTSUNGS PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la lha mi yi dvags dud 'gro sogs kyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, lha yi bdud rtsi snang ba'i snang ba la tshad mar song ba dang , yi dvags la rnag khrag snang ba'i snang ba la tshad mar song ba yod kyang , snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag sogs med na, sems can dmyal ba'i mig shes des, dmyal ba'i lcags sreg dang , ral gri'i nags tshal dang , me tshogs la sogs pa snang ba'i snang ba tsam la tshad mar song ba yod kyi, de dag dngos gnas pa la tshad mar song ba med par thal, khyod kyi dam bca' 'thad pa gang zhig, rgyu mtshan mtshungs pa'i phyir",
    "english": "And consider again this case where a pleasure being and a human and a craving spirit and an animal or the like all sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. According to you, it would have to be genuine when something that just looked like ambrosia appeared to the pleasure being, and it would have to be genuine when something that just looked like pus and blood appeared to the craving spirit, but there couldn't be any pus and blood out there to appear as pus and blood. And if this were the case, then consider the visual consciousness of a being in the hells. It would then have to be a valid perception towards something appearing to it that just looked like the burning steel of the hells, and towards something that just looked like the forest of swords, and towards something that just looked like a mass of fire, and so on. Finally, this person would not have any valid perceptions at all towards any of these things as actual objects. Why would this all have to be so? If your idea were correct, it would have to be, for the logic here is identical to your own.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA LHA MI YI DVAGS DUD 'GRO SOGS KYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, LHA YI BDUD RTZI SNANG BA'I SNANG BA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA DANG , YI DVAGS LA RNAG KHRAG SNANG BA'I SNANG BA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA YOD KYANG , SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG SOGS MED NA, SEMS CAN DMYAL BA'I MIG SHES DES, DMYAL BA'I LCAGS SREG DANG , RAL GRI'I NAGS TSAL DANG , ME TSOGS LA SOGS PA SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM LA TSAD MAR SONG BA YOD KYI, DE DAG DNGOS GNAS PA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA MED PAR THAL, KHYOD KYI DAM BCA' 'THAD PA GANG ZHIG, RGYU MTSAN MTSUNGS PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la lha mi yi dvags dud 'gro sogs kyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, lha yi bdud rtsi snang ba'i snang ba la tshad mar song ba dang , yi dvags la rnag khrag snang ba'i snang ba la tshad mar song ba yod kyang , snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag sogs med na, sems can dmyal ba'i mig shes des, dmyal ba'i lcags sreg dang , ral gri'i nags tshal dang , me tshogs la sogs pa snang ba'i snang ba tsam la tshad mar song ba yod kyi, de dag dngos gnas pa la tshad mar song ba med par thal, khyod kyi dam bca' 'thad pa gang zhig, rgyu mtshan mtshungs pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"And consider again this case where a pleasure being and a human and a craving spirit and an animal or the like all sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. According to you, it would have to be genuine when something that just looked like ambrosia appeared to the pleasure being, and it would have to be genuine when something that just looked like pus and blood appeared to the craving spirit, but there couldn't be any pus and blood out there to appear as pus and blood. And if this were the case, then consider the visual consciousness of a being in the hells. It would then have to be a valid perception towards something appearing to it that just looked like the burning steel of the hells, and towards something that just looked like the forest of swords, and towards something that just looked like a mass of fire, and so on. Finally, this person would not have any valid perceptions at all towards any of these things as actual objects. Why would this all have to be so? If your idea were correct, it would have to be, for the logic here is identical to your own.\"}"
  },
  "1683": {
    "id": 1683,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 301,
    "acip": "'DOD NA, LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PAS, DER SKYES PA'I 'GRO BA'I LUS SREG PA DANG , GTUB PA LA SOGS PA DNGOS GNAS PA ZHIG MED PAR THAL, 'DOD PA DE'I PHYIR, 'DOD NA, DMYAL BA'I SDUG BSNGAL MED PAR THAL, 'DOD PA DE'I PHYIR, GZHAN YANG DE LTAR CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, MI'I MIG SHES TSAD MAR SONG BA'I CHU DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE'I TSE NA RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "'dod na, lcags sreg la sogs pas, der skyes pa'i 'gro ba'i lus sreg pa dang , gtub pa la sogs pa dngos gnas pa zhig med par thal, 'dod pa de'i phyir, 'dod na, dmyal ba'i sdug bsngal med par thal, 'dod pa de'i phyir, gzhan yang de ltar cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, mi'i mig shes tshad mar song ba'i chu dngos gnas pa med par thal, de'i tshe na rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med pa'i phyir",
    "english": "And suppose you agree that this hell being could have no valid perceptions of the type we mentioned. Well then, the burning steel and other objects could never perform any real actions: they could never burn the bodies of the people born there, they could never chop them up, and so on. Why? Well because of what you just agreed to. And suppose you agree to this; that they could never perform any real actions. Well then, the torment of the hells itself then must not even exist, by your own admission. And remember too that case where the beings all sit down and look at the same thing. You must be saying then that the actual water, the thing towards which the visual consciousness of the human is a valid perception, doesn't exist at all. Why? Well because, according to you, no actual pus and blood exists either in the same situation.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"'DOD NA, LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PAS, DER SKYES PA'I 'GRO BA'I LUS SREG PA DANG , GTUB PA LA SOGS PA DNGOS GNAS PA ZHIG MED PAR THAL, 'DOD PA DE'I PHYIR, 'DOD NA, DMYAL BA'I SDUG BSNGAL MED PAR THAL, 'DOD PA DE'I PHYIR, GZHAN YANG DE LTAR CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, MI'I MIG SHES TSAD MAR SONG BA'I CHU DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE'I TSE NA RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"'dod na, lcags sreg la sogs pas, der skyes pa'i 'gro ba'i lus sreg pa dang , gtub pa la sogs pa dngos gnas pa zhig med par thal, 'dod pa de'i phyir, 'dod na, dmyal ba'i sdug bsngal med par thal, 'dod pa de'i phyir, gzhan yang de ltar cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, mi'i mig shes tshad mar song ba'i chu dngos gnas pa med par thal, de'i tshe na rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"And suppose you agree that this hell being could have no valid perceptions of the type we mentioned. Well then, the burning steel and other objects could never perform any real actions: they could never burn the bodies of the people born there, they could never chop them up, and so on. Why? Well because of what you just agreed to. And suppose you agree to this; that they could never perform any real actions. Well then, the torment of the hells itself then must not even exist, by your own admission. And remember too that case where the beings all sit down and look at the same thing. You must be saying then that the actual water, the thing towards which the visual consciousness of the human is a valid perception, doesn't exist at all. Why? Well because, according to you, no actual pus and blood exists either in the same situation.\"}"
  },
  "1684": {
    "id": 1684,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 302,
    "acip": "KHO NA RE, DE'I TSE NA CHU DNGOS GNAS PA YOD DE, CHUS LUS 'KHRUD PA DANG 'TSOD PAR BYED PA MI RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR ZER NA, 'O NA, DE'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS YOD PAR THAL, DE'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG LA LONGS SPYOD PA DANG , LTO BA DANG MGRIN PA LA SOGS PA 'TSIG PA YI DVAGS RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "kho na re, de'i tshe na chu dngos gnas pa yod de, chus lus 'khrud pa dang 'tshod par byed pa mi rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir zer na, 'o na, de'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas yod par thal, de'i tshe na, rnag khrag la longs spyod pa dang , lto ba dang mgrin pa la sogs pa 'tshig pa yi dvags rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Someone might now make the following claim: In this situation, there does exist some real water there. This is because the human can confirm the water with his own experience, as it performs all the functions of water--as he uses it to wash himself, or as he uses it to cook something. Well then, in the same situation there must exist some real pus and blood there as well, because in this same situation the craving spirit can confirm the pus and blood with his own experience as they perform their functions--as he drinks them, and then as the sizzle in his throat and stomach, and so on.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"KHO NA RE, DE'I TSE NA CHU DNGOS GNAS PA YOD DE, CHUS LUS 'KHRUD PA DANG 'TSOD PAR BYED PA MI RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR ZER NA, 'O NA, DE'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS YOD PAR THAL, DE'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG LA LONGS SPYOD PA DANG , LTO BA DANG MGRIN PA LA SOGS PA 'TSIG PA YI DVAGS RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"kho na re, de'i tshe na chu dngos gnas pa yod de, chus lus 'khrud pa dang 'tshod par byed pa mi rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir zer na, 'o na, de'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas yod par thal, de'i tshe na, rnag khrag la longs spyod pa dang , lto ba dang mgrin pa la sogs pa 'tshig pa yi dvags rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Someone might now make the following claim: In this situation, there does exist some real water there. This is because the human can confirm the water with his own experience, as it performs all the functions of water--as he uses it to wash himself, or as he uses it to cook something. Well then, in the same situation there must exist some real pus and blood there as well, because in this same situation the craving spirit can confirm the pus and blood with his own experience as they perform their functions--as he drinks them, and then as the sizzle in his throat and stomach, and so on.\"}"
  },
  "1685": {
    "id": 1685,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 303,
    "acip": "BYAS PA LA KHO NA RE, MI MTSUNGS TE, YI DVAGS LA DE LTAR 'BYUNG BA DE, YI DVAGS RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA, DES NA MI LA YANG DE LTAR 'BYUNG BA MED DE, DE MI RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "byas pa la kho na re, mi mtshungs te, yi dvags la de ltar 'byung ba de, yi dvags rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, zer na, des na mi la yang de ltar 'byung ba med de, de mi rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Someone may respond to this argument with the following claim: The two cases are not the same. When all this happens to the craving spirit, it's nothing more than his own imagination. Well then, what happens to the human can't be happening to him either, because it's nothing more than his imagination.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"BYAS PA LA KHO NA RE, MI MTSUNGS TE, YI DVAGS LA DE LTAR 'BYUNG BA DE, YI DVAGS RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA, DES NA MI LA YANG DE LTAR 'BYUNG BA MED DE, DE MI RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"byas pa la kho na re, mi mtshungs te, yi dvags la de ltar 'byung ba de, yi dvags rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, zer na, des na mi la yang de ltar 'byung ba med de, de mi rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Someone may respond to this argument with the following claim: The two cases are not the same. When all this happens to the craving spirit, it's nothing more than his own imagination. Well then, what happens to the human can't be happening to him either, because it's nothing more than his imagination.\"}"
  },
  "1686": {
    "id": 1686,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 304,
    "acip": "BYAS PA LA KHO NA RE, MI LA LUS 'KHRUD PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE MI RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "byas pa la kho na re, mi la lus 'khrud pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal, de mi rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, zer na",
    "english": "Someone may respond to this with another claim: When the human washes himself with the water and so on, it must not be something real, because it's nothing more than his own imagination.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"BYAS PA LA KHO NA RE, MI LA LUS 'KHRUD PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE MI RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"byas pa la kho na re, mi la lus 'khrud pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal, de mi rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone may respond to this with another claim: When the human washes himself with the water and so on, it must not be something real, because it's nothing more than his own imagination.\"}"
  },
  "1687": {
    "id": 1687,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 305,
    "acip": "YI DVAGS LA YANG LTO BSREG PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE YI DVAGS RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, 'DOD NA, YI DVAGS KYI SDUG BSNGAL MED PAR THAL,",
    "wylie": "yi dvags la yang lto bsreg pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal, de yi dvags rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, 'dod na, yi dvags kyi sdug bsngal med par thal",
    "english": "Are you saying then that when the pus and blood sizzle in the stomach of the craving spirit, and so on, it can't be something real? For that too is nothing more than his own imagination. You agree? Well then, the suffering of craving spirits must not exist at all.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"YI DVAGS LA YANG LTO BSREG PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE YI DVAGS RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, 'DOD NA, YI DVAGS KYI SDUG BSNGAL MED PAR THAL,\", \"wylie\": \"yi dvags la yang lto bsreg pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal, de yi dvags rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, 'dod na, yi dvags kyi sdug bsngal med par thal\", \"english\": \"Are you saying then that when the pus and blood sizzle in the stomach of the craving spirit, and so on, it can't be something real? For that too is nothing more than his own imagination. You agree? Well then, the suffering of craving spirits must not exist at all.\"}"
  },
  "1688": {
    "id": 1688,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 306,
    "acip": "KHO NA RE, DE LTAR CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, RAB RIB CAN GYIS BSE RU LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD MED PA GANG ZHIG, RAB RIB DANG LDAN DBANG PO CAN MTSUNGS PA, , CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "kho na re, de ltar cig car du bltas pa'i tshe, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par thal, rab rib can gyis bse ru la bltas pa de'i tshe bse ru'i nang du skra shad med pa gang zhig, rab rib dang ldan dbang po can mtshungs pa, , chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes gsungs pa'i phyir, zer na",
    "english": "Someone might make the following claim: Isn't it true that when all those beings sit down together and look at something, there isn't any actual pus and blood at all? Because isn't it true first of all that, when a person with cataracts looks into a porcelain basin, there is no strand of hair in the basin at all? And, secondly, doesn't [Master Dharmakirti's] text itself say, Identical to the case of someone where his sense power has a cataract, Is the mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"KHO NA RE, DE LTAR CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, RAB RIB CAN GYIS BSE RU LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD MED PA GANG ZHIG, RAB RIB DANG LDAN DBANG PO CAN MTSUNGS PA, , CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"kho na re, de ltar cig car du bltas pa'i tshe, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par thal, rab rib can gyis bse ru la bltas pa de'i tshe bse ru'i nang du skra shad med pa gang zhig, rab rib dang ldan dbang po can mtshungs pa, , chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes gsungs pa'i phyir, zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone might make the following claim: Isn't it true that when all those beings sit down together and look at something, there isn't any actual pus and blood at all? Because isn't it true first of all that, when a person with cataracts looks into a porcelain basin, there is no strand of hair in the basin at all? And, secondly, doesn't [Master Dharmakirti's] text itself say, Identical to the case of someone where his sense power has a cataract, Is the mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\"}"
  },
  "1689": {
    "id": 1689,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 307,
    "acip": "SKYON MED DE, DBU SEMS GNYIS DON SHES YOD MED MTSUNGS MI MTSUNGS DPYOD PA'I SKABS YIN PA'I PHYIR DANG , YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES LA CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I TSE NA, DE LTAR SNANG BA'I MIG SHES TSAD MA MA YIN PA DANG , CHU KLUNG LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA ZHIG YOD PA MI 'GAL BA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "skyon med de, dbu sems gnyis don shes yod med mtshungs mi mtshungs dpyod pa'i skabs yin pa'i phyir dang , yi dvags kyi mig shes la chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i tshe na, de ltar snang ba'i mig shes tshad ma ma yin pa dang , chu klung la bltas pa de'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa zhig yod pa mi 'gal ba'i phyir",
    "english": "And yet there is no such problem, for this quotation appears in the section where we are examining the question of whether, in the schools of the Middle Way and the Mind-Only, an object and the perception of it must be equivalent in either both existing or both not existing. Moreover, there is another fact about this situation, where the stream of water appears as pus and blood to the visual consciousness of the craving spirit. It is no inconsistency to say that the visual consciousness that sees things this way is not a valid perception, and to say at the same time that--when the craving spirit looks at the stream of water--there does exist there actual pus and blood.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"SKYON MED DE, DBU SEMS GNYIS DON SHES YOD MED MTSUNGS MI MTSUNGS DPYOD PA'I SKABS YIN PA'I PHYIR DANG , YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES LA CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I TSE NA, DE LTAR SNANG BA'I MIG SHES TSAD MA MA YIN PA DANG , CHU KLUNG LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA ZHIG YOD PA MI 'GAL BA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"skyon med de, dbu sems gnyis don shes yod med mtshungs mi mtshungs dpyod pa'i skabs yin pa'i phyir dang , yi dvags kyi mig shes la chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i tshe na, de ltar snang ba'i mig shes tshad ma ma yin pa dang , chu klung la bltas pa de'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa zhig yod pa mi 'gal ba'i phyir\", \"english\": \"And yet there is no such problem, for this quotation appears in the section where we are examining the question of whether, in the schools of the Middle Way and the Mind-Only, an object and the perception of it must be equivalent in either both existing or both not existing. Moreover, there is another fact about this situation, where the stream of water appears as pus and blood to the visual consciousness of the craving spirit. It is no inconsistency to say that the visual consciousness that sees things this way is not a valid perception, and to say at the same time that--when the craving spirit looks at the stream of water--there does exist there actual pus and blood.\"}"
  },
  "1690": {
    "id": 1690,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 308,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DMYAL BA'I LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PA DER SKYES PA'I SEMS CAN GYI SNANG BA TZAM YIN GYI, DNGOS GNAS SU GRUB PA MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DE 'DRA'I BYED PA PO SU YANG MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, SPYOD 'JUG LAS, LCAGS SREG SA GZHI SU YIS BYAS, , ME TSOGS DE DAG CI LAS BYUNG , , DE 'DRA DE DAG THAMS CAD KYANG , SDIG SEMS YIN PAR THUB PAS GSUNGS, , ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par thal, dmyal ba'i lcags sreg la sogs pa der skyes pa'i sems can gyi snang ba tsam yin gyi, dngos gnas su grub pa med pa'i phyir, der thal, de 'dra'i byed pa po su yang med pa'i phyir, der thal, spyod 'jug las, lcags sreg sa gzhi su yis byas, , me tshogs de dag ci las byung , , de 'dra de dag thams cad kyang , sdig sems yin par thub pas gsungs, , zhes gsungs pa'i phyir, zer na",
    "english": "Someone again may come and claim the following: Let's consider once more this situation where three different types of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. Isn't it true that there is no actual pus and blood there? Because isn't it true that the burning steel and so on in the hells is only something that appears to a person who is born there, but that there is nothing there which actually is these objects? Because isn't it true that there is no one at all who went and made all these kinds of things? And isn't this a fact, because doesn't the text of The Bodhisattva's Way of Life say: Who made the burning steel that acts As the floor of the world of hell? Where did all the mass of flames You find there all come from? The Able Ones have spoken that Everything there like this Is nothing at all other than The mind of what's non-virtue.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DMYAL BA'I LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PA DER SKYES PA'I SEMS CAN GYI SNANG BA TZAM YIN GYI, DNGOS GNAS SU GRUB PA MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DE 'DRA'I BYED PA PO SU YANG MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, SPYOD 'JUG LAS, LCAGS SREG SA GZHI SU YIS BYAS, , ME TSOGS DE DAG CI LAS BYUNG , , DE 'DRA DE DAG THAMS CAD KYANG , SDIG SEMS YIN PAR THUB PAS GSUNGS, , ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par thal, dmyal ba'i lcags sreg la sogs pa der skyes pa'i sems can gyi snang ba tsam yin gyi, dngos gnas su grub pa med pa'i phyir, der thal, de 'dra'i byed pa po su yang med pa'i phyir, der thal, spyod 'jug las, lcags sreg sa gzhi su yis byas, , me tshogs de dag ci las byung , , de 'dra de dag thams cad kyang , sdig sems yin par thub pas gsungs, , zhes gsungs pa'i phyir, zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone again may come and claim the following: Let's consider once more this situation where three different types of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. Isn't it true that there is no actual pus and blood there? Because isn't it true that the burning steel and so on in the hells is only something that appears to a person who is born there, but that there is nothing there which actually is these objects? Because isn't it true that there is no one at all who went and made all these kinds of things? And isn't this a fact, because doesn't the text of The Bodhisattva's Way of Life say: Who made the burning steel that acts As the floor of the world of hell? Where did all the mass of flames You find there all come from? The Able Ones have spoken that Everything there like this Is nothing at all other than The mind of what's non-virtue.\"}"
  },
  "1691": {
    "id": 1691,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 309,
    "acip": "SKYON MED DE, DE'I DON LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PA DE DAG DBANG PHYUG RTAG PA SOGS KYIS BLO'I G-YO BA SNGON DU BTANG NAS BYAS PA MA YIN PAR DER SKYES PA'I 'GRO BA'I SDIG SEMS BYED PA POR BSTAN PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "skyon med de, de'i don lcags sreg la sogs pa de dag dbang phyug rtag pa sogs kyis blo'i g-yo ba sngon du btang nas byas pa ma yin par der skyes pa'i 'gro ba'i sdig sems byed pa por bstan pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Yet there is no such problem. The point of this quotation is to say that the burning steel and so on are not something that was created by some unchanging creator being or something like that; by someone who thought it over first and then created them. The lines are meant to show us that what really made all these things is the non-virtuous states of mind had by the beings who have to take birth there.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"SKYON MED DE, DE'I DON LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PA DE DAG DBANG PHYUG RTAG PA SOGS KYIS BLO'I G-YO BA SNGON DU BTANG NAS BYAS PA MA YIN PAR DER SKYES PA'I 'GRO BA'I SDIG SEMS BYED PA POR BSTAN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"skyon med de, de'i don lcags sreg la sogs pa de dag dbang phyug rtag pa sogs kyis blo'i g-yo ba sngon du btang nas byas pa ma yin par der skyes pa'i 'gro ba'i sdig sems byed pa por bstan pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Yet there is no such problem. The point of this quotation is to say that the burning steel and so on are not something that was created by some unchanging creator being or something like that; by someone who thought it over first and then created them. The lines are meant to show us that what really made all these things is the non-virtuous states of mind had by the beings who have to take birth there.\"}"
  },
  "1692": {
    "id": 1692,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 310,
    "acip": "DE LTAR MA YIN NA, DGE BA BCU LA SPYOD PA'I SKYES BU DAM PA MTHO RIS SU SKYES NAS DE'I BDE BA MYONG BA DANG , MI DGE BCU LA SPYOD PA'I SEMS CAN DMAN PA NGAN SONG DU SKYES NAS DE'I SDUG BSNGAL MYONG BA GNYIS LA, 'KHRUL SNANG BZANG NGAN TZAM MA GTOGS LUS KYIS BDE SDUG MYONG MI MYONG GI KHYAD PAR MED PAR THAL, KHYOD KYI RIGS PA DE 'THAD PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "de ltar ma yin na, dge ba bcu la spyod pa'i skyes bu dam pa mtho ris su skyes nas de'i bde ba myong ba dang , mi dge bcu la spyod pa'i sems can dman pa ngan song du skyes nas de'i sdug bsngal myong ba gnyis la, 'khrul snang bzang ngan tsam ma gtogs lus kyis bde sdug myong mi myong gi khyad par med par thal, khyod kyi rigs pa de 'thad pa'i phyir",
    "english": "If this were not the case, then consider those holy people who lead their lives following the ten virtues, and who are then born into the higher realms, and then experience the pleasures of these realms. And consider too those miserable people who lead their lives following the ten non-virtues, and who are then born into the lower realms, and then experience the sufferings of these realms. Is the difference between them just that they are having some better or worse kind of misperception, and not whether they are experiencing pleasure or pain? This would have to be the case, if your reasoning were correct.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"DE LTAR MA YIN NA, DGE BA BCU LA SPYOD PA'I SKYES BU DAM PA MTHO RIS SU SKYES NAS DE'I BDE BA MYONG BA DANG , MI DGE BCU LA SPYOD PA'I SEMS CAN DMAN PA NGAN SONG DU SKYES NAS DE'I SDUG BSNGAL MYONG BA GNYIS LA, 'KHRUL SNANG BZANG NGAN TZAM MA GTOGS LUS KYIS BDE SDUG MYONG MI MYONG GI KHYAD PAR MED PAR THAL, KHYOD KYI RIGS PA DE 'THAD PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"de ltar ma yin na, dge ba bcu la spyod pa'i skyes bu dam pa mtho ris su skyes nas de'i bde ba myong ba dang , mi dge bcu la spyod pa'i sems can dman pa ngan song du skyes nas de'i sdug bsngal myong ba gnyis la, 'khrul snang bzang ngan tsam ma gtogs lus kyis bde sdug myong mi myong gi khyad par med par thal, khyod kyi rigs pa de 'thad pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"If this were not the case, then consider those holy people who lead their lives following the ten virtues, and who are then born into the higher realms, and then experience the pleasures of these realms. And consider too those miserable people who lead their lives following the ten non-virtues, and who are then born into the lower realms, and then experience the sufferings of these realms. Is the difference between them just that they are having some better or worse kind of misperception, and not whether they are experiencing pleasure or pain? This would have to be the case, if your reasoning were correct.\"}"
  },
  "1693": {
    "id": 1693,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 311,
    "acip": "'DOD NA, MTHO RIS KYI BDE BA DANG , NGAN SONG GI SDUG BSNGAL MED PAR THAL, 'DOD PA DE'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "'dod na, mtho ris kyi bde ba dang , ngan song gi sdug bsngal med par thal, 'dod pa de'i phyir",
    "english": "Suppose you agree that it is only a matter of better or worse misperceptions. Are you saying then that the pleasures of the higher realms don't even exist, and that the pains of the lower realms don't even exist? You must be, if you agree this way.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"'DOD NA, MTHO RIS KYI BDE BA DANG , NGAN SONG GI SDUG BSNGAL MED PAR THAL, 'DOD PA DE'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"'dod na, mtho ris kyi bde ba dang , ngan song gi sdug bsngal med par thal, 'dod pa de'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Suppose you agree that it is only a matter of better or worse misperceptions. Are you saying then that the pleasures of the higher realms don't even exist, and that the pains of the lower realms don't even exist? You must be, if you agree this way.\"}"
  },
  "1694": {
    "id": 1694,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 312,
    "acip": "DES NA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, TSAD MTSUNGS NGES PAR DGOS PA MA YIN TE, DGOS NA, PHOR PA GANG PO DE'I NANG DU GNAS KYI BLOS GNAS SHING , THA MAL GYI MIG SHES KYIS MI BZOD PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , DE'I TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , RGYA MTSO'I NANG NA GNAS PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , RGYA MTSO'I TSAD LA TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , LHA MA YIN 'GA' ZHIG LA, MTSON CHA PHOR GANG SNANG DGOS PA LA SOGS PA'I SKYON YOD PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "des na, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, tshad mtshungs nges par dgos pa ma yin te, dgos na, phor pa gang po de'i nang du gnas kyi blos gnas shing , tha mal gyi mig shes kyis mi bzod pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , de'i tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , rgya mtsho'i nang na gnas pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , rgya mtsho'i tshad la tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , lha ma yin 'ga' zhig la, mtshon cha phor gang snang dgos pa la sogs pa'i skyon yod pa'i phyir",
    "english": "In conclusion now, let us consider again these three kinds of beings, each with their different karma, as they sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It's not necessarily true that they must all have valid perceptions which are identical. If they did, then the three beings looking at the glass of water would have to think of the water as a place to live, in the way that a creature living in water would. The three beings as well would have to see the water in the same way that microscopic organisms living in the water, little beings imperceptible to normal visual consciousness, see it with their own visual consciousness. Then too the visual consciousness of microscopic organisms living in the depths of the ocean would have to be a valid perception towards the entire extent of the sea. And certain kinds of near-gods too would have to see weapons as glasses of water, and on and on; the problems raised would be many.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"DES NA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, TSAD MTSUNGS NGES PAR DGOS PA MA YIN TE, DGOS NA, PHOR PA GANG PO DE'I NANG DU GNAS KYI BLOS GNAS SHING , THA MAL GYI MIG SHES KYIS MI BZOD PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , DE'I TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , RGYA MTSO'I NANG NA GNAS PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , RGYA MTSO'I TSAD LA TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , LHA MA YIN 'GA' ZHIG LA, MTSON CHA PHOR GANG SNANG DGOS PA LA SOGS PA'I SKYON YOD PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"des na, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, tshad mtshungs nges par dgos pa ma yin te, dgos na, phor pa gang po de'i nang du gnas kyi blos gnas shing , tha mal gyi mig shes kyis mi bzod pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , de'i tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , rgya mtsho'i nang na gnas pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , rgya mtsho'i tshad la tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , lha ma yin 'ga' zhig la, mtshon cha phor gang snang dgos pa la sogs pa'i skyon yod pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"In conclusion now, let us consider again these three kinds of beings, each with their different karma, as they sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It's not necessarily true that they must all have valid perceptions which are identical. If they did, then the three beings looking at the glass of water would have to think of the water as a place to live, in the way that a creature living in water would. The three beings as well would have to see the water in the same way that microscopic organisms living in the water, little beings imperceptible to normal visual consciousness, see it with their own visual consciousness. Then too the visual consciousness of microscopic organisms living in the depths of the ocean would have to be a valid perception towards the entire extent of the sea. And certain kinds of near-gods too would have to see weapons as glasses of water, and on and on; the problems raised would be many.\"}"
  },
  "1695": {
    "id": 1695,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 313,
    "acip": "DE'I TSE NA, TSAD MTSUNGS NGES PAR MI DGOS KYANG , TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM YOD PA SRID PA YIN TE, LAS KYI DBANG GIS DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM BSKYED NUS PA SNGAR GYI RIGS PAS 'GRUB, DE BSKYED NUS NA, LAS KYI DBANG GIS SNOD TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM SKYE BA YOD PAR YANG MTSUNGS PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "de'i tshe na, tshad mtshungs nges par mi dgos kyang , tshad mtshungs gsum yod pa srid pa yin te, las kyi dbang gis dngos po tshad grub pa gsum bskyed nus pa sngar gyi rigs pas 'grub, de bskyed nus na, las kyi dbang gis snod tshad mtshungs gsum skye ba yod par yang mtshungs pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Again consider this same situation. Even though it is not necessarily true that the valid perceptions are identical, it is possible for there to be three valid perceptions here which happen to be identical. This is because, as we have already established logically, there can be a case where by the force of karma three different objects, each one confirmed by a valid perception, start to exist. And since this is possible, then it is equally possible that, by the force of karma, three equivalent valid perceptions of a vessel could start to exist as well.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"DE'I TSE NA, TSAD MTSUNGS NGES PAR MI DGOS KYANG , TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM YOD PA SRID PA YIN TE, LAS KYI DBANG GIS DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM BSKYED NUS PA SNGAR GYI RIGS PAS 'GRUB, DE BSKYED NUS NA, LAS KYI DBANG GIS SNOD TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM SKYE BA YOD PAR YANG MTSUNGS PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"de'i tshe na, tshad mtshungs nges par mi dgos kyang , tshad mtshungs gsum yod pa srid pa yin te, las kyi dbang gis dngos po tshad grub pa gsum bskyed nus pa sngar gyi rigs pas 'grub, de bskyed nus na, las kyi dbang gis snod tshad mtshungs gsum skye ba yod par yang mtshungs pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Again consider this same situation. Even though it is not necessarily true that the valid perceptions are identical, it is possible for there to be three valid perceptions here which happen to be identical. This is because, as we have already established logically, there can be a case where by the force of karma three different objects, each one confirmed by a valid perception, start to exist. And since this is possible, then it is equally possible that, by the force of karma, three equivalent valid perceptions of a vessel could start to exist as well.\"}"
  },
  "1696": {
    "id": 1696,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 314,
    "acip": "RNAG LA SOGS PA'I DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE SPYIR GO SA 'GOG NUS KYI DNGOS PO YIN KYANG , DE LTAR BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA GO SA 'GOG BYED KYI DNGOS PO MA YIN PA MI 'GAL TE, RI RAB GRU BZHI DANG , ZLUM PO LA SOGS PA PHAN TSUN GO SA 'GOG NUS KYI DNGOS PO YIN KYANG , RI RAB GCIG GI GO SA NA, DE GNYIS KA YOD PA SRID PA LTA BU YIN PA'I PHYIR, LCAGS GONG DMAR 'BAR BA TSA BA YIN KYANG , LCAGS SNGAGS BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA'I TSE, DE'I MYONG BYAR GYUR PA'I REG BYA DE TSA BA'I REG BYA MA YIN TE, TSA BA MA YIN PA'I REG BYA YIN PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "rnag la sogs pa'i dngos po gsum po de spyir go sa 'gog nus kyi dngos po yin kyang , de ltar bltas pa de'i tshe na go sa 'gog byed kyi dngos po ma yin pa mi 'gal te, ri rab gru bzhi dang , zlum po la sogs pa phan tshun go sa 'gog nus kyi dngos po yin kyang , ri rab gcig gi go sa na, de gnyis ka yod pa srid pa lta bu yin pa'i phyir, lcags gong dmar 'bar ba tsha ba yin kyang , lcags sngags btab pa'i lag pas reg pa'i tshe, de'i myong byar gyur pa'i reg bya de tsha ba'i reg bya ma yin te, tsha ba ma yin pa'i reg bya yin pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Generally speaking, each of the three objects mentioned--the pus and the other two--are things of the type that block other objects from entering into the space which they themselves occupy. It is no contradiction though to say that, in this situation where the beings are looking this way, they are not objects such that they block other things from entering into the space they occupy. This is true for the following reason. A central mountain of the world which is square in shape, and a central mountain of the world which is round in shape, and the like, are objects such that they block other things from entering the space they occupy. Nevertheless, it is possible for both these things to occupy the space taken up by a single central mountain of the world. A red-hot ball of steel is something that's hot, but consider what happens when a person touches it after he has used the mantra of steel on his hand. The sensation that he feels is not a sensation of heat; on the contrary, it is a sensation of something not heat. [This concludes the section of the text entitled \"The Stream.\"] 128 The Asian Classics Institute Course V: How Karma Works Reading Seven: Black and White Deeds, the \"Path of Action,\" and the Root and Branch Non-Virtues",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"RNAG LA SOGS PA'I DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE SPYIR GO SA 'GOG NUS KYI DNGOS PO YIN KYANG , DE LTAR BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA GO SA 'GOG BYED KYI DNGOS PO MA YIN PA MI 'GAL TE, RI RAB GRU BZHI DANG , ZLUM PO LA SOGS PA PHAN TSUN GO SA 'GOG NUS KYI DNGOS PO YIN KYANG , RI RAB GCIG GI GO SA NA, DE GNYIS KA YOD PA SRID PA LTA BU YIN PA'I PHYIR, LCAGS GONG DMAR 'BAR BA TSA BA YIN KYANG , LCAGS SNGAGS BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA'I TSE, DE'I MYONG BYAR GYUR PA'I REG BYA DE TSA BA'I REG BYA MA YIN TE, TSA BA MA YIN PA'I REG BYA YIN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"rnag la sogs pa'i dngos po gsum po de spyir go sa 'gog nus kyi dngos po yin kyang , de ltar bltas pa de'i tshe na go sa 'gog byed kyi dngos po ma yin pa mi 'gal te, ri rab gru bzhi dang , zlum po la sogs pa phan tshun go sa 'gog nus kyi dngos po yin kyang , ri rab gcig gi go sa na, de gnyis ka yod pa srid pa lta bu yin pa'i phyir, lcags gong dmar 'bar ba tsha ba yin kyang , lcags sngags btab pa'i lag pas reg pa'i tshe, de'i myong byar gyur pa'i reg bya de tsha ba'i reg bya ma yin te, tsha ba ma yin pa'i reg bya yin pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Generally speaking, each of the three objects mentioned--the pus and the other two--are things of the type that block other objects from entering into the space which they themselves occupy. It is no contradiction though to say that, in this situation where the beings are looking this way, they are not objects such that they block other things from entering into the space they occupy. This is true for the following reason. A central mountain of the world which is square in shape, and a central mountain of the world which is round in shape, and the like, are objects such that they block other things from entering the space they occupy. Nevertheless, it is possible for both these things to occupy the space taken up by a single central mountain of the world. A red-hot ball of steel is something that's hot, but consider what happens when a person touches it after he has used the mantra of steel on his hand. The sensation that he feels is not a sensation of heat; on the contrary, it is a sensation of something not heat. [This concludes the section of the text entitled \\\"The Stream.\\\"] 128 The Asian Classics Institute Course V: How Karma Works Reading Seven: Black and White Deeds, the \\\"Path of Action,\\\" and the Root and Branch Non-Virtues\"}"
  },
  "6000": {
    "id": 6000,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 449,
    "acip": "BYAS PA LA KHO NA RE, MI LA LUS 'KHRUD PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE MI RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "byas pa la kho na re, mi la lus 'khrud pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal, de mi rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, zer na",
    "english": "Someone may respond to this with another claim: When the human washes himself with the water and so on, it must not be something real, because it's nothing more than his own imagination.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"BYAS PA LA KHO NA RE, MI LA LUS 'KHRUD PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE MI RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"byas pa la kho na re, mi la lus 'khrud pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal, de mi rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone may respond to this with another claim: When the human washes himself with the water and so on, it must not be something real, because it's nothing more than his own imagination.\"}"
  },
  "6001": {
    "id": 6001,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 450,
    "acip": "YI DVAGS LA YANG LTO BSREG PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE YI DVAGS RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, 'DOD NA, YI DVAGS KYI SDUG BSNGAL MED PAR THAL,",
    "wylie": "yi dvags la yang lto bsreg pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal, de yi dvags rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, 'dod na, yi dvags kyi sdug bsngal med par thal",
    "english": "Are you saying then that when the pus and blood sizzle in the stomach of the craving spirit, and so on, it can't be something real? For that too is nothing more than his own imagination. You agree? Well then, the suffering of craving spirits must not exist at all.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"YI DVAGS LA YANG LTO BSREG PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE YI DVAGS RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, 'DOD NA, YI DVAGS KYI SDUG BSNGAL MED PAR THAL,\", \"wylie\": \"yi dvags la yang lto bsreg pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal, de yi dvags rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, 'dod na, yi dvags kyi sdug bsngal med par thal\", \"english\": \"Are you saying then that when the pus and blood sizzle in the stomach of the craving spirit, and so on, it can't be something real? For that too is nothing more than his own imagination. You agree? Well then, the suffering of craving spirits must not exist at all.\"}"
  },
  "6002": {
    "id": 6002,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 451,
    "acip": "KHO NA RE, DE LTAR CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, RAB RIB CAN GYIS BSE RU LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD MED PA GANG ZHIG, RAB RIB DANG LDAN DBANG PO CAN MTSUNGS PA, , CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "kho na re, de ltar cig car du bltas pa'i tshe, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par thal, rab rib can gyis bse ru la bltas pa de'i tshe bse ru'i nang du skra shad med pa gang zhig, rab rib dang ldan dbang po can mtshungs pa, , chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes gsungs pa'i phyir, zer na",
    "english": "Someone might make the following claim: Isn't it true that when all those beings sit down together and look at something, there isn't any actual pus and blood at all? Because isn't it true first of all that, when a person with cataracts looks into a porcelain basin, there is no strand of hair in the basin at all? And, secondly, doesn't [Master Dharmakirti's] text itself say, Identical to the case of someone where his sense power has a cataract, Is the mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"KHO NA RE, DE LTAR CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, RAB RIB CAN GYIS BSE RU LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD MED PA GANG ZHIG, RAB RIB DANG LDAN DBANG PO CAN MTSUNGS PA, , CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"kho na re, de ltar cig car du bltas pa'i tshe, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par thal, rab rib can gyis bse ru la bltas pa de'i tshe bse ru'i nang du skra shad med pa gang zhig, rab rib dang ldan dbang po can mtshungs pa, , chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes gsungs pa'i phyir, zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone might make the following claim: Isn't it true that when all those beings sit down together and look at something, there isn't any actual pus and blood at all? Because isn't it true first of all that, when a person with cataracts looks into a porcelain basin, there is no strand of hair in the basin at all? And, secondly, doesn't [Master Dharmakirti's] text itself say, Identical to the case of someone where his sense power has a cataract, Is the mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\"}"
  },
  "5999": {
    "id": 5999,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 448,
    "acip": "BYAS PA LA KHO NA RE, MI MTSUNGS TE, YI DVAGS LA DE LTAR 'BYUNG BA DE, YI DVAGS RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA, DES NA MI LA YANG DE LTAR 'BYUNG BA MED DE, DE MI RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "byas pa la kho na re, mi mtshungs te, yi dvags la de ltar 'byung ba de, yi dvags rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, zer na, des na mi la yang de ltar 'byung ba med de, de mi rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Someone may respond to this argument with the following claim: The two cases are not the same. When all this happens to the craving spirit, it's nothing more than his own imagination. Well then, what happens to the human can't be happening to him either, because it's nothing more than his imagination.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"BYAS PA LA KHO NA RE, MI MTSUNGS TE, YI DVAGS LA DE LTAR 'BYUNG BA DE, YI DVAGS RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA, DES NA MI LA YANG DE LTAR 'BYUNG BA MED DE, DE MI RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"byas pa la kho na re, mi mtshungs te, yi dvags la de ltar 'byung ba de, yi dvags rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir, zer na, des na mi la yang de ltar 'byung ba med de, de mi rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Someone may respond to this argument with the following claim: The two cases are not the same. When all this happens to the craving spirit, it's nothing more than his own imagination. Well then, what happens to the human can't be happening to him either, because it's nothing more than his imagination.\"}"
  },
  "29371": {
    "id": 29371,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2052,
    "acip": "DANG PO NI, RNAM PAR SHES PA SMRA BA DBU MA PA'I LUGS JI SKAD BSTAN PA MI BZOD CING, RGYAL BA'I DGONGS PA MIN PAS, RANG GI RNAM RTOG GIS SBYAR BA'I LUGS BRJOD PA'I SGO NAS DON 'JOG PAR BYED PA RNAMS KYIS, RANG GI GZHUNG LAS JI SKAD DU BSHAD PA'I LUGS GSAL BAR BYA BA'I PHYIR PHYOGS SNGA MA SMRAS PA,",
    "wylie": "dang po ni, rnam par shes pa smra ba dbu ma pa'i lugs ji skad bstan pa mi bzod cing, rgyal ba'i dgongs pa min pas, rang gi rnam rtog gis sbyar ba'i lugs brjod pa'i sgo nas don 'jog par byed pa rnams kyis, rang gi gzhung las ji skad du bshad pa'i lugs gsal bar bya ba'i phyir phyogs snga ma smras pa",
    "english": "Here is the first. Those who assert that everything is consciousness cannot bear the position that those of the Middle Way describe, and say that it is not what the Victors really mean. Rather, these people seek to establish how things actually are by expressing a position which is just a creation of their own imagination. And so in an attempt to clarify the system presented in their own classical texts, they begin with the following opening statement.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DANG PO NI, RNAM PAR SHES PA SMRA BA DBU MA PA'I LUGS JI SKAD BSTAN PA MI BZOD CING, RGYAL BA'I DGONGS PA MIN PAS, RANG GI RNAM RTOG GIS SBYAR BA'I LUGS BRJOD PA'I SGO NAS DON 'JOG PAR BYED PA RNAMS KYIS, RANG GI GZHUNG LAS JI SKAD DU BSHAD PA'I LUGS GSAL BAR BYA BA'I PHYIR PHYOGS SNGA MA SMRAS PA,\", \"wylie\": \"dang po ni, rnam par shes pa smra ba dbu ma pa'i lugs ji skad bstan pa mi bzod cing, rgyal ba'i dgongs pa min pas, rang gi rnam rtog gis sbyar ba'i lugs brjod pa'i sgo nas don 'jog par byed pa rnams kyis, rang gi gzhung las ji skad du bshad pa'i lugs gsal bar bya ba'i phyir phyogs snga ma smras pa\", \"english\": \"Here is the first. Those who assert that everything is consciousness cannot bear the position that those of the Middle Way describe, and say that it is not what the Victors really mean. Rather, these people seek to establish how things actually are by expressing a position which is just a creation of their own imagination. And so in an attempt to clarify the system presented in their own classical texts, they begin with the following opening statement.\"}"
  },
  "34933": {
    "id": 34933,
    "course": "P7",
    "seq": 44,
    "acip": "YI DWAGS LA YANG LTO BSREG PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL DE YI DWAGS, RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR 'DOD NA YI DWAGS KYI SDUG BSNGAL MED, PAR THAL",
    "wylie": "yi dwags la yang lto bsreg pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal de yi dwags, rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir 'dod na yi dwags kyi sdug bsngal med, par thal",
    "english": "Are you saying then that when the pus and blood sizzle in the stomach of the craving spirit, and so on, it can't be something real? For that too is nothing more than his own imagination. You agree? Well then, the suffering of craving spirits must not exist at all.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"YI DWAGS LA YANG LTO BSREG PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL DE YI DWAGS, RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR 'DOD NA YI DWAGS KYI SDUG BSNGAL MED, PAR THAL\", \"wylie\": \"yi dwags la yang lto bsreg pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal de yi dwags, rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir 'dod na yi dwags kyi sdug bsngal med, par thal\", \"english\": \"Are you saying then that when the pus and blood sizzle in the stomach of the craving spirit, and so on, it can't be something real? For that too is nothing more than his own imagination. You agree? Well then, the suffering of craving spirits must not exist at all.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
  },
  "5998": {
    "id": 5998,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 447,
    "acip": "KHO NA RE, DE'I TSE NA CHU DNGOS GNAS PA YOD DE, CHUS LUS 'KHRUD PA DANG 'TSOD PAR BYED PA MI RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR ZER NA, 'O NA, DE'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS YOD PAR THAL, DE'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG LA LONGS SPYOD PA DANG , LTO BA DANG MGRIN PA LA SOGS PA 'TSIG PA YI DVAGS RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "kho na re, de'i tshe na chu dngos gnas pa yod de, chus lus 'khrud pa dang 'tshod par byed pa mi rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir zer na, 'o na, de'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas yod par thal, de'i tshe na, rnag khrag la longs spyod pa dang , lto ba dang mgrin pa la sogs pa 'tshig pa yi dvags rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Someone might now make the following claim: In this situation, there does exist some real water there. This is because the human can confirm the water with his own experience, as it performs all the functions of water--as he uses it to wash himself, or as he uses it to cook something. Well then, in the same situation there must exist some real pus and blood there as well, because in this same situation the craving spirit can confirm the pus and blood with his own experience as they perform their functions--as he drinks them, and then as the sizzle in his throat and stomach, and so on.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"KHO NA RE, DE'I TSE NA CHU DNGOS GNAS PA YOD DE, CHUS LUS 'KHRUD PA DANG 'TSOD PAR BYED PA MI RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR ZER NA, 'O NA, DE'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS YOD PAR THAL, DE'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG LA LONGS SPYOD PA DANG , LTO BA DANG MGRIN PA LA SOGS PA 'TSIG PA YI DVAGS RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"kho na re, de'i tshe na chu dngos gnas pa yod de, chus lus 'khrud pa dang 'tshod par byed pa mi rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir zer na, 'o na, de'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas yod par thal, de'i tshe na, rnag khrag la longs spyod pa dang , lto ba dang mgrin pa la sogs pa 'tshig pa yi dvags rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Someone might now make the following claim: In this situation, there does exist some real water there. This is because the human can confirm the water with his own experience, as it performs all the functions of water--as he uses it to wash himself, or as he uses it to cook something. Well then, in the same situation there must exist some real pus and blood there as well, because in this same situation the craving spirit can confirm the pus and blood with his own experience as they perform their functions--as he drinks them, and then as the sizzle in his throat and stomach, and so on.\"}"
  },
  "34930": {
    "id": 34930,
    "course": "P7",
    "seq": 41,
    "acip": "KHO NA RE DE'I TSE NA CHU DNGOS GNAS PA YOD DE CHUS LUS 'KHRUD PA DANG 'TSOD PAR, BYED PA MI RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR ZER NA 'O NA DE'I TSE NA RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS YOD PAR THAL DE'I TSE NA RNAG KHRAG LA LONGS SPYOD PA DANG LTO BA DANG MGRIN PA LA SOGS PA 'TSIG PA YI DWAGS RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR",
    "wylie": "kho na re de'i tshe na chu dngos gnas pa yod de chus lus 'khrud pa dang 'tshod par, byed pa mi rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir zer na 'o na de'i tshe na rnag khrag dngos gnas yod par thal de'i tshe na rnag khrag la longs spyod pa dang lto ba dang mgrin pa la sogs pa 'tshig pa yi dwags rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Someone might now make the following claim: In this situation, there does exist some real water there. This is because the human can confirm the water with his own experience, as it performs all the functions of water—as he uses it to wash himself, or as he uses it to cook something. Well then, in the same situation there must exist some real pus and blood there as well, because in this same situation the craving spirit can confirm the pus and blood with his own experience as they perform their functions—as he drinks them, and then as the sizzle in his throat and stomach, and so on.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"KHO NA RE DE'I TSE NA CHU DNGOS GNAS PA YOD DE CHUS LUS 'KHRUD PA DANG 'TSOD PAR, BYED PA MI RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR ZER NA 'O NA DE'I TSE NA RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS YOD PAR THAL DE'I TSE NA RNAG KHRAG LA LONGS SPYOD PA DANG LTO BA DANG MGRIN PA LA SOGS PA 'TSIG PA YI DWAGS RANG GIS MYONG BAS 'GRUB PA'I PHYIR\", \"wylie\": \"kho na re de'i tshe na chu dngos gnas pa yod de chus lus 'khrud pa dang 'tshod par, byed pa mi rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir zer na 'o na de'i tshe na rnag khrag dngos gnas yod par thal de'i tshe na rnag khrag la longs spyod pa dang lto ba dang mgrin pa la sogs pa 'tshig pa yi dwags rang gis myong bas 'grub pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Someone might now make the following claim: In this situation, there does exist some real water there. This is because the human can confirm the water with his own experience, as it performs all the functions of water—as he uses it to wash himself, or as he uses it to cook something. Well then, in the same situation there must exist some real pus and blood there as well, because in this same situation the craving spirit can confirm the pus and blood with his own experience as they perform their functions—as he drinks them, and then as the sizzle in his throat and stomach, and so on.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
  },
  "34932": {
    "id": 34932,
    "course": "P7",
    "seq": 43,
    "acip": "BYAS PA LA KHO NA RE MI LA LUS 'KHRUD PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL DE, MI RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR ZER NA",
    "wylie": "byas pa la kho na re mi la lus 'khrud pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal de, mi rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir zer na",
    "english": "Someone may respond to this with another claim: When the human washes himself with the water and so on, it must not be something real, because it's nothing more than his",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"BYAS PA LA KHO NA RE MI LA LUS 'KHRUD PA SOGS DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL DE, MI RANG GI BLOS RLOM PA TZAM YIN PA'I PHYIR ZER NA\", \"wylie\": \"byas pa la kho na re mi la lus 'khrud pa sogs dngos gnas pa med par thal de, mi rang gi blos rlom pa tsam yin pa'i phyir zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone may respond to this with another claim: When the human washes himself with the water and so on, it must not be something real, because it's nothing more than his\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
  },
  "1629": {
    "id": 1629,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 247,
    "acip": "CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES SOGS KYI SKABS SU MTHA' DPYAD PA LA, CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES DANG , BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR SNANG BA'I RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG SHES DANG , SA GZHI KENG RUS KYIS KHYAB PAR SNANG BA'I KENG RUS GSAL SNANG CAN GYI TING NGE 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA RNAMS KYI YUL DE DANG , DER YOD MED RNAM PA KUN DU MTSUNGS SAM MI MTSUNGS ZHE NA,",
    "wylie": "chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes sogs kyi skabs su mtha' dpyad pa la, chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i yi dvags kyi mig shes dang , bse ru'i nang du skra shad 'dzag par snang ba'i rab rib can gyi mig shes dang , sa gzhi keng rus kyis khyab par snang ba'i keng rus gsal snang can gyi ting nge 'dzin dang bcas pa rnams kyi yul de dang , der yod med rnam pa kun du mtshungs sam mi mtshungs zhe na",
    "english": "Here we will analyze the statement [from Entering the Middle Way, by Master Chandrakirti (650 AD)] where it says, \"...The mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\" One may begin with the following question: Let's consider the objects of the following states of mind: the visual consciousness of a craving spirit where a river of water looks like pus and blood; the visual consciousness of a person with a kind of cataract where a clean white porcelain basin looks like a hair has fallen into it; and that kind of meditation where you visualize skeletons--where you imagine that the entire surface of the earth is covered with the bones of corpses. Are all these objects completely equivalent, as far as being something that exists or doesn't exist?",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES SOGS KYI SKABS SU MTHA' DPYAD PA LA, CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES DANG , BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR SNANG BA'I RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG SHES DANG , SA GZHI KENG RUS KYIS KHYAB PAR SNANG BA'I KENG RUS GSAL SNANG CAN GYI TING NGE 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA RNAMS KYI YUL DE DANG , DER YOD MED RNAM PA KUN DU MTSUNGS SAM MI MTSUNGS ZHE NA,\", \"wylie\": \"chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes sogs kyi skabs su mtha' dpyad pa la, chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i yi dvags kyi mig shes dang , bse ru'i nang du skra shad 'dzag par snang ba'i rab rib can gyi mig shes dang , sa gzhi keng rus kyis khyab par snang ba'i keng rus gsal snang can gyi ting nge 'dzin dang bcas pa rnams kyi yul de dang , der yod med rnam pa kun du mtshungs sam mi mtshungs zhe na\", \"english\": \"Here we will analyze the statement [from Entering the Middle Way, by Master Chandrakirti (650 AD)] where it says, \\\"...The mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\\\" One may begin with the following question: Let's consider the objects of the following states of mind: the visual consciousness of a craving spirit where a river of water looks like pus and blood; the visual consciousness of a person with a kind of cataract where a clean white porcelain basin looks like a hair has fallen into it; and that kind of meditation where you visualize skeletons--where you imagine that the entire surface of the earth is covered with the bones of corpses. Are all these objects completely equivalent, as far as being something that exists or doesn't exist?\"}"
  },
  "5944": {
    "id": 5944,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 393,
    "acip": "CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES SOGS KYI SKABS SU MTHA' DPYAD PA LA, CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES DANG , BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR SNANG BA'I RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG SHES DANG , SA GZHI KENG RUS KYIS KHYAB PAR SNANG BA'I KENG RUS GSAL SNANG CAN GYI TING NGE 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA RNAMS KYI YUL DE DANG , DER YOD MED RNAM PA KUN DU MTSUNGS SAM MI MTSUNGS ZHE NA,",
    "wylie": "chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes sogs kyi skabs su mtha' dpyad pa la, chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i yi dvags kyi mig shes dang , bse ru'i nang du skra shad 'dzag par snang ba'i rab rib can gyi mig shes dang , sa gzhi keng rus kyis khyab par snang ba'i keng rus gsal snang can gyi ting nge 'dzin dang bcas pa rnams kyi yul de dang , der yod med rnam pa kun du mtshungs sam mi mtshungs zhe na",
    "english": "Here we will analyze the statement [from Entering the Middle Way, by Master Chandrakirti (650 AD)] where it says, \"...The mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\" One may begin with the following question: Let's consider the objects of the following states of mind: the visual consciousness of a craving spirit where a river of water looks like pus and blood; the visual consciousness of a person with a kind of cataract where a clean white porcelain basin looks like a hair has fallen into it; and that kind of meditation where you visualize skeletons--where you imagine that the entire surface of the earth is covered with the bones of corpses. Are all these objects completely equivalent, as far as being something that exists or doesn't exist?",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES SOGS KYI SKABS SU MTHA' DPYAD PA LA, CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES DANG , BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR SNANG BA'I RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG SHES DANG , SA GZHI KENG RUS KYIS KHYAB PAR SNANG BA'I KENG RUS GSAL SNANG CAN GYI TING NGE 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA RNAMS KYI YUL DE DANG , DER YOD MED RNAM PA KUN DU MTSUNGS SAM MI MTSUNGS ZHE NA,\", \"wylie\": \"chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes sogs kyi skabs su mtha' dpyad pa la, chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i yi dvags kyi mig shes dang , bse ru'i nang du skra shad 'dzag par snang ba'i rab rib can gyi mig shes dang , sa gzhi keng rus kyis khyab par snang ba'i keng rus gsal snang can gyi ting nge 'dzin dang bcas pa rnams kyi yul de dang , der yod med rnam pa kun du mtshungs sam mi mtshungs zhe na\", \"english\": \"Here we will analyze the statement [from Entering the Middle Way, by Master Chandrakirti (650 AD)] where it says, \\\"...The mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\\\" One may begin with the following question: Let's consider the objects of the following states of mind: the visual consciousness of a craving spirit where a river of water looks like pus and blood; the visual consciousness of a person with a kind of cataract where a clean white porcelain basin looks like a hair has fallen into it; and that kind of meditation where you visualize skeletons--where you imagine that the entire surface of the earth is covered with the bones of corpses. Are all these objects completely equivalent, as far as being something that exists or doesn't exist?\"}"
  },
  "5995": {
    "id": 5995,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 444,
    "acip": "GZHAN YANG , SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG MED CING DER SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM GYIS BZA' BTUNG GI BYA BA BYED NA, RAB RIB CAN LA BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD SNANG BAS 'CHING BA DANG , DE LA SNANG BA'I SBRANG BUS LUS ZA BA DANG , SMIG RGYU'I CHUS CHU'I BYA BA BYED NUS PA SOGS YIN PAR THAL, SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG MED KYANG , RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM GYIS BZA' BTUNG GI BYA BA BYED NUS PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "gzhan yang , snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag med cing der snang ba'i snang ba tsam gyis bza' btung gi bya ba byed na, rab rib can la bse ru'i nang du skra shad snang bas 'ching ba dang , de la snang ba'i sbrang bus lus za ba dang , smig rgyu'i chus chu'i bya ba byed nus pa sogs yin par thal, snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag med kyang , rnag khrag tu snang ba'i snang ba tsam gyis bza' btung gi bya ba byed nus pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Suppose moreover that there were no pus and blood out there to appear as the pus and blood, and suppose that the mere appearance of something looking like pus and blood could ever function as something to eat or drink. Well then, you would also have to be able to use a comb on the hair that appears to exist on a porcelain sink to a person with cataracts. And a horsefly that appeared to the same person would have to be able to give him a bite. And the water of a mirage would have to provide all the normal functions of water, and so on. Why so? Well because, according to you, there is no pus and blood out there to appear as pus and blood; according to you, the mere appearance of something looking like pus and blood can provide all the functions of things that you eat and drink.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"GZHAN YANG , SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG MED CING DER SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM GYIS BZA' BTUNG GI BYA BA BYED NA, RAB RIB CAN LA BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD SNANG BAS 'CHING BA DANG , DE LA SNANG BA'I SBRANG BUS LUS ZA BA DANG , SMIG RGYU'I CHUS CHU'I BYA BA BYED NUS PA SOGS YIN PAR THAL, SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG MED KYANG , RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM GYIS BZA' BTUNG GI BYA BA BYED NUS PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"gzhan yang , snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag med cing der snang ba'i snang ba tsam gyis bza' btung gi bya ba byed na, rab rib can la bse ru'i nang du skra shad snang bas 'ching ba dang , de la snang ba'i sbrang bus lus za ba dang , smig rgyu'i chus chu'i bya ba byed nus pa sogs yin par thal, snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag med kyang , rnag khrag tu snang ba'i snang ba tsam gyis bza' btung gi bya ba byed nus pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Suppose moreover that there were no pus and blood out there to appear as the pus and blood, and suppose that the mere appearance of something looking like pus and blood could ever function as something to eat or drink. Well then, you would also have to be able to use a comb on the hair that appears to exist on a porcelain sink to a person with cataracts. And a horsefly that appeared to the same person would have to be able to give him a bite. And the water of a mirage would have to provide all the normal functions of water, and so on. Why so? Well because, according to you, there is no pus and blood out there to appear as pus and blood; according to you, the mere appearance of something looking like pus and blood can provide all the functions of things that you eat and drink.\"}"
  },
  "6841": {
    "id": 6841,
    "course": "C16",
    "seq": 823,
    "acip": "CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES SOGS KYI SKABS SU MTHA' DPYAD PA LA, CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES DANG , BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR SNANG BA'I RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG SHES DANG , SA GZHI KENG RUS KYIS KHYAB PAR SNANG BA'I KENG RUS GSAL SNANG CAN GYI TING NGE 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA RNAMS KYI YUL DE DANG , DER YOD MED RNAM PA KUN DU MTSUNGS SAM MI MTSUNGS ZHE NA,",
    "wylie": "chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes sogs kyi skabs su mtha' dpyad pa la, chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i yi dvags kyi mig shes dang , bse ru'i nang du skra shad 'dzag par snang ba'i rab rib can gyi mig shes dang , sa gzhi keng rus kyis khyab par snang ba'i keng rus gsal snang can gyi ting nge 'dzin dang bcas pa rnams kyi yul de dang , der yod med rnam pa kun du mtshungs sam mi mtshungs zhe na",
    "english": "Here we will analyze the statement [from Entering the Middle Way, by Master Chandrakirti (650 AD)] where it says, \"...The mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\" One may begin with the following question: Let's consider the objects of the following states of mind: the visual consciousness of a craving spirit where a river of water looks like pus and blood; the visual consciousness of a person with a kind of cataract where a clean white porcelain basin looks like a hair has fallen into it; and that kind of meditation where you visualize skeletons--where you imagine that the entire surface of the earth is covered with the bones of corpses. Are all these objects completely equivalent, as far as being something that exists or doesn't exist?",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES SOGS KYI SKABS SU MTHA' DPYAD PA LA, CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES DANG , BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR SNANG BA'I RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG SHES DANG , SA GZHI KENG RUS KYIS KHYAB PAR SNANG BA'I KENG RUS GSAL SNANG CAN GYI TING NGE 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA RNAMS KYI YUL DE DANG , DER YOD MED RNAM PA KUN DU MTSUNGS SAM MI MTSUNGS ZHE NA,\", \"wylie\": \"chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes sogs kyi skabs su mtha' dpyad pa la, chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i yi dvags kyi mig shes dang , bse ru'i nang du skra shad 'dzag par snang ba'i rab rib can gyi mig shes dang , sa gzhi keng rus kyis khyab par snang ba'i keng rus gsal snang can gyi ting nge 'dzin dang bcas pa rnams kyi yul de dang , der yod med rnam pa kun du mtshungs sam mi mtshungs zhe na\", \"english\": \"Here we will analyze the statement [from Entering the Middle Way, by Master Chandrakirti (650 AD)] where it says, \\\"...The mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\\\" One may begin with the following question: Let's consider the objects of the following states of mind: the visual consciousness of a craving spirit where a river of water looks like pus and blood; the visual consciousness of a person with a kind of cataract where a clean white porcelain basin looks like a hair has fallen into it; and that kind of meditation where you visualize skeletons--where you imagine that the entire surface of the earth is covered with the bones of corpses. Are all these objects completely equivalent, as far as being something that exists or doesn't exist?\"}"
  },
  "8911": {
    "id": 8911,
    "course": "C18",
    "seq": 1116,
    "acip": "CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES SOGS KYI SKABS SU MTHA' DPYAD PA LA, CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES DANG , BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR SNANG BA'I RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG SHES DANG , SA GZHI KENG RUS KYIS KHYAB PAR SNANG BA'I KENG RUS GSAL SNANG CAN GYI TING NGE 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA RNAMS KYI YUL DE DANG , DER YOD MED RNAM PA KUN DU MTSUNGS SAM MI MTSUNGS ZHE NA,",
    "wylie": "chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes sogs kyi skabs su mtha' dpyad pa la, chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i yi dvags kyi mig shes dang , bse ru'i nang du skra shad 'dzag par snang ba'i rab rib can gyi mig shes dang , sa gzhi keng rus kyis khyab par snang ba'i keng rus gsal snang can gyi ting nge 'dzin dang bcas pa rnams kyi yul de dang , der yod med rnam pa kun du mtshungs sam mi mtshungs zhe na",
    "english": "Here we will analyze the statement [from Entering the Middle Way, by Master Chandrakirti (650 AD)] where it says, \"...The mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\" One may begin with the following question: Let's consider the objects of the following states of mind: the visual consciousness of a craving spirit where a river of water looks like pus and blood; the visual consciousness of a person with a kind of cataract where a clean white porcelain basin looks like a hair has fallen into it; and that kind of meditation where you visualize skeletons--where you imagine that the entire surface of the earth is covered with the bones of corpses. Are all these objects completely equivalent, as far as being something that exists or doesn't exist?",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C18\", \"acip\": \"CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAG BLO YANG , , ZHES SOGS KYI SKABS SU MTHA' DPYAD PA LA, CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES DANG , BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR SNANG BA'I RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG SHES DANG , SA GZHI KENG RUS KYIS KHYAB PAR SNANG BA'I KENG RUS GSAL SNANG CAN GYI TING NGE 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA RNAMS KYI YUL DE DANG , DER YOD MED RNAM PA KUN DU MTSUNGS SAM MI MTSUNGS ZHE NA,\", \"wylie\": \"chu 'bab klung la yi dvags rnag blo yang , , zhes sogs kyi skabs su mtha' dpyad pa la, chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i yi dvags kyi mig shes dang , bse ru'i nang du skra shad 'dzag par snang ba'i rab rib can gyi mig shes dang , sa gzhi keng rus kyis khyab par snang ba'i keng rus gsal snang can gyi ting nge 'dzin dang bcas pa rnams kyi yul de dang , der yod med rnam pa kun du mtshungs sam mi mtshungs zhe na\", \"english\": \"Here we will analyze the statement [from Entering the Middle Way, by Master Chandrakirti (650 AD)] where it says, \\\"...The mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\\\" One may begin with the following question: Let's consider the objects of the following states of mind: the visual consciousness of a craving spirit where a river of water looks like pus and blood; the visual consciousness of a person with a kind of cataract where a clean white porcelain basin looks like a hair has fallen into it; and that kind of meditation where you visualize skeletons--where you imagine that the entire surface of the earth is covered with the bones of corpses. Are all these objects completely equivalent, as far as being something that exists or doesn't exist?\"}"
  },
  "24009": {
    "id": 24009,
    "course": "GIE",
    "seq": 497,
    "acip": "'PHAGS PA BRGYAD PA DANG, CHAGS BRAL SNGON SONG GI ZHUGS PA DBANG PO RDUL RNO GNYIS DANG, DE'I 'BRAS GNAS GNYIS DANG, LHA DANG MI'I RIGS NAS RIGS SKYE GNYIS DANG, TSE GCIG BAR CHAD GCIG PA DANG, PHYIR MI 'ONG BAR 'DA' BA DANG, SKYES TZAM NYID NAS 'DA' BA DANG, 'DU BYED DANG BCAS TE 'DA' BA DANG, 'DU BYED MED PAR 'DA' BA DANG, 'PHAR BA GSUM DANG, SRID RTZE'I MTHAR THUG 'GRO DANG, GZUGS MED NYER 'GRO DANG, MTHONG CHOS ZHI DANG, LUS MNGON BYED DANG, BSE RU LTA BU STE DGE 'DUN NYI SHU,",
    "wylie": "'phags pa brgyad pa dang, chags bral sngon song gi zhugs pa dbang po rdul rno gnyis dang, de'i 'bras gnas gnyis dang, lha dang mi'i rigs nas rigs skye gnyis dang, tshe gcig bar chad gcig pa dang, phyir mi 'ong bar 'da' ba dang, skyes tsam nyid nas 'da' ba dang, 'du byed dang bcas te 'da' ba dang, 'du byed med par 'da' ba dang, 'phar ba gsum dang, srid rtse'i mthar thug 'gro dang, gzugs med nyer 'gro dang, mthong chos zhi dang, lus mngon byed dang, bse ru lta bu ste dge 'dun nyi shu",
    "english": "Here then are the twenty divisions of practitioners: (1) realized beings of the eighth type; (2–3) practitioners of either dull or sharp powers, who are at the entering stage but have already eliminated desire; (4–5) the same practitioners at the stage where they are residing in the result; (6–7) the two types of beings who pass from one high state and take rebirth either among worldly gods or humans (a process called “from nobility to nobility”); (8) “single blockers,” who are kept from their goal only by a single lifetime; (9) non-returners who go beyond within the intermediate state; (10) those who go beyond from the moment they take rebirth; (11) those who go beyond as they apply themselves; (12) those who go beyond without applying themselves; (13–15) those of the three types of spiritual upgrading first; (16) those who first “go to the highest,” or the level called “Peak of Existence”; (17) those who first go to the formless realm; (18) those who experience peace as something they see in this life; (19) those who manifest in a body; and (20) those who are like a rhinoceros. Returning to Choney Lama's commentary:]",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"GIE\", \"acip\": \"'PHAGS PA BRGYAD PA DANG, CHAGS BRAL SNGON SONG GI ZHUGS PA DBANG PO RDUL RNO GNYIS DANG, DE'I 'BRAS GNAS GNYIS DANG, LHA DANG MI'I RIGS NAS RIGS SKYE GNYIS DANG, TSE GCIG BAR CHAD GCIG PA DANG, PHYIR MI 'ONG BAR 'DA' BA DANG, SKYES TZAM NYID NAS 'DA' BA DANG, 'DU BYED DANG BCAS TE 'DA' BA DANG, 'DU BYED MED PAR 'DA' BA DANG, 'PHAR BA GSUM DANG, SRID RTZE'I MTHAR THUG 'GRO DANG, GZUGS MED NYER 'GRO DANG, MTHONG CHOS ZHI DANG, LUS MNGON BYED DANG, BSE RU LTA BU STE DGE 'DUN NYI SHU,\", \"wylie\": \"'phags pa brgyad pa dang, chags bral sngon song gi zhugs pa dbang po rdul rno gnyis dang, de'i 'bras gnas gnyis dang, lha dang mi'i rigs nas rigs skye gnyis dang, tshe gcig bar chad gcig pa dang, phyir mi 'ong bar 'da' ba dang, skyes tsam nyid nas 'da' ba dang, 'du byed dang bcas te 'da' ba dang, 'du byed med par 'da' ba dang, 'phar ba gsum dang, srid rtse'i mthar thug 'gro dang, gzugs med nyer 'gro dang, mthong chos zhi dang, lus mngon byed dang, bse ru lta bu ste dge 'dun nyi shu\", \"english\": \"Here then are the twenty divisions of practitioners: (1) realized beings of the eighth type; (2–3) practitioners of either dull or sharp powers, who are at the entering stage but have already eliminated desire; (4–5) the same practitioners at the stage where they are residing in the result; (6–7) the two types of beings who pass from one high state and take rebirth either among worldly gods or humans (a process called “from nobility to nobility”); (8) “single blockers,” who are kept from their goal only by a single lifetime; (9) non-returners who go beyond within the intermediate state; (10) those who go beyond from the moment they take rebirth; (11) those who go beyond as they apply themselves; (12) those who go beyond without applying themselves; (13–15) those of the three types of spiritual upgrading first; (16) those who first “go to the highest,” or the level called “Peak of Existence”; (17) those who first go to the formless realm; (18) those who experience peace as something they see in this life; (19) those who manifest in a body; and (20) those who are like a rhinoceros. Returning to Choney Lama's commentary:]\"}"
  },
  "27849": {
    "id": 27849,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 530,
    "acip": "GNYIS PA NI, 'O NA MNGON RTOGS RGYAN LAS, ,GZUNG DON RTOG PA SPONG PHYIR DANG, ,'DZIN PA MI SPONG PHYIR DANG NI, ,RTEN GYIS BSE RU LTA BU'I LAM, ,YANG DAG BSDUS PAR SHES PAR BYA, ZHES RANG RGYAL GYI LAM GYIS GZUNG BA LA BDEN ZHEN GYI RTOG PA SPONG NUS KYANG, 'DZIN PA LA BDEN ZHEN SPONG MI NUS PAR GSUNGS PA DANG,",
    "wylie": "gnyis pa ni, 'o na mngon rtogs rgyan las, ,gzung don rtog pa spong phyir dang, ,'dzin pa mi spong phyir dang ni, ,rten gyis bse ru lta bu'i lam, ,yang dag bsdus par shes par bya, zhes rang rgyal gyi lam gyis gzung ba la bden zhen gyi rtog pa spong nus kyang, 'dzin pa la bden zhen spong mi nus par gsungs pa dang",
    "english": "This brings us to our second step from above: a refutation of arguments not covered in the autocommentary. “How then can it be correct,” one may ask, “that in the Jewel of Realizations it says that, by using the path of the self-made buddhas, one can rid oneself of the idea where we imagine that objects held by the mind could exist in truth; but at the same time are unable to rid ourselves of the idea that the states of mind which hold onto objects exist in truth? For that work does say, The path of those like a rhinoceros Is known as 'the path Which is perfectly combined,' Because one rids oneself Of wrong ideas of the object held; But not wrong ideas About what holds them; And because of the person Who follows it.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"GNYIS PA NI, 'O NA MNGON RTOGS RGYAN LAS, ,GZUNG DON RTOG PA SPONG PHYIR DANG, ,'DZIN PA MI SPONG PHYIR DANG NI, ,RTEN GYIS BSE RU LTA BU'I LAM, ,YANG DAG BSDUS PAR SHES PAR BYA, ZHES RANG RGYAL GYI LAM GYIS GZUNG BA LA BDEN ZHEN GYI RTOG PA SPONG NUS KYANG, 'DZIN PA LA BDEN ZHEN SPONG MI NUS PAR GSUNGS PA DANG,\", \"wylie\": \"gnyis pa ni, 'o na mngon rtogs rgyan las, ,gzung don rtog pa spong phyir dang, ,'dzin pa mi spong phyir dang ni, ,rten gyis bse ru lta bu'i lam, ,yang dag bsdus par shes par bya, zhes rang rgyal gyi lam gyis gzung ba la bden zhen gyi rtog pa spong nus kyang, 'dzin pa la bden zhen spong mi nus par gsungs pa dang\", \"english\": \"This brings us to our second step from above: a refutation of arguments not covered in the autocommentary. “How then can it be correct,” one may ask, “that in the Jewel of Realizations it says that, by using the path of the self-made buddhas, one can rid oneself of the idea where we imagine that objects held by the mind could exist in truth; but at the same time are unable to rid ourselves of the idea that the states of mind which hold onto objects exist in truth? For that work does say, The path of those like a rhinoceros Is known as 'the path Which is perfectly combined,' Because one rids oneself Of wrong ideas of the object held; But not wrong ideas About what holds them; And because of the person Who follows it.\"}"
  },
  "27850": {
    "id": 27850,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 531,
    "acip": "YANG ,NYON MONGS SHES BYA LAM GSUM GYI, ,NYAMS PHYIR SLOB MA BSE RU DANG, ,RGYAL SRAS RNAMS KYI DAG PA STE, ZHES GZUNG BA LA BDEN ZHEN SHES SGRIB TU GSUNGS PA JI LTAR DRANG ZHE NA,",
    "wylie": "yang ,nyon mongs shes bya lam gsum gyi, ,nyams phyir slob ma bse ru dang, ,rgyal sras rnams kyi dag pa ste, zhes gzung ba la bden zhen shes sgrib tu gsungs pa ji ltar drang zhe na",
    "english": "“And what of the part in the same text where it states that a belief that objects held by the mind exist in truth is an obstacle to knowledge? For it says— Those of the negative emotions And of the objects of knowledge Wither away through the use Of three different paths; Therefore the purity Of students who are rhinoceroses And those who are children Of the Buddhas…”",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"YANG ,NYON MONGS SHES BYA LAM GSUM GYI, ,NYAMS PHYIR SLOB MA BSE RU DANG, ,RGYAL SRAS RNAMS KYI DAG PA STE, ZHES GZUNG BA LA BDEN ZHEN SHES SGRIB TU GSUNGS PA JI LTAR DRANG ZHE NA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang ,nyon mongs shes bya lam gsum gyi, ,nyams phyir slob ma bse ru dang, ,rgyal sras rnams kyi dag pa ste, zhes gzung ba la bden zhen shes sgrib tu gsungs pa ji ltar drang zhe na\", \"english\": \"“And what of the part in the same text where it states that a belief that objects held by the mind exist in truth is an obstacle to knowledge? For it says— Those of the negative emotions And of the objects of knowledge Wither away through the use Of three different paths; Therefore the purity Of students who are rhinoceroses And those who are children Of the Buddhas…”\"}"
  },
  "28953": {
    "id": 28953,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1634,
    "acip": "MIG RAB RIB KYIS BSLAD PA'I MTHU YIS RAB RIB CAN GYIS RANG GI LAG GI BSE RU LA SOGS PA'I BZA' BTUNG GI SNOD KYI NANG DU, SKRA SHAD DANG LA SOGS PAS SBRANG MA LA SOGS PA MTHONG BA NA,",
    "wylie": "mig rab rib kyis bslad pa'i mthu yis rab rib can gyis rang gi lag gi bse ru la sogs pa'i bza' btung gi snod kyi nang du, skra shad dang la sogs pas sbrang ma la sogs pa mthong ba na",
    "english": "Suppose then that—due to the fact that a person's eyes are infected with some cataracts—a person who possesses such optical defects looks into a cup of horn held in their hand (or any similar vessel for eating or drinking), and perceives there a strand of hair “or something of the like” (which words indicate anything similar perceived inside the cup; say, a fly or the like).",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"MIG RAB RIB KYIS BSLAD PA'I MTHU YIS RAB RIB CAN GYIS RANG GI LAG GI BSE RU LA SOGS PA'I BZA' BTUNG GI SNOD KYI NANG DU, SKRA SHAD DANG LA SOGS PAS SBRANG MA LA SOGS PA MTHONG BA NA,\", \"wylie\": \"mig rab rib kyis bslad pa'i mthu yis rab rib can gyis rang gi lag gi bse ru la sogs pa'i bza' btung gi snod kyi nang du, skra shad dang la sogs pas sbrang ma la sogs pa mthong ba na\", \"english\": \"Suppose then that—due to the fact that a person's eyes are infected with some cataracts—a person who possesses such optical defects looks into a cup of horn held in their hand (or any similar vessel for eating or drinking), and perceives there a strand of hair “or something of the like” (which words indicate anything similar perceived inside the cup; say, a fly or the like).\"}"
  },
  "34699": {
    "id": 34699,
    "course": "P5",
    "seq": 122,
    "acip": "CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DWAGS RNAG BLO YANG ZHES SOGS KYI SKABS SU MTHA' DPYAD PA, LA CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I YI DWAGS KYI MIG SHES DANG BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR SNANG BA'I RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG SHES DANG SA GZHI KENG RUS KYIS KHYAB PAR SNANG BA'I KENG RUS GSAL SNANG CAN GYI TING NGE 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA RNAMS KYI YUL DE DANG DER YOD MED RNAM PA KUN DU MTSUNGS SAM MI MTSUNGS ZHE NA",
    "wylie": "chu 'bab klung la yi dwags rnag blo yang zhes sogs kyi skabs su mtha' dpyad pa, la chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i yi dwags kyi mig shes dang bse ru'i nang du skra shad 'dzag par snang ba'i rab rib can gyi mig shes dang sa gzhi keng rus kyis khyab par snang ba'i keng rus gsal snang can gyi ting nge 'dzin dang bcas pa rnams kyi yul de dang der yod med rnam pa kun du mtshungs sam mi mtshungs zhe na",
    "english": "Here we will analyze the statement [from Entering the Middle Way, by Master Chandrakirti (650 AD)] where it says, \"...The mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\" One may begin with the following question: Let's consider the objects of the following states of mind: the visual consciousness of a craving spirit where a river of water looks like pus and blood; the visual consciousness of a person with a kind of cataract where a clean white porcelain basin looks like a hair has fallen into it; and that kind of meditation where you visualize skeletons—where you imagine that the entire surface of the earth is covered with the bones of corpses. Are all these objects completely equivalent, as far as being something that exists or doesn't exist?",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"P5\", \"acip\": \"CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DWAGS RNAG BLO YANG ZHES SOGS KYI SKABS SU MTHA' DPYAD PA, LA CHU KLUNG RNAG KHRAG TU SNANG BA'I YI DWAGS KYI MIG SHES DANG BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR SNANG BA'I RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG SHES DANG SA GZHI KENG RUS KYIS KHYAB PAR SNANG BA'I KENG RUS GSAL SNANG CAN GYI TING NGE 'DZIN DANG BCAS PA RNAMS KYI YUL DE DANG DER YOD MED RNAM PA KUN DU MTSUNGS SAM MI MTSUNGS ZHE NA\", \"wylie\": \"chu 'bab klung la yi dwags rnag blo yang zhes sogs kyi skabs su mtha' dpyad pa, la chu klung rnag khrag tu snang ba'i yi dwags kyi mig shes dang bse ru'i nang du skra shad 'dzag par snang ba'i rab rib can gyi mig shes dang sa gzhi keng rus kyis khyab par snang ba'i keng rus gsal snang can gyi ting nge 'dzin dang bcas pa rnams kyi yul de dang der yod med rnam pa kun du mtshungs sam mi mtshungs zhe na\", \"english\": \"Here we will analyze the statement [from Entering the Middle Way, by Master Chandrakirti (650 AD)] where it says, \\\"...The mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\\\" One may begin with the following question: Let's consider the objects of the following states of mind: the visual consciousness of a craving spirit where a river of water looks like pus and blood; the visual consciousness of a person with a kind of cataract where a clean white porcelain basin looks like a hair has fallen into it; and that kind of meditation where you visualize skeletons—where you imagine that the entire surface of the earth is covered with the bones of corpses. Are all these objects completely equivalent, as far as being something that exists or doesn't exist?\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 0.993}"
  },
  "34934": {
    "id": 34934,
    "course": "P7",
    "seq": 45,
    "acip": "KHO NA RE DE LTAR CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR, THAL RAB RIB CAN GYIS BSE RU LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD MED PA GANG ZHIG RAB RIB DANG LDAN DBANG PO CAN MTSUNGS PA CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DWAGS RNAG BLO YANG ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR ZER NA",
    "wylie": "kho na re de ltar cig car du bltas pa'i tshe rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par, thal rab rib can gyis bse ru la bltas pa de'i tshe bse ru'i nang du skra shad med pa gang zhig rab rib dang ldan dbang po can mtshungs pa chu 'bab klung la yi dwags rnag blo yang zhes gsungs pa'i phyir zer na",
    "english": "Someone might make the following claim: Isn't it true that when all those beings sit down together and look at something, there isn't any actual pus and blood at all? Because isn't it true first of all that, when a person with cataracts looks into a porcelain basin, there is no strand of hair in the basin at all? And, secondly, doesn't [Master Dharmakirti's] text itself say, Identical to the case of someone where his sense power has a cataract, Is the mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"KHO NA RE DE LTAR CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR, THAL RAB RIB CAN GYIS BSE RU LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE BSE RU'I NANG DU SKRA SHAD MED PA GANG ZHIG RAB RIB DANG LDAN DBANG PO CAN MTSUNGS PA CHU 'BAB KLUNG LA YI DWAGS RNAG BLO YANG ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR ZER NA\", \"wylie\": \"kho na re de ltar cig car du bltas pa'i tshe rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par, thal rab rib can gyis bse ru la bltas pa de'i tshe bse ru'i nang du skra shad med pa gang zhig rab rib dang ldan dbang po can mtshungs pa chu 'bab klung la yi dwags rnag blo yang zhes gsungs pa'i phyir zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone might make the following claim: Isn't it true that when all those beings sit down together and look at something, there isn't any actual pus and blood at all? Because isn't it true first of all that, when a person with cataracts looks into a porcelain basin, there is no strand of hair in the basin at all? And, secondly, doesn't [Master Dharmakirti's] text itself say, Identical to the case of someone where his sense power has a cataract, Is the mind of a craving spirit as well, which sees a stream of water as pus.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 0.989}"
  },
  "29642": {
    "id": 29642,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2323,
    "acip": "[VI.71a] ,RAB RIB DANG LDAN DBANG PO CAN MTSUNGS LA, ,CHU 'BAB RLUNG {%KLUNG} LA YI DAGS {%DVAGS} RNAG BLO YANG,",
    "wylie": "[vi.71a] ,rab rib dang ldan dbang po can mtshungs la, ,chu 'bab rlung {%klung} la yi dags {%dvags} rnag blo yang",
    "english": "For cases equivalent To a state of mind With a sense power That has cataracts, There is also The state of mind Of pus for those Craving spirits Towards a stream Of fresh water.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"[VI.71a] ,RAB RIB DANG LDAN DBANG PO CAN MTSUNGS LA, ,CHU 'BAB RLUNG {%KLUNG} LA YI DAGS {%DVAGS} RNAG BLO YANG,\", \"wylie\": \"[vi.71a] ,rab rib dang ldan dbang po can mtshungs la, ,chu 'bab rlung {%klung} la yi dags {%dvags} rnag blo yang\", \"english\": \"For cases equivalent To a state of mind With a sense power That has cataracts, There is also The state of mind Of pus for those Craving spirits Towards a stream Of fresh water.\"}"
  },
  "33253": {
    "id": 33253,
    "course": "EM",
    "seq": 545,
    "acip": "[N48] ,RKYEN GYI TSIG DANG PHUNG PO DANG, ,RNAM PAR RIG PA GANG ZAG SEMS, ,CHOS RNAMS 'DI DAG MTSAN NYID NI, ,RMI LAM SKRA SHAD LTA BU YIN,",
    "wylie": "[n48] ,rkyen gyi tshig dang phung po dang, ,rnam par rig pa gang zag sems, ,chos rnams 'di dag mtshan nyid ni, ,rmi lam skra shad lta bu yin",
    "english": "The words that are The causes of things; And the parts to a person; And their capacity of thought; The person themselves, The mind itself— The qualities that make All these things what they are Are exactly as real As a dream; As a crack in your porcelain Bathroom sink That's really just A fallen hair.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"EM\", \"acip\": \"[N48] ,RKYEN GYI TSIG DANG PHUNG PO DANG, ,RNAM PAR RIG PA GANG ZAG SEMS, ,CHOS RNAMS 'DI DAG MTSAN NYID NI, ,RMI LAM SKRA SHAD LTA BU YIN,\", \"wylie\": \"[n48] ,rkyen gyi tshig dang phung po dang, ,rnam par rig pa gang zag sems, ,chos rnams 'di dag mtshan nyid ni, ,rmi lam skra shad lta bu yin\", \"english\": \"The words that are The causes of things; And the parts to a person; And their capacity of thought; The person themselves, The mind itself— The qualities that make All these things what they are Are exactly as real As a dream; As a crack in your porcelain Bathroom sink That's really just A fallen hair.\"}"
  },
  "28839": {
    "id": 28839,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1520,
    "acip": "GANG GI TSE GZHAN SKYE DGAG PA 'DI LA DON DAM GYI KHYAD PAR SBYOR BA YIN PA DE'I TSE, JI LTAR MIG NI RAB RIB CAN GYI SHES PAS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA DMIGS PAS, RAB RIB KYIS MA BSLAD PA'I SHES PA LA SKRA SHAD SOGS MI SNANG BA LA GNOD PA MIN PA",
    "wylie": "gang gi tshe gzhan skye dgag pa 'di la don dam gyi khyad par sbyor ba yin pa de'i tshe, ji ltar mig ni rab rib can gyi shes pas skra shad la sogs pa dmigs pas, rab rib kyis ma bslad pa'i shes pa la skra shad sogs mi snang ba la gnod pa min pa",
    "english": "Suppose that as we deny that things could grow from things other than themselves, we apply the distinction that we are referring here to growing in an ultimate sense. Think then of how a state of mind influenced by eyes clouded with cataracts perceives a strand of hair, or something similar. This state of mind cannot draw into question the way in which no strand of hair or the like ever makes its appearance to a state of mind which is not infected by such cataracts.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"GANG GI TSE GZHAN SKYE DGAG PA 'DI LA DON DAM GYI KHYAD PAR SBYOR BA YIN PA DE'I TSE, JI LTAR MIG NI RAB RIB CAN GYI SHES PAS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA DMIGS PAS, RAB RIB KYIS MA BSLAD PA'I SHES PA LA SKRA SHAD SOGS MI SNANG BA LA GNOD PA MIN PA\", \"wylie\": \"gang gi tshe gzhan skye dgag pa 'di la don dam gyi khyad par sbyor ba yin pa de'i tshe, ji ltar mig ni rab rib can gyi shes pas skra shad la sogs pa dmigs pas, rab rib kyis ma bslad pa'i shes pa la skra shad sogs mi snang ba la gnod pa min pa\", \"english\": \"Suppose that as we deny that things could grow from things other than themselves, we apply the distinction that we are referring here to growing in an ultimate sense. Think then of how a state of mind influenced by eyes clouded with cataracts perceives a strand of hair, or something similar. This state of mind cannot draw into question the way in which no strand of hair or the like ever makes its appearance to a state of mind which is not infected by such cataracts.\"}"
  },
  "28948": {
    "id": 28948,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1629,
    "acip": "DE'I DON NI DE KHO NA NYID KYI DON GZHAN LAS SHES PAR BYA BA MIN PAR GSUNGS PA'I 'GREL PA TSIG GSAL LAS, JI LTAR RAB RIB CAN DAG GIS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA'I NGO BO PHYIN CI LOG PA MTHONG BA NA, RAB RIB MED PAS BSTAN DU ZIN KYANG, RAB RIB MED PA LTAR SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA RANG GI NGO BO MA MTHONG BA'I TSUL GYIS, RTOGS PAR BYA BA JI LTAR GNAS PA BZHIN RTOGS PAR MI NUS KYI,",
    "wylie": "de'i don ni de kho na nyid kyi don gzhan las shes par bya ba min par gsungs pa'i 'grel pa tshig gsal las, ji ltar rab rib can dag gis skra shad la sogs pa'i ngo bo phyin ci log pa mthong ba na, rab rib med pas bstan du zin kyang, rab rib med pa ltar skra shad la sogs pa rang gi ngo bo ma mthong ba'i tshul gyis, rtogs par bya ba ji ltar gnas pa bzhin rtogs par mi nus kyi",
    "english": "The point here is explained by A Clarification of the Verses, commenting upon statements that the meaning of suchness is not something that can be learned from others: Suppose that those with cataracts see, mistakenly, something with the nature of a strand of hair, or the like. Someone who has no such cataracts can explain the situation to them, but still they will not be able to stop themselves from seeing the thing as having the nature of a hair or the like. As such, we say that a person is unable to understand something in the same way it exists for someone who does understand the thing; but rather…",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE'I DON NI DE KHO NA NYID KYI DON GZHAN LAS SHES PAR BYA BA MIN PAR GSUNGS PA'I 'GREL PA TSIG GSAL LAS, JI LTAR RAB RIB CAN DAG GIS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA'I NGO BO PHYIN CI LOG PA MTHONG BA NA, RAB RIB MED PAS BSTAN DU ZIN KYANG, RAB RIB MED PA LTAR SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA RANG GI NGO BO MA MTHONG BA'I TSUL GYIS, RTOGS PAR BYA BA JI LTAR GNAS PA BZHIN RTOGS PAR MI NUS KYI,\", \"wylie\": \"de'i don ni de kho na nyid kyi don gzhan las shes par bya ba min par gsungs pa'i 'grel pa tshig gsal las, ji ltar rab rib can dag gis skra shad la sogs pa'i ngo bo phyin ci log pa mthong ba na, rab rib med pas bstan du zin kyang, rab rib med pa ltar skra shad la sogs pa rang gi ngo bo ma mthong ba'i tshul gyis, rtogs par bya ba ji ltar gnas pa bzhin rtogs par mi nus kyi\", \"english\": \"The point here is explained by A Clarification of the Verses, commenting upon statements that the meaning of suchness is not something that can be learned from others: Suppose that those with cataracts see, mistakenly, something with the nature of a strand of hair, or the like. Someone who has no such cataracts can explain the situation to them, but still they will not be able to stop themselves from seeing the thing as having the nature of a hair or the like. As such, we say that a person is unable to understand something in the same way it exists for someone who does understand the thing; but rather…\"}"
  },
  "28949": {
    "id": 28949,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1630,
    "acip": "ZHES RAB RIB CAN LA RAB RIB MED PAS SKRA SHAD MED DO ZHES BSTAN KYANG, RAB RIB MED PAS MTHONG BA 'DRA BA'I SKRA SHAD MED PA MI RTOGS PAR GSUNGS PAS, NYAN PA POS DE LTAR MA RTOGS KYANG SKRA SHAD MED PA MI RTOGS PA MIN NO,,",
    "wylie": "zhes rab rib can la rab rib med pas skra shad med do zhes bstan kyang, rab rib med pas mthong ba 'dra ba'i skra shad med pa mi rtogs par gsungs pas, nyan pa pos de ltar ma rtogs kyang skra shad med pa mi rtogs pa min no",
    "english": "What this is saying is that a person who has no cataracts can say to a person who still does have them, “There's no strand of hair there!”—but the latter person will not be able to realize the lack of a hair there in the same way that the person with no cataracts sees this. We can say though that even though the person listening to the correct explanation cannot realize this object in the same way, it's not though the case that they have no realization at all then that there is no hair there.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"ZHES RAB RIB CAN LA RAB RIB MED PAS SKRA SHAD MED DO ZHES BSTAN KYANG, RAB RIB MED PAS MTHONG BA 'DRA BA'I SKRA SHAD MED PA MI RTOGS PAR GSUNGS PAS, NYAN PA POS DE LTAR MA RTOGS KYANG SKRA SHAD MED PA MI RTOGS PA MIN NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"zhes rab rib can la rab rib med pas skra shad med do zhes bstan kyang, rab rib med pas mthong ba 'dra ba'i skra shad med pa mi rtogs par gsungs pas, nyan pa pos de ltar ma rtogs kyang skra shad med pa mi rtogs pa min no\", \"english\": \"What this is saying is that a person who has no cataracts can say to a person who still does have them, “There's no strand of hair there!”—but the latter person will not be able to realize the lack of a hair there in the same way that the person with no cataracts sees this. We can say though that even though the person listening to the correct explanation cannot realize this object in the same way, it's not though the case that they have no realization at all then that there is no hair there.\"}"
  },
  "28952": {
    "id": 28952,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1633,
    "acip": ",RAB RIB MTHU YIS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA'I, ,NGO BO LOG PA GANG ZHIG RNAM BRTAGS PA, ,DE NYID BDAG NYID GANG DU MIG DAG PAS, ,MTHONG DE DE NYID DE BZHIN 'DIR SHES KYIS {%GYIS},",
    "wylie": "rab rib mthu yis skra shad la sogs pa'i, ,ngo bo log pa gang zhig rnam brtags pa, ,de nyid bdag nyid gang du mig dag pas, ,mthong de de nyid de bzhin 'dir shes kyis {%gyis}",
    "english": "Suppose that a person, Due to some cataracts, Thinks they see something As different than it really is: They perceive a strand Of hair, or something of the like. And now suppose a person With clear eyes looks down Where those actual things Are supposed to be. They are the one Who sees the real; Understand the case here As being the same.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \",RAB RIB MTHU YIS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA'I, ,NGO BO LOG PA GANG ZHIG RNAM BRTAGS PA, ,DE NYID BDAG NYID GANG DU MIG DAG PAS, ,MTHONG DE DE NYID DE BZHIN 'DIR SHES KYIS {%GYIS},\", \"wylie\": \"rab rib mthu yis skra shad la sogs pa'i, ,ngo bo log pa gang zhig rnam brtags pa, ,de nyid bdag nyid gang du mig dag pas, ,mthong de de nyid de bzhin 'dir shes kyis {%gyis}\", \"english\": \"Suppose that a person, Due to some cataracts, Thinks they see something As different than it really is: They perceive a strand Of hair, or something of the like. And now suppose a person With clear eyes looks down Where those actual things Are supposed to be. They are the one Who sees the real; Understand the case here As being the same.\"}"
  },
  "28954": {
    "id": 28954,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1635,
    "acip": "SKRA SHAD DANG SBRANG MA SOGS YIN PAR NGO BO LOG PA GANG ZHIG RNAM PAR BRTAGS PA NA, DE BSAL BAR 'DOD NAS SNOD DE LA YANG DANG YANG DU BSGRE BZLOG BYED PA'I TSEGS THOB PAR RIG STE,",
    "wylie": "skra shad dang sbrang ma sogs yin par ngo bo log pa gang zhig rnam par brtags pa na, de bsal bar 'dod nas snod de la yang dang yang du bsgre bzlog byed pa'i tshegs thob par rig ste",
    "english": "When they think that they see something then, as different than it really is—when they see the hair or the fly or whatever it may be—then naturally they would want to get it out of the cup, and so it would be completely appropriate for them to go to the trouble of turning the cup upside down and knocking it against the table repeatedly, to get the unwanted thing out.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"SKRA SHAD DANG SBRANG MA SOGS YIN PAR NGO BO LOG PA GANG ZHIG RNAM PAR BRTAGS PA NA, DE BSAL BAR 'DOD NAS SNOD DE LA YANG DANG YANG DU BSGRE BZLOG BYED PA'I TSEGS THOB PAR RIG STE,\", \"wylie\": \"skra shad dang sbrang ma sogs yin par ngo bo log pa gang zhig rnam par brtags pa na, de bsal bar 'dod nas snod de la yang dang yang du bsgre bzlog byed pa'i tshegs thob par rig ste\", \"english\": \"When they think that they see something then, as different than it really is—when they see the hair or the fly or whatever it may be—then naturally they would want to get it out of the cup, and so it would be completely appropriate for them to go to the trouble of turning the cup upside down and knocking it against the table repeatedly, to get the unwanted thing out.\"}"
  },
  "28955": {
    "id": 28955,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1636,
    "acip": "MIG DAG PA'I RAB RIB MED PAS DE'I GAN DU PHYIN NAS, SKRA SHAD SOGS DE DAG NYID KYI BDAG NYID GZHI GANG DU MTHONG BA DER MIG GTAD KYANG SKRA SHAD KYI RNAM PA DE DAG MA DMIGS SHING, SKRA SHAD DE'I RTEN CAN TE KHYAD PAR GYI CHOS GANG YANG RTOG PAR MI BYED DO,,",
    "wylie": "mig dag pa'i rab rib med pas de'i gan du phyin nas, skra shad sogs de dag nyid kyi bdag nyid gzhi gang du mthong ba der mig gtad kyang skra shad kyi rnam pa de dag ma dmigs shing, skra shad de'i rten can te khyad par gyi chos gang yang rtog par mi byed do",
    "english": "And then suppose that a person with clear eyes—someone without any cataracts—walks over to this other fellow and looks down to the spot where the first person thought they saw one of those actual things: the strand of hair or the like.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"MIG DAG PA'I RAB RIB MED PAS DE'I GAN DU PHYIN NAS, SKRA SHAD SOGS DE DAG NYID KYI BDAG NYID GZHI GANG DU MTHONG BA DER MIG GTAD KYANG SKRA SHAD KYI RNAM PA DE DAG MA DMIGS SHING, SKRA SHAD DE'I RTEN CAN TE KHYAD PAR GYI CHOS GANG YANG RTOG PAR MI BYED DO,,\", \"wylie\": \"mig dag pa'i rab rib med pas de'i gan du phyin nas, skra shad sogs de dag nyid kyi bdag nyid gzhi gang du mthong ba der mig gtad kyang skra shad kyi rnam pa de dag ma dmigs shing, skra shad de'i rten can te khyad par gyi chos gang yang rtog par mi byed do\", \"english\": \"And then suppose that a person with clear eyes—someone without any cataracts—walks over to this other fellow and looks down to the spot where the first person thought they saw one of those actual things: the strand of hair or the like.\"}"
  },
  "28956": {
    "id": 28956,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1637,
    "acip": "YANG GANG GI TSE RAB RIB CAN GYIS RAB RIB MED PA LA, SKRA SHAD MTHONG NGO ZHES RANG GI BSAM PA STON PAR BYED PA DE'I TSE,",
    "wylie": "yang gang gi tshe rab rib can gyis rab rib med pa la, skra shad mthong ngo zhes rang gi bsam pa ston par byed pa de'i tshe",
    "english": "And suppose then that the person with the cataracts expresses what they think to the person who has no cataracts: “I see a strand of hair there.”",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"YANG GANG GI TSE RAB RIB CAN GYIS RAB RIB MED PA LA, SKRA SHAD MTHONG NGO ZHES RANG GI BSAM PA STON PAR BYED PA DE'I TSE,\", \"wylie\": \"yang gang gi tshe rab rib can gyis rab rib med pa la, skra shad mthong ngo zhes rang gi bsam pa ston par byed pa de'i tshe\", \"english\": \"And suppose then that the person with the cataracts expresses what they think to the person who has no cataracts: “I see a strand of hair there.”\"}"
  },
  "28957": {
    "id": 28957,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1638,
    "acip": "RAB RIB CAN GYI RNAM RTOG BSAL BAR 'DOD PAS DE'I NGOR BYAS NAS, 'DI NA SKRA SHAD MED DO ZHES DGAG PA LHUR BYED PA'I TSIG SMRA MOD KYANG, SMRA BA PO 'DI LA SKRA SHAD LA SKUR PA 'DEBS PA MED DO,,",
    "wylie": "rab rib can gyi rnam rtog bsal bar 'dod pas de'i ngor byas nas, 'di na skra shad med do zhes dgag pa lhur byed pa'i tshig smra mod kyang, smra ba po 'di la skra shad la skur pa 'debs pa med do",
    "english": "This other person will naturally then want to clear up the misperception that the one with the cataracts is having, and will do their very best to say something to help them stop their mistake: “But look, actually there's no strand of hair there at all.” We could not though in this case say that the speaker was trying to deny a hair that was actually there.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"RAB RIB CAN GYI RNAM RTOG BSAL BAR 'DOD PAS DE'I NGOR BYAS NAS, 'DI NA SKRA SHAD MED DO ZHES DGAG PA LHUR BYED PA'I TSIG SMRA MOD KYANG, SMRA BA PO 'DI LA SKRA SHAD LA SKUR PA 'DEBS PA MED DO,,\", \"wylie\": \"rab rib can gyi rnam rtog bsal bar 'dod pas de'i ngor byas nas, 'di na skra shad med do zhes dgag pa lhur byed pa'i tshig smra mod kyang, smra ba po 'di la skra shad la skur pa 'debs pa med do\", \"english\": \"This other person will naturally then want to clear up the misperception that the one with the cataracts is having, and will do their very best to say something to help them stop their mistake: “But look, actually there's no strand of hair there at all.” We could not though in this case say that the speaker was trying to deny a hair that was actually there.\"}"
  },
  "28958": {
    "id": 28958,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1639,
    "acip": "RAB RIB CAN GYIS MTHONG BA'I SKRA SHAD KYI DE KHO NA NYID NI, RAB RIB MED PAS MTHONG BA DE YIN GYI, RAB RIB CAN GYIS MTHONG BA DE MIN NO,,",
    "wylie": "rab rib can gyis mthong ba'i skra shad kyi de kho na nyid ni, rab rib med pas mthong ba de yin gyi, rab rib can gyis mthong ba de min no",
    "english": "The one who sees the real nature—the suchness—of the hair that the person with the cataracts perceives is the person without the cataracts; it is not in fact something that the person with the cataracts is capable of perceiving.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"RAB RIB CAN GYIS MTHONG BA'I SKRA SHAD KYI DE KHO NA NYID NI, RAB RIB MED PAS MTHONG BA DE YIN GYI, RAB RIB CAN GYIS MTHONG BA DE MIN NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"rab rib can gyis mthong ba'i skra shad kyi de kho na nyid ni, rab rib med pas mthong ba de yin gyi, rab rib can gyis mthong ba de min no\", \"english\": \"The one who sees the real nature—the suchness—of the hair that the person with the cataracts perceives is the person without the cataracts; it is not in fact something that the person with the cataracts is capable of perceiving.\"}"
  },
  "28960": {
    "id": 28960,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1641,
    "acip": ",SHES PAR BYED TSUL NI, MA RIG PA'I RAB RIB KYIS GNOD PA BYAS PA STE BLO BSLAD PAS DE KHO NA NYID MA MTHONG BA RNAMS KYIS, PHUNG PO DANG KHAMS DANG SKYE MCHED LA SOGS PA'I RANG GI NGO BO DMIGS PA NI, PHUNG SOGS DE DAG GI NGO BO KUN RDZOB PA STE RAB RIB CAN GYIS SKRA SHAD DMIGS PA LTA BU'O,,",
    "wylie": "shes par byed tshul ni, ma rig pa'i rab rib kyis gnod pa byas pa ste blo bslad pas de kho na nyid ma mthong ba rnams kyis, phung po dang khams dang skye mched la sogs pa'i rang gi ngo bo dmigs pa ni, phung sogs de dag gi ngo bo kun rdzob pa ste rab rib can gyis skra shad dmigs pa lta bu'o",
    "english": "And just how should we accomplish this understanding? Those who are being hurt by the cataracts of misunderstanding—those whose minds are infected—cannot see the real nature of things: their suchness. When they look upon the nature of the parts of a person; and the categories of things; and the doors of sense and all of the like, they see the nature of these things that deceives. They are just like those people with cataracts, who perceive a strand of hair.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \",SHES PAR BYED TSUL NI, MA RIG PA'I RAB RIB KYIS GNOD PA BYAS PA STE BLO BSLAD PAS DE KHO NA NYID MA MTHONG BA RNAMS KYIS, PHUNG PO DANG KHAMS DANG SKYE MCHED LA SOGS PA'I RANG GI NGO BO DMIGS PA NI, PHUNG SOGS DE DAG GI NGO BO KUN RDZOB PA STE RAB RIB CAN GYIS SKRA SHAD DMIGS PA LTA BU'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"shes par byed tshul ni, ma rig pa'i rab rib kyis gnod pa byas pa ste blo bslad pas de kho na nyid ma mthong ba rnams kyis, phung po dang khams dang skye mched la sogs pa'i rang gi ngo bo dmigs pa ni, phung sogs de dag gi ngo bo kun rdzob pa ste rab rib can gyis skra shad dmigs pa lta bu'o\", \"english\": \"And just how should we accomplish this understanding? Those who are being hurt by the cataracts of misunderstanding—those whose minds are infected—cannot see the real nature of things: their suchness. When they look upon the nature of the parts of a person; and the categories of things; and the doors of sense and all of the like, they see the nature of these things that deceives. They are just like those people with cataracts, who perceive a strand of hair.\"}"
  },
  "28962": {
    "id": 28962,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1643,
    "acip": "RAB RIB CAN MA YIN PA'I MIG GIS SKRA SHAD MA MTHONG BA'I TSUL DU, PHUNG SOGS KYI RANG BZHIN GANG DU GZIGS PA'I YUL DE NI SANGS RGYAS DE RNAMS KYI DON DAM PA'I BDEN PA'O,,",
    "wylie": "rab rib can ma yin pa'i mig gis skra shad ma mthong ba'i tshul du, phung sogs kyi rang bzhin gang du gzigs pa'i yul de ni sangs rgyas de rnams kyi don dam pa'i bden pa'o",
    "english": "They are considering these things just as the person whose eyes have no cataracts, and who sees no strands of hair. They look at the same thing—the same parts of the person and so on that were seen as having some nature of their own—and perceive it as the ultimate reality of the Buddhas. Knowing by failing to see",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"RAB RIB CAN MA YIN PA'I MIG GIS SKRA SHAD MA MTHONG BA'I TSUL DU, PHUNG SOGS KYI RANG BZHIN GANG DU GZIGS PA'I YUL DE NI SANGS RGYAS DE RNAMS KYI DON DAM PA'I BDEN PA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"rab rib can ma yin pa'i mig gis skra shad ma mthong ba'i tshul du, phung sogs kyi rang bzhin gang du gzigs pa'i yul de ni sangs rgyas de rnams kyi don dam pa'i bden pa'o\", \"english\": \"They are considering these things just as the person whose eyes have no cataracts, and who sees no strands of hair. They look at the same thing—the same parts of the person and so on that were seen as having some nature of their own—and perceive it as the ultimate reality of the Buddhas. Knowing by failing to see\"}"
  },
  "28964": {
    "id": 28964,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1645,
    "acip": "GAL TE RAB RIB MED PA'I MIG GIS SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA TZAM YANG MA MTHONG BA BZHIN DU, SANGS RGYAS KYIS MA RIG PAS BSLAD PA'I BLO LA SNANG BA'I PHUNG SOGS KUN RDZOB PA MA GZIGS NA, DE RNAMS MED PAR 'GYUR TE, YOD NA NI SANGS RGYAS KYIS GZIGS DGOS PA'I PHYIR RO, ,PHUNG SOGS KUN RDZOB PA RNAMS MED NA NI SANGS RGYAS THOB PA YANG MED PAR 'GYUR TE, DANG POR SEMS BSKYED PA'I GANG ZAG NI MA RIG PAS BSLAD PA CAN YIN PA'I PHYIR RO ZHE NA,",
    "wylie": "gal te rab rib med pa'i mig gis skra shad du snang ba tsam yang ma mthong ba bzhin du, sangs rgyas kyis ma rig pas bslad pa'i blo la snang ba'i phung sogs kun rdzob pa ma gzigs na, de rnams med par 'gyur te, yod na ni sangs rgyas kyis gzigs dgos pa'i phyir ro, ,phung sogs kun rdzob pa rnams med na ni sangs rgyas thob pa yang med par 'gyur te, dang por sems bskyed pa'i gang zag ni ma rig pas bslad pa can yin pa'i phyir ro zhe na",
    "english": "One may come then with the following argument: A person whose eyes are free of any cataracts doesn't perceive even the false appearance of a strand of hair. And a Buddha could never perceive the deceptive forms of the parts to a person and so on which make their appearance to a state of mind which is infected by misunderstanding. And that's simply because—if anything does in fact exist—then it has to be seen by an Enlightened Being. But if, on the other hand, the deceptive forms of the parts to a person and so on didn't exist at all, then there would exist no such thing as someone achieving enlightenment. And that in turn is because a person who first attains the Wish to achieve enlightenment for all living beings is someone who is still infected by misunderstanding.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"GAL TE RAB RIB MED PA'I MIG GIS SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA TZAM YANG MA MTHONG BA BZHIN DU, SANGS RGYAS KYIS MA RIG PAS BSLAD PA'I BLO LA SNANG BA'I PHUNG SOGS KUN RDZOB PA MA GZIGS NA, DE RNAMS MED PAR 'GYUR TE, YOD NA NI SANGS RGYAS KYIS GZIGS DGOS PA'I PHYIR RO, ,PHUNG SOGS KUN RDZOB PA RNAMS MED NA NI SANGS RGYAS THOB PA YANG MED PAR 'GYUR TE, DANG POR SEMS BSKYED PA'I GANG ZAG NI MA RIG PAS BSLAD PA CAN YIN PA'I PHYIR RO ZHE NA,\", \"wylie\": \"gal te rab rib med pa'i mig gis skra shad du snang ba tsam yang ma mthong ba bzhin du, sangs rgyas kyis ma rig pas bslad pa'i blo la snang ba'i phung sogs kun rdzob pa ma gzigs na, de rnams med par 'gyur te, yod na ni sangs rgyas kyis gzigs dgos pa'i phyir ro, ,phung sogs kun rdzob pa rnams med na ni sangs rgyas thob pa yang med par 'gyur te, dang por sems bskyed pa'i gang zag ni ma rig pas bslad pa can yin pa'i phyir ro zhe na\", \"english\": \"One may come then with the following argument: A person whose eyes are free of any cataracts doesn't perceive even the false appearance of a strand of hair. And a Buddha could never perceive the deceptive forms of the parts to a person and so on which make their appearance to a state of mind which is infected by misunderstanding. And that's simply because—if anything does in fact exist—then it has to be seen by an Enlightened Being. But if, on the other hand, the deceptive forms of the parts to a person and so on didn't exist at all, then there would exist no such thing as someone achieving enlightenment. And that in turn is because a person who first attains the Wish to achieve enlightenment for all living beings is someone who is still infected by misunderstanding.\"}"
  },
  "28970": {
    "id": 28970,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1651,
    "acip": "RAB RIB DANG BRAL BA'I MIG SHES LA, RAB RIB CAN LA SNANG BA'I SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA MED KYANG, SNANG BA DE MED MI DGOS PAS SANGS RGYAS DANG MI 'DRA'O,,",
    "wylie": "rab rib dang bral ba'i mig shes la, rab rib can la snang ba'i skra shad du snang ba med kyang, snang ba de med mi dgos pas sangs rgyas dang mi 'dra'o",
    "english": "There is no such thing as something making its appearance as the strand of hair that appears to a person who has cataracts, as one which appears to a person whose visual consciousness is free of such cataracts. Nonetheless, it is not the case that the appearance would have to be something that did not exist; thus the case is different from that with an Enlightened Being.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"RAB RIB DANG BRAL BA'I MIG SHES LA, RAB RIB CAN LA SNANG BA'I SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA MED KYANG, SNANG BA DE MED MI DGOS PAS SANGS RGYAS DANG MI 'DRA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"rab rib dang bral ba'i mig shes la, rab rib can la snang ba'i skra shad du snang ba med kyang, snang ba de med mi dgos pas sangs rgyas dang mi 'dra'o\", \"english\": \"There is no such thing as something making its appearance as the strand of hair that appears to a person who has cataracts, as one which appears to a person whose visual consciousness is free of such cataracts. Nonetheless, it is not the case that the appearance would have to be something that did not exist; thus the case is different from that with an Enlightened Being.\"}"
  },
  "29148": {
    "id": 29148,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1829,
    "acip": "'DIR GZUGS BRNYAN LAS NI DE 'DZIN PA'I MIG SHES SKYE BAR GSUNGS PAS, GZUGS BRNYAN DNGOS PO YIN ZHING SHES PA DANG NGO BO THA DAD PAS NI, PHYI ROL GYI DON YIN LA DE YANG MIG SHES KYI DMIGS RKYEN YIN PAS, GZUGS KYI SKYE MCHED DU BZHED PA YIN TE, ZLA GNYIS DANG SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA DANG SGYU MA SOGS DANG, SGRA BRNYAN SOGS LA YANG DE BZHIN DU SHES PAR BYA'O,,",
    "wylie": "'dir gzugs brnyan las ni de 'dzin pa'i mig shes skye bar gsungs pas, gzugs brnyan dngos po yin zhing shes pa dang ngo bo tha dad pas ni, phyi rol gyi don yin la de yang mig shes kyi dmigs rkyen yin pas, gzugs kyi skye mched du bzhed pa yin te, zla gnyis dang skra shad du snang ba dang sgyu ma sogs dang, sgra brnyan sogs la yang de bzhin du shes par bya'o",
    "english": "When this says that the visual awareness which grasps to a reflection has grown from a reflection, what it's saying is that the reflection itself is an actual thing, and that it is not such that to be the awareness of it is to be the reflection. As such, the reflection is an outside object, and is serving as an “object factor” for the arisal of the visual consciousness. Thus then the reflection is accepted here as belonging to the door of sense of visible form. You should understand appearances such as the doubled moon, and the hair in the sink—as well as illusions and echoes and the like—as being the same.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"'DIR GZUGS BRNYAN LAS NI DE 'DZIN PA'I MIG SHES SKYE BAR GSUNGS PAS, GZUGS BRNYAN DNGOS PO YIN ZHING SHES PA DANG NGO BO THA DAD PAS NI, PHYI ROL GYI DON YIN LA DE YANG MIG SHES KYI DMIGS RKYEN YIN PAS, GZUGS KYI SKYE MCHED DU BZHED PA YIN TE, ZLA GNYIS DANG SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA DANG SGYU MA SOGS DANG, SGRA BRNYAN SOGS LA YANG DE BZHIN DU SHES PAR BYA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"'dir gzugs brnyan las ni de 'dzin pa'i mig shes skye bar gsungs pas, gzugs brnyan dngos po yin zhing shes pa dang ngo bo tha dad pas ni, phyi rol gyi don yin la de yang mig shes kyi dmigs rkyen yin pas, gzugs kyi skye mched du bzhed pa yin te, zla gnyis dang skra shad du snang ba dang sgyu ma sogs dang, sgra brnyan sogs la yang de bzhin du shes par bya'o\", \"english\": \"When this says that the visual awareness which grasps to a reflection has grown from a reflection, what it's saying is that the reflection itself is an actual thing, and that it is not such that to be the awareness of it is to be the reflection. As such, the reflection is an outside object, and is serving as an “object factor” for the arisal of the visual consciousness. Thus then the reflection is accepted here as belonging to the door of sense of visible form. You should understand appearances such as the doubled moon, and the hair in the sink—as well as illusions and echoes and the like—as being the same.\"}"
  },
  "29149": {
    "id": 29149,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1830,
    "acip": "DE LTAR NA DBANG SHES 'KHRUL PA LA SNANG BA'I BYAD DANG ZLA GNYIS DANG SKRA SHAD SOGS NI, DBANG PO LA 'PHRAL GYI GNOD PA MED PA'I SHES PA LNGA LA SNANG BA'I RANG GI MTSAN NYID KYIS GRUB PA DANG 'DRA'O,,",
    "wylie": "de ltar na dbang shes 'khrul pa la snang ba'i byad dang zla gnyis dang skra shad sogs ni, dbang po la 'phral gyi gnod pa med pa'i shes pa lnga la snang ba'i rang gi mtshan nyid kyis grub pa dang 'dra'o",
    "english": "And so what's being said here is that when our sense perceptions are mistaken, and an object which is actually just a reflection in the mirror appears to us—or we see things like the two moons, or a hair lying there—it is similar to the case with our five sense perceptions when they are not affected by any adventitious defect, but things appear to them to exist by definition.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE LTAR NA DBANG SHES 'KHRUL PA LA SNANG BA'I BYAD DANG ZLA GNYIS DANG SKRA SHAD SOGS NI, DBANG PO LA 'PHRAL GYI GNOD PA MED PA'I SHES PA LNGA LA SNANG BA'I RANG GI MTSAN NYID KYIS GRUB PA DANG 'DRA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"de ltar na dbang shes 'khrul pa la snang ba'i byad dang zla gnyis dang skra shad sogs ni, dbang po la 'phral gyi gnod pa med pa'i shes pa lnga la snang ba'i rang gi mtshan nyid kyis grub pa dang 'dra'o\", \"english\": \"And so what's being said here is that when our sense perceptions are mistaken, and an object which is actually just a reflection in the mirror appears to us—or we see things like the two moons, or a hair lying there—it is similar to the case with our five sense perceptions when they are not affected by any adventitious defect, but things appear to them to exist by definition.\"}"
  },
  "29291": {
    "id": 29291,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1972,
    "acip": ",JI LTAR YUL NI YOD NYID MIN MTSUNGS KYANG, ,RAB RIB CAN GYIS SKRA SHAD RNAM PAR NI, ,MTHONG GI DNGOS GZHAN RNAM PAR MA YIN LTAR, ,DE BZHIN SMIN LAS SLAR SMIN MIN SHES KYIS {%GYIS},",
    "wylie": "ji ltar yul ni yod nyid min mtshungs kyang, ,rab rib can gyis skra shad rnam par ni, ,mthong gi dngos gzhan rnam par ma yin ltar, ,de bzhin smin las slar smin min shes kyis {%gyis}",
    "english": "It's also like objects Equivalent in the way They're not really there; Those with cataracts Are sure they see Strands of hair, But other things That we don't even see. Similarly, You should understand That it's not as if What has ripened Will ripen once again.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \",JI LTAR YUL NI YOD NYID MIN MTSUNGS KYANG, ,RAB RIB CAN GYIS SKRA SHAD RNAM PAR NI, ,MTHONG GI DNGOS GZHAN RNAM PAR MA YIN LTAR, ,DE BZHIN SMIN LAS SLAR SMIN MIN SHES KYIS {%GYIS},\", \"wylie\": \"ji ltar yul ni yod nyid min mtshungs kyang, ,rab rib can gyis skra shad rnam par ni, ,mthong gi dngos gzhan rnam par ma yin ltar, ,de bzhin smin las slar smin min shes kyis {%gyis}\", \"english\": \"It's also like objects Equivalent in the way They're not really there; Those with cataracts Are sure they see Strands of hair, But other things That we don't even see. Similarly, You should understand That it's not as if What has ripened Will ripen once again.\"}"
  },
  "29292": {
    "id": 29292,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1973,
    "acip": "JI LTAR TE DPER NA YUL NI YOD PA NYID MIN PAR MTSUNGS BZHIN DU, RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG GIS YOD PA MIN PA'I SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA'I RNAM PAR NI MTHONG GI ,DE LAS GZHAN PA'I DNGOS PO'I RNAM PA BONG BU'I RVA DANG, MO GSHAM GYI BU LA SOGS PA'I RNAM PAR MTHONG BA MA YIN PA LTAR,",
    "wylie": "ji ltar te dper na yul ni yod pa nyid min par mtshungs bzhin du, rab rib can gyi mig gis yod pa min pa'i skra shad la sogs pa'i rnam par ni mthong gi ,de las gzhan pa'i dngos po'i rnam pa bong bu'i rva dang, mo gsham gyi bu la sogs pa'i rnam par mthong ba ma yin pa ltar",
    "english": "It's like, for example, how specific objects might be equivalent in the way that they are not really there. Eyes affected by cataracts are sure they see strands of hair or the like which don't even exist; but there are other appearances of things that we don't even see—things like the horns on a rabbit's head, or the child of a barren woman.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"JI LTAR TE DPER NA YUL NI YOD PA NYID MIN PAR MTSUNGS BZHIN DU, RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG GIS YOD PA MIN PA'I SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA'I RNAM PAR NI MTHONG GI ,DE LAS GZHAN PA'I DNGOS PO'I RNAM PA BONG BU'I RVA DANG, MO GSHAM GYI BU LA SOGS PA'I RNAM PAR MTHONG BA MA YIN PA LTAR,\", \"wylie\": \"ji ltar te dper na yul ni yod pa nyid min par mtshungs bzhin du, rab rib can gyi mig gis yod pa min pa'i skra shad la sogs pa'i rnam par ni mthong gi ,de las gzhan pa'i dngos po'i rnam pa bong bu'i rva dang, mo gsham gyi bu la sogs pa'i rnam par mthong ba ma yin pa ltar\", \"english\": \"It's like, for example, how specific objects might be equivalent in the way that they are not really there. Eyes affected by cataracts are sure they see strands of hair or the like which don't even exist; but there are other appearances of things that we don't even see—things like the horns on a rabbit's head, or the child of a barren woman.\"}"
  },
  "29296": {
    "id": 29296,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 1977,
    "acip": "RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG GIS SKRA SHAD SOGS MTHONG BA DANG, BONG BU'I RVA LA SOGS PA MI MTHONG BA'I NGES PA DE'I PHYIR RNAM SMIN YID DU 'ONG BA NI MI DGE BA LAS MI 'BYUNG LA, RNAM SMIN YID DU MI 'ONG BA NI DGE BA LAS MA YIN PA'I PHYIR, RNAM SMIN MI DGE BA STE YID DU MI 'ONG BA NI, NAG PO'I LAS LAS YIN LA, RNAM SMIN DGE BA STE YID DU 'ONG BA NI DGE BA LAS 'BYUNG BA YIN PAR MTHONG ZHING,",
    "wylie": "rab rib can gyi mig gis skra shad sogs mthong ba dang, bong bu'i rva la sogs pa mi mthong ba'i nges pa de'i phyir rnam smin yid du 'ong ba ni mi dge ba las mi 'byung la, rnam smin yid du mi 'ong ba ni dge ba las ma yin pa'i phyir, rnam smin mi dge ba ste yid du mi 'ong ba ni, nag po'i las las yin la, rnam smin dge ba ste yid du 'ong ba ni dge ba las 'byung ba yin par mthong zhing",
    "english": "Eyes affected by cataracts see things like strands of hair; but it is definite that they do not see things like horns on top of the head of a rabbit. As such, it is not the case that a pleasant ripening of karma can come from a non-virtuous deed; and neither can an unpleasant ripening come from a virtuous deed. We can see then that a non-virtuous ripening—an unpleasant one—is something that comes from a black karma; while a virtuous ripening—a pleasant one—is something that comes from a good deed.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"RAB RIB CAN GYI MIG GIS SKRA SHAD SOGS MTHONG BA DANG, BONG BU'I RVA LA SOGS PA MI MTHONG BA'I NGES PA DE'I PHYIR RNAM SMIN YID DU 'ONG BA NI MI DGE BA LAS MI 'BYUNG LA, RNAM SMIN YID DU MI 'ONG BA NI DGE BA LAS MA YIN PA'I PHYIR, RNAM SMIN MI DGE BA STE YID DU MI 'ONG BA NI, NAG PO'I LAS LAS YIN LA, RNAM SMIN DGE BA STE YID DU 'ONG BA NI DGE BA LAS 'BYUNG BA YIN PAR MTHONG ZHING,\", \"wylie\": \"rab rib can gyi mig gis skra shad sogs mthong ba dang, bong bu'i rva la sogs pa mi mthong ba'i nges pa de'i phyir rnam smin yid du 'ong ba ni mi dge ba las mi 'byung la, rnam smin yid du mi 'ong ba ni dge ba las ma yin pa'i phyir, rnam smin mi dge ba ste yid du mi 'ong ba ni, nag po'i las las yin la, rnam smin dge ba ste yid du 'ong ba ni dge ba las 'byung ba yin par mthong zhing\", \"english\": \"Eyes affected by cataracts see things like strands of hair; but it is definite that they do not see things like horns on top of the head of a rabbit. As such, it is not the case that a pleasant ripening of karma can come from a non-virtuous deed; and neither can an unpleasant ripening come from a virtuous deed. We can see then that a non-virtuous ripening—an unpleasant one—is something that comes from a black karma; while a virtuous ripening—a pleasant one—is something that comes from a good deed.\"}"
  },
  "29417": {
    "id": 29417,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2098,
    "acip": "DANG PO LA GNYIS, RMI LAM GYI DPE DGAG PA, SKRA SHAD MTHONG BA'I DPE DGAG PA'O,,",
    "wylie": "dang po la gnyis, rmi lam gyi dpe dgag pa, skra shad mthong ba'i dpe dgag pa'o",
    "english": "The first section comes in two steps of its own: denying the metaphor of a dream; and denying the metaphor of seeing a strand of hair.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DANG PO LA GNYIS, RMI LAM GYI DPE DGAG PA, SKRA SHAD MTHONG BA'I DPE DGAG PA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"dang po la gnyis, rmi lam gyi dpe dgag pa, skra shad mthong ba'i dpe dgag pa'o\", \"english\": \"The first section comes in two steps of its own: denying the metaphor of a dream; and denying the metaphor of seeing a strand of hair.\"}"
  },
  "29481": {
    "id": 29481,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2162,
    "acip": "'O NA RAB RIB CAN GYIS YOD PA MA YIN PA'I SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA DMIGS PA'I PHYIR, PHYI ROL MED KYANG RNAM PAR SHES PA RANG BZHIN GYIS YOD PA YIN NO ZHE NA",
    "wylie": "'o na rab rib can gyis yod pa ma yin pa'i skra shad la sogs pa dmigs pa'i phyir, phyi rol med kyang rnam par shes pa rang bzhin gyis yod pa yin no zhe na",
    "english": "One might come with the following: It is indeed the case that—even though there are no outer objects—awareness does exist through a nature of its own; this is due to the fact that a person who has cataracts on their eyes does perceive strands of hair and the like which do not actually exist.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"'O NA RAB RIB CAN GYIS YOD PA MA YIN PA'I SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA DMIGS PA'I PHYIR, PHYI ROL MED KYANG RNAM PAR SHES PA RANG BZHIN GYIS YOD PA YIN NO ZHE NA\", \"wylie\": \"'o na rab rib can gyis yod pa ma yin pa'i skra shad la sogs pa dmigs pa'i phyir, phyi rol med kyang rnam par shes pa rang bzhin gyis yod pa yin no zhe na\", \"english\": \"One might come with the following: It is indeed the case that—even though there are no outer objects—awareness does exist through a nature of its own; this is due to the fact that a person who has cataracts on their eyes does perceive strands of hair and the like which do not actually exist.\"}"
  },
  "29482": {
    "id": 29482,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2163,
    "acip": "DE YANG MI 'THAD DO, ,CI'I PHYIR ZHE NA, MIG GI DBANG PO RAB RIB DANG BCAS PA LA BLO GANG GIS RAB RIB KYI MTHU LAS SKRA SHAD RNAMS GANG MTHONG BA NA,",
    "wylie": "de yang mi 'thad do, ,ci'i phyir zhe na, mig gi dbang po rab rib dang bcas pa la blo gang gis rab rib kyi mthu las skra shad rnams gang mthong ba na",
    "english": "This too though is a mistaken idea. And why is that? Let's think of a sense power of the eye which has cataracts; that is, when a particular state of mind sees certain strands of hair due to the influence of cataracts.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE YANG MI 'THAD DO, ,CI'I PHYIR ZHE NA, MIG GI DBANG PO RAB RIB DANG BCAS PA LA BLO GANG GIS RAB RIB KYI MTHU LAS SKRA SHAD RNAMS GANG MTHONG BA NA,\", \"wylie\": \"de yang mi 'thad do, ,ci'i phyir zhe na, mig gi dbang po rab rib dang bcas pa la blo gang gis rab rib kyi mthu las skra shad rnams gang mthong ba na\", \"english\": \"This too though is a mistaken idea. And why is that? Let's think of a sense power of the eye which has cataracts; that is, when a particular state of mind sees certain strands of hair due to the influence of cataracts.\"}"
  },
  "29483": {
    "id": 29483,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2164,
    "acip": "GANG ZAG DE'I BLO YI MTHONG BA LA LTOS NA NI, MIG SHES DANG SKRA BSHAD {%SHAD} KYI RNAM PA STE SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA'I YUL GNYIS CHAR YANG YOD PA YIN LA, DON GSAL MTHONG BA STE RAB RIB MED PA'I MIG GIS MTHONG BA LA LTOS NA NI SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA DANG, DE SNANG BA'I SHES PA GNYIS KA'ANG BRDZUN PA STE MA SKYES PA YIN TE, SNANG BA TZAM GYI YUL YANG MED PAR SHES PA YOD PA NGES PAR DKA' BA'I PHYIR RO,,",
    "wylie": "gang zag de'i blo yi mthong ba la ltos na ni, mig shes dang skra bshad {%shad} kyi rnam pa ste skra shad du snang ba'i yul gnyis char yang yod pa yin la, don gsal mthong ba ste rab rib med pa'i mig gis mthong ba la ltos na ni skra shad du snang ba dang, de snang ba'i shes pa gnyis ka'ang brdzun pa ste ma skyes pa yin te, snang ba tsam gyi yul yang med par shes pa yod pa nges par dka' ba'i phyir ro",
    "english": "Relative to what the mind of this person sees, the visual consciousness and the aspect of the strand of hair—the object which appears as though it were a strand of hair—do both indeed exist. Relative though to the sight of someone who is seeing objects clearly—that is, to the sight of an eye which has no cataracts—both the appearance of something as a strand of hair and the state of mind which holds this appearance are false; which is to say, neither one has actually grown. That is, it would be difficult to say that one ascertained the existence of a state of mind if there were not even any object which was just appearing to be there.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"GANG ZAG DE'I BLO YI MTHONG BA LA LTOS NA NI, MIG SHES DANG SKRA BSHAD {%SHAD} KYI RNAM PA STE SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA'I YUL GNYIS CHAR YANG YOD PA YIN LA, DON GSAL MTHONG BA STE RAB RIB MED PA'I MIG GIS MTHONG BA LA LTOS NA NI SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA DANG, DE SNANG BA'I SHES PA GNYIS KA'ANG BRDZUN PA STE MA SKYES PA YIN TE, SNANG BA TZAM GYI YUL YANG MED PAR SHES PA YOD PA NGES PAR DKA' BA'I PHYIR RO,,\", \"wylie\": \"gang zag de'i blo yi mthong ba la ltos na ni, mig shes dang skra bshad {%shad} kyi rnam pa ste skra shad du snang ba'i yul gnyis char yang yod pa yin la, don gsal mthong ba ste rab rib med pa'i mig gis mthong ba la ltos na ni skra shad du snang ba dang, de snang ba'i shes pa gnyis ka'ang brdzun pa ste ma skyes pa yin te, snang ba tsam gyi yul yang med par shes pa yod pa nges par dka' ba'i phyir ro\", \"english\": \"Relative to what the mind of this person sees, the visual consciousness and the aspect of the strand of hair—the object which appears as though it were a strand of hair—do both indeed exist. Relative though to the sight of someone who is seeing objects clearly—that is, to the sight of an eye which has no cataracts—both the appearance of something as a strand of hair and the state of mind which holds this appearance are false; which is to say, neither one has actually grown. That is, it would be difficult to say that one ascertained the existence of a state of mind if there were not even any object which was just appearing to be there.\"}"
  },
  "29485": {
    "id": 29485,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2166,
    "acip": ",GAL TE SHES BYA MED PAR BLO YOD NA, ,SKRA DE'I YUL DANG MIG NI RJES 'BREL PA'I, ,RAB RIB MED LA'ANG SKRA SHAD BLOR 'GYUR NA, ,DE LTA MA YIN DE PHYIR DE YOD MIN,",
    "wylie": "gal te shes bya med par blo yod na, ,skra de'i yul dang mig ni rjes 'brel pa'i, ,rab rib med la'ang skra shad blor 'gyur na, ,de lta ma yin de phyir de yod min",
    "english": "Suppose a state of mind Were there in the absence Of an existing object; The object of that strand Would have an eye which Was connected with it; And one who had No cataracts would also Have a state of mind With a strand of hair— And for this reason It is not the case; It is not something Which could exist.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \",GAL TE SHES BYA MED PAR BLO YOD NA, ,SKRA DE'I YUL DANG MIG NI RJES 'BREL PA'I, ,RAB RIB MED LA'ANG SKRA SHAD BLOR 'GYUR NA, ,DE LTA MA YIN DE PHYIR DE YOD MIN,\", \"wylie\": \"gal te shes bya med par blo yod na, ,skra de'i yul dang mig ni rjes 'brel pa'i, ,rab rib med la'ang skra shad blor 'gyur na, ,de lta ma yin de phyir de yod min\", \"english\": \"Suppose a state of mind Were there in the absence Of an existing object; The object of that strand Would have an eye which Was connected with it; And one who had No cataracts would also Have a state of mind With a strand of hair— And for this reason It is not the case; It is not something Which could exist.\"}"
  },
  "29486": {
    "id": 29486,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2167,
    "acip": "DE LTA MA YIN TE GAL TE RAB RIB CAN LA SHES BYA SKRA SHAD MED PAR SKRA SHAD KYI RNAM PA CAN GYI RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PA'I BLO SKYE BA YOD NA",
    "wylie": "de lta ma yin te gal te rab rib can la shes bya skra shad med par skra shad kyi rnam pa can gyi rang bzhin gyis grub pa'i blo skye ba yod na",
    "english": "And suppose it were not this way; suppose a state of mind were there—that it started—with the person who had the cataracts, which existed through some nature of its own and held the appearance of a strand of hair even in the absence of any strand of hair which was an existing object.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE LTA MA YIN TE GAL TE RAB RIB CAN LA SHES BYA SKRA SHAD MED PAR SKRA SHAD KYI RNAM PA CAN GYI RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PA'I BLO SKYE BA YOD NA\", \"wylie\": \"de lta ma yin te gal te rab rib can la shes bya skra shad med par skra shad kyi rnam pa can gyi rang bzhin gyis grub pa'i blo skye ba yod na\", \"english\": \"And suppose it were not this way; suppose a state of mind were there—that it started—with the person who had the cataracts, which existed through some nature of its own and held the appearance of a strand of hair even in the absence of any strand of hair which was an existing object.\"}"
  },
  "29487": {
    "id": 29487,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2168,
    "acip": "GANG DU RAB RIB CAN GYIS SKRA SHAD DE MTHONG BA'I YUL DER MIG NI RJES SU 'BREL BA STE GTAD PA'I RAB RIB MED PA LA'ANG RAB RIB CAN DANG 'DRA BAR SKRA SHAD MTHONG BA'I BLO SKYE BAR 'GYUR TE, YUL MED PAR MTSUNGS PA'I PHYIR RO,,",
    "wylie": "gang du rab rib can gyis skra shad de mthong ba'i yul der mig ni rjes su 'brel ba ste gtad pa'i rab rib med pa la'ang rab rib can dang 'dra bar skra shad mthong ba'i blo skye bar 'gyur te, yul med par mtshungs pa'i phyir ro",
    "english": "The object which was such that the person with the cataracts saw it as a strand of hair would have to have an eye which was connected with it; that is, which was attending to it. And in that case, even a person who had no cataracts would also give rise to a state of mind where they saw a strand of hair, in the same way that the the person with the cataracts did. And this is because the object is equally a non-entity, in both cases.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"GANG DU RAB RIB CAN GYIS SKRA SHAD DE MTHONG BA'I YUL DER MIG NI RJES SU 'BREL BA STE GTAD PA'I RAB RIB MED PA LA'ANG RAB RIB CAN DANG 'DRA BAR SKRA SHAD MTHONG BA'I BLO SKYE BAR 'GYUR TE, YUL MED PAR MTSUNGS PA'I PHYIR RO,,\", \"wylie\": \"gang du rab rib can gyis skra shad de mthong ba'i yul der mig ni rjes su 'brel ba ste gtad pa'i rab rib med pa la'ang rab rib can dang 'dra bar skra shad mthong ba'i blo skye bar 'gyur te, yul med par mtshungs pa'i phyir ro\", \"english\": \"The object which was such that the person with the cataracts saw it as a strand of hair would have to have an eye which was connected with it; that is, which was attending to it. And in that case, even a person who had no cataracts would also give rise to a state of mind where they saw a strand of hair, in the same way that the the person with the cataracts did. And this is because the object is equally a non-entity, in both cases.\"}"
  },
  "29488": {
    "id": 29488,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2169,
    "acip": "'DI NI RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PA'I GZHAN LAS SKYE NA, GZHAN YIN TSAD THAMS CAD LAS SKYE BAR 'PHEN PA BZHIN DU RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PA'I SHES PA CIG SKYE NA, YUL SKRA SHAD MED PAR MTSUNGS PA LA, RAB RIB CAN LA DE MTHONG BA SKYE LA, RAB RIB MED PA LA DE MTHONG BA MI SKYE BA MI 'THAD DE, SHES PA DE RAB RIB LA MI LTOS PAR 'PHUL NUS PAS 'BREL MED DU 'GRO BA'I PHYIR RO,,",
    "wylie": "'di ni rang bzhin gyis grub pa'i gzhan las skye na, gzhan yin tshad thams cad las skye bar 'phen pa bzhin du rang bzhin gyis grub pa'i shes pa cig skye na, yul skra shad med par mtshungs pa la, rab rib can la de mthong ba skye la, rab rib med pa la de mthong ba mi skye ba mi 'thad de, shes pa de rab rib la mi ltos par 'phul nus pas 'brel med du 'gro ba'i phyir ro",
    "english": "If something could grow from something other than it which existed through some nature of its own, then that implies that it could grow as well from each & every other object in the universe which was other than it. Just so, if it were possible for one state of mind to grow which existed through some nature of its own, then it would be incorrect to say that—in a case where the strand of hair were equally absent—the sight of it would grow in a person who had cataracts but the sight of it would not grow in a person who had no cataracts. That is, since there would be the potential for this state of mind to make itself known without any dependence upon cataracts, everything would happen without any connection to anything.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"'DI NI RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PA'I GZHAN LAS SKYE NA, GZHAN YIN TSAD THAMS CAD LAS SKYE BAR 'PHEN PA BZHIN DU RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PA'I SHES PA CIG SKYE NA, YUL SKRA SHAD MED PAR MTSUNGS PA LA, RAB RIB CAN LA DE MTHONG BA SKYE LA, RAB RIB MED PA LA DE MTHONG BA MI SKYE BA MI 'THAD DE, SHES PA DE RAB RIB LA MI LTOS PAR 'PHUL NUS PAS 'BREL MED DU 'GRO BA'I PHYIR RO,,\", \"wylie\": \"'di ni rang bzhin gyis grub pa'i gzhan las skye na, gzhan yin tshad thams cad las skye bar 'phen pa bzhin du rang bzhin gyis grub pa'i shes pa cig skye na, yul skra shad med par mtshungs pa la, rab rib can la de mthong ba skye la, rab rib med pa la de mthong ba mi skye ba mi 'thad de, shes pa de rab rib la mi ltos par 'phul nus pas 'brel med du 'gro ba'i phyir ro\", \"english\": \"If something could grow from something other than it which existed through some nature of its own, then that implies that it could grow as well from each & every other object in the universe which was other than it. Just so, if it were possible for one state of mind to grow which existed through some nature of its own, then it would be incorrect to say that—in a case where the strand of hair were equally absent—the sight of it would grow in a person who had cataracts but the sight of it would not grow in a person who had no cataracts. That is, since there would be the potential for this state of mind to make itself known without any dependence upon cataracts, everything would happen without any connection to anything.\"}"
  },
  "29492": {
    "id": 29492,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2173,
    "acip": "GAL TE SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA'I SKRA SHAD LTA BU'I YUL GYI YOD PA NYID RNAM SHES SKYE BA'I RGYU YIN NA, RAB RIB MED PA LA YANG SKRA SHAD SNANG BAR 'GYUR BA ZHIG NA DE NI MIN NO,,",
    "wylie": "gal te skra shad du snang ba'i skra shad lta bu'i yul gyi yod pa nyid rnam shes skye ba'i rgyu yin na, rab rib med pa la yang skra shad snang bar 'gyur ba zhig na de ni min no",
    "english": "Suppose one were to say that—if the very existence of an object similar to a strand of hair that appeared as a strand of hair were the cause of an awareness growing—then the strand of hair would appear as well even to a person with no cataracts. Our answer then would be “That's not the case.”",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"GAL TE SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA'I SKRA SHAD LTA BU'I YUL GYI YOD PA NYID RNAM SHES SKYE BA'I RGYU YIN NA, RAB RIB MED PA LA YANG SKRA SHAD SNANG BAR 'GYUR BA ZHIG NA DE NI MIN NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"gal te skra shad du snang ba'i skra shad lta bu'i yul gyi yod pa nyid rnam shes skye ba'i rgyu yin na, rab rib med pa la yang skra shad snang bar 'gyur ba zhig na de ni min no\", \"english\": \"Suppose one were to say that—if the very existence of an object similar to a strand of hair that appeared as a strand of hair were the cause of an awareness growing—then the strand of hair would appear as well even to a person with no cataracts. Our answer then would be “That's not the case.”\"}"
  },
  "29496": {
    "id": 29496,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2177,
    "acip": "DE'I PHYIR GANG ZAG GANG LA SKRA'I RNAM PA CAN GYI SHES PA SNGA MA GZHAN GYIS BZHAG PA'I BAG CHAGS SMIN PA YOD PA DE KHO NA LA, SKRA SHAD KYI RNAM PA CAN GYI SHES PA 'BYUNG GI",
    "wylie": "de'i phyir gang zag gang la skra'i rnam pa can gyi shes pa snga ma gzhan gyis bzhag pa'i bag chags smin pa yod pa de kho na la, skra shad kyi rnam pa can gyi shes pa 'byung gi",
    "english": "As such, it is only a person who is experiencing the ripening of a deep seed that was planted by another, previous state of mind that possessed an image of a hair that will have an awareness arise with the image of a strand of hair.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE'I PHYIR GANG ZAG GANG LA SKRA'I RNAM PA CAN GYI SHES PA SNGA MA GZHAN GYIS BZHAG PA'I BAG CHAGS SMIN PA YOD PA DE KHO NA LA, SKRA SHAD KYI RNAM PA CAN GYI SHES PA 'BYUNG GI\", \"wylie\": \"de'i phyir gang zag gang la skra'i rnam pa can gyi shes pa snga ma gzhan gyis bzhag pa'i bag chags smin pa yod pa de kho na la, skra shad kyi rnam pa can gyi shes pa 'byung gi\", \"english\": \"As such, it is only a person who is experiencing the ripening of a deep seed that was planted by another, previous state of mind that possessed an image of a hair that will have an awareness arise with the image of a strand of hair.\"}"
  },
  "29497": {
    "id": 29497,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2178,
    "acip": ",GANG GI PHYIR RAB RIB MED PAR YUL MTHONG BA DAG LA SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA'I BLO YI NUS PA BAG CHAGS NI SMIN PA MED PA DE'I PHYIR RAB RIB MED PA DE LA SKRA SHAD MTHONG BA'I BLO SKYE BAR MI 'GYUR BA YIN GYI, SHES BYA SKRA SHAD DU YOD PA'I DNGOS PO DANG BRAL BAS BLO DE MI SKYE BA MIN NO ZHE NA,",
    "wylie": "gang gi phyir rab rib med par yul mthong ba dag la skra shad du snang ba'i blo yi nus pa bag chags ni smin pa med pa de'i phyir rab rib med pa de la skra shad mthong ba'i blo skye bar mi 'gyur ba yin gyi, shes bya skra shad du yod pa'i dngos po dang bral bas blo de mi skye ba min no zhe na",
    "english": "It is not however the case that there would be any ripening of a deep seed—the potential for a state of mind that took on the aspect of a strand of hair—with those who saw the object but had no cataracts. And for this reason then, it is not the case that a state of mind where they saw a strand of hair could ever come to grow with these individuals who had no cataracts. It is not though the case that this state of mind would fail to grow because it was devoid of any entity which possessed an existing object which was a strand of hair. Suppose then that this were your position. The present has no potential on its own",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \",GANG GI PHYIR RAB RIB MED PAR YUL MTHONG BA DAG LA SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA'I BLO YI NUS PA BAG CHAGS NI SMIN PA MED PA DE'I PHYIR RAB RIB MED PA DE LA SKRA SHAD MTHONG BA'I BLO SKYE BAR MI 'GYUR BA YIN GYI, SHES BYA SKRA SHAD DU YOD PA'I DNGOS PO DANG BRAL BAS BLO DE MI SKYE BA MIN NO ZHE NA,\", \"wylie\": \"gang gi phyir rab rib med par yul mthong ba dag la skra shad du snang ba'i blo yi nus pa bag chags ni smin pa med pa de'i phyir rab rib med pa de la skra shad mthong ba'i blo skye bar mi 'gyur ba yin gyi, shes bya skra shad du yod pa'i dngos po dang bral bas blo de mi skye ba min no zhe na\", \"english\": \"It is not however the case that there would be any ripening of a deep seed—the potential for a state of mind that took on the aspect of a strand of hair—with those who saw the object but had no cataracts. And for this reason then, it is not the case that a state of mind where they saw a strand of hair could ever come to grow with these individuals who had no cataracts. It is not though the case that this state of mind would fail to grow because it was devoid of any entity which possessed an existing object which was a strand of hair. Suppose then that this were your position. The present has no potential on its own\"}"
  },
  "29646": {
    "id": 29646,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2327,
    "acip": "MIG SHES LA SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA NI GZUGS BRNYAN BZHIN DU GZUGS KYI SKYE MCHED DO,,",
    "wylie": "mig shes la skra shad du snang ba ni gzugs brnyan bzhin du gzugs kyi skye mched do",
    "english": "When a strand of hair makes its appearance to the consciousness of the eye it belongs, like a reflection in a mirror, to the sense door of visible form.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"MIG SHES LA SKRA SHAD DU SNANG BA NI GZUGS BRNYAN BZHIN DU GZUGS KYI SKYE MCHED DO,,\", \"wylie\": \"mig shes la skra shad du snang ba ni gzugs brnyan bzhin du gzugs kyi skye mched do\", \"english\": \"When a strand of hair makes its appearance to the consciousness of the eye it belongs, like a reflection in a mirror, to the sense door of visible form.\"}"
  },
  "29667": {
    "id": 29667,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2348,
    "acip": "SHES BYA DANG SHES PA LA RANG BZHIN GYIS YOD MED KYI KHYAD PAR MED PAR STON PA YIN LA, SKRA SHAD MED NA DER SNANG BA'I SHES PA YANG MED CES PA DANG, SGYU MA LA RTA GLANG DU ZHEN PA'I ZHEN YUL MED NA DER 'DZIN PA'I SHES PA YANG MED CES SOGS NI, SLOB DPON 'DI'I LUGS GTAN MIN NO,,",
    "wylie": "shes bya dang shes pa la rang bzhin gyis yod med kyi khyad par med par ston pa yin la, skra shad med na der snang ba'i shes pa yang med ces pa dang, sgyu ma la rta glang du zhen pa'i zhen yul med na der 'dzin pa'i shes pa yang med ces sogs ni, slob dpon 'di'i lugs gtan min no",
    "english": "What this is indicating is that there is no difference between knowable things and the awareness which knows them, as far as existing through some nature of their own, or not. And what this is saying is that—if there is no strand of hair—then neither is there any state of mind which is taking that strand as its aspect. It is absolutely not the position of this master though to say that—if the object that one was thinking was there when we perceived a horse or cow in an illusion didn't really exist—then the state of mind that was grasping to it didn't exist either.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"SHES BYA DANG SHES PA LA RANG BZHIN GYIS YOD MED KYI KHYAD PAR MED PAR STON PA YIN LA, SKRA SHAD MED NA DER SNANG BA'I SHES PA YANG MED CES PA DANG, SGYU MA LA RTA GLANG DU ZHEN PA'I ZHEN YUL MED NA DER 'DZIN PA'I SHES PA YANG MED CES SOGS NI, SLOB DPON 'DI'I LUGS GTAN MIN NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"shes bya dang shes pa la rang bzhin gyis yod med kyi khyad par med par ston pa yin la, skra shad med na der snang ba'i shes pa yang med ces pa dang, sgyu ma la rta glang du zhen pa'i zhen yul med na der 'dzin pa'i shes pa yang med ces sogs ni, slob dpon 'di'i lugs gtan min no\", \"english\": \"What this is indicating is that there is no difference between knowable things and the awareness which knows them, as far as existing through some nature of their own, or not. And what this is saying is that—if there is no strand of hair—then neither is there any state of mind which is taking that strand as its aspect. It is absolutely not the position of this master though to say that—if the object that one was thinking was there when we perceived a horse or cow in an illusion didn't really exist—then the state of mind that was grasping to it didn't exist either.\"}"
  },
  "29989": {
    "id": 29989,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2670,
    "acip": "DE'I PHYIR RNAM PA THAMS CAD DU RNAM PAR SHES PA NI RANG BZHIN GYIS YOD PA MA YIN PA KHO NA STE, RAB RIB CAN GYIS DMIGS PA'I SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA LTAR, PHYIN CI LOG GI RKYEN YOD NA YOD PA'I PHYIR LA, DE KHO NA LTAR PHYIN CI LOG GI RKYEN MED NA MED PA'I PHYIR RO SNYAM DU BSAMS SO,,",
    "wylie": "de'i phyir rnam pa thams cad du rnam par shes pa ni rang bzhin gyis yod pa ma yin pa kho na ste, rab rib can gyis dmigs pa'i skra shad la sogs pa ltar, phyin ci log gi rkyen yod na yod pa'i phyir la, de kho na ltar phyin ci log gi rkyen med na med pa'i phyir ro snyam du bsams so",
    "english": "And thus we can say that consciousness could never be something—no matter which way you look at it—that could exist through some nature of its own. Such a thing would be similar to the strand of hair that those who have cataracts think they see—something like that. If you say that something exists only because it has an erroneous factor, then for the very same reason we'd have to say that if something lacked an erroneous factor, then it couldn't exist at all. At least, that's the way it seems to me.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE'I PHYIR RNAM PA THAMS CAD DU RNAM PAR SHES PA NI RANG BZHIN GYIS YOD PA MA YIN PA KHO NA STE, RAB RIB CAN GYIS DMIGS PA'I SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA LTAR, PHYIN CI LOG GI RKYEN YOD NA YOD PA'I PHYIR LA, DE KHO NA LTAR PHYIN CI LOG GI RKYEN MED NA MED PA'I PHYIR RO SNYAM DU BSAMS SO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de'i phyir rnam pa thams cad du rnam par shes pa ni rang bzhin gyis yod pa ma yin pa kho na ste, rab rib can gyis dmigs pa'i skra shad la sogs pa ltar, phyin ci log gi rkyen yod na yod pa'i phyir la, de kho na ltar phyin ci log gi rkyen med na med pa'i phyir ro snyam du bsams so\", \"english\": \"And thus we can say that consciousness could never be something—no matter which way you look at it—that could exist through some nature of its own. Such a thing would be similar to the strand of hair that those who have cataracts think they see—something like that. If you say that something exists only because it has an erroneous factor, then for the very same reason we'd have to say that if something lacked an erroneous factor, then it couldn't exist at all. At least, that's the way it seems to me.\"}"
  },
  "30235": {
    "id": 30235,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2916,
    "acip": ",JI LTAR RAB RIB MTHU YIS 'GA' ZHIG SKRA SHAD ZLA GNYIS DANG, ,RMA BYA'I MDONGS DANG SBRANG MA LA SOGS LOG PAR 'DZIN BYED PA, ,DE BZHIN DU NI GTI [209b] MUG SKYON GYI DBANG GIS MI MKHAS PAS, ,'DUS BYAS LTA ZHIG SNA TSOGS BLO GROS KYIS NI RTOGS PAR 'GYUR,",
    "wylie": "ji ltar rab rib mthu yis 'ga' zhig skra shad zla gnyis dang, ,rma bya'i mdongs dang sbrang ma la sogs log par 'dzin byed pa, ,de bzhin du ni gti [209b] mug skyon gyi dbang gis mi mkhas pas, ,'dus byas lta zhig sna tshogs blo gros kyis ni rtogs par 'gyur",
    "english": "It's possible For some people, By force of a cataract, To be made to hold, Mistakenly, A strand of hair, One moon as two, The pattern Of a peacock's feathers, A random fly, or Something of the like [f. 209b]. The same sort of thing Happens with those Who are not wise, Due to the problem Of their dark ignorance. They perceive Various objects Like produced things With their intellect.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \",JI LTAR RAB RIB MTHU YIS 'GA' ZHIG SKRA SHAD ZLA GNYIS DANG, ,RMA BYA'I MDONGS DANG SBRANG MA LA SOGS LOG PAR 'DZIN BYED PA, ,DE BZHIN DU NI GTI [209b] MUG SKYON GYI DBANG GIS MI MKHAS PAS, ,'DUS BYAS LTA ZHIG SNA TSOGS BLO GROS KYIS NI RTOGS PAR 'GYUR,\", \"wylie\": \"ji ltar rab rib mthu yis 'ga' zhig skra shad zla gnyis dang, ,rma bya'i mdongs dang sbrang ma la sogs log par 'dzin byed pa, ,de bzhin du ni gti [209b] mug skyon gyi dbang gis mi mkhas pas, ,'dus byas lta zhig sna tshogs blo gros kyis ni rtogs par 'gyur\", \"english\": \"It's possible For some people, By force of a cataract, To be made to hold, Mistakenly, A strand of hair, One moon as two, The pattern Of a peacock's feathers, A random fly, or Something of the like [f. 209b]. The same sort of thing Happens with those Who are not wise, Due to the problem Of their dark ignorance. They perceive Various objects Like produced things With their intellect.\"}"
  },
  "30236": {
    "id": 30236,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2917,
    "acip": "JI LTAR DBANG PO RAB RIB KYIS BSLAD PA'I MTHU YIS RAB RIB CAN 'GA' ZHIG GIS SKRA SHAD DANG ZLA BA GNYIS DANG RMA BYA'I MDONGS DANG SBRANG MA LA SOGS PA LOG PAR TE MED KYANG YOD PA LTAR 'DZIN PA STE MTHONG BAR BYED PA",
    "wylie": "ji ltar dbang po rab rib kyis bslad pa'i mthu yis rab rib can 'ga' zhig gis skra shad dang zla ba gnyis dang rma bya'i mdongs dang sbrang ma la sogs pa log par te med kyang yod pa ltar 'dzin pa ste mthong bar byed pa",
    "english": "It's possible for ones sense powers to be skewed by force of the effects of a cataract; so that some people with cataracts may mistakenly see a strand of hair that's not actually there; or one moon that appears as two; or a spot of color like the pattern on a peacock's feathers; a random fly; or something of the like. When we say “mistakenly” here, what we mean is that—even though none of these things is actually there—our condition makes us hold to or see them as existing.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"JI LTAR DBANG PO RAB RIB KYIS BSLAD PA'I MTHU YIS RAB RIB CAN 'GA' ZHIG GIS SKRA SHAD DANG ZLA BA GNYIS DANG RMA BYA'I MDONGS DANG SBRANG MA LA SOGS PA LOG PAR TE MED KYANG YOD PA LTAR 'DZIN PA STE MTHONG BAR BYED PA\", \"wylie\": \"ji ltar dbang po rab rib kyis bslad pa'i mthu yis rab rib can 'ga' zhig gis skra shad dang zla ba gnyis dang rma bya'i mdongs dang sbrang ma la sogs pa log par te med kyang yod pa ltar 'dzin pa ste mthong bar byed pa\", \"english\": \"It's possible for ones sense powers to be skewed by force of the effects of a cataract; so that some people with cataracts may mistakenly see a strand of hair that's not actually there; or one moon that appears as two; or a spot of color like the pattern on a peacock's feathers; a random fly; or something of the like. When we say “mistakenly” here, what we mean is that—even though none of these things is actually there—our condition makes us hold to or see them as existing.\"}"
  },
  "30250": {
    "id": 30250,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2931,
    "acip": ",GANG DAG RAB RIB CAN SOGS YUL 'GYUR BA, ,SKRA SHAD LA SOGS DE DAG MA SKYES PAS, ,RE ZHIG DE DAG NYID LA BRTZAD BYA STE, ,PHYI NAS MA RIG RAB RIB RJES 'BREL LA'O,",
    "wylie": "gang dag rab rib can sogs yul 'gyur ba, ,skra shad la sogs de dag ma skyes pas, ,re zhig de dag nyid la brtsad bya ste, ,phyi nas ma rig rab rib rjes 'brel la'o",
    "english": "Strands of hair Or the like act As objects For a person Who possesses Cataracts or such; And these Have never started. So let's assume first That we seek out The way that Things really are. After that, There's the connection To the cataracts Of misunderstanding.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \",GANG DAG RAB RIB CAN SOGS YUL 'GYUR BA, ,SKRA SHAD LA SOGS DE DAG MA SKYES PAS, ,RE ZHIG DE DAG NYID LA BRTZAD BYA STE, ,PHYI NAS MA RIG RAB RIB RJES 'BREL LA'O,\", \"wylie\": \"gang dag rab rib can sogs yul 'gyur ba, ,skra shad la sogs de dag ma skyes pas, ,re zhig de dag nyid la brtsad bya ste, ,phyi nas ma rig rab rib rjes 'brel la'o\", \"english\": \"Strands of hair Or the like act As objects For a person Who possesses Cataracts or such; And these Have never started. So let's assume first That we seek out The way that Things really are. After that, There's the connection To the cataracts Of misunderstanding.\"}"
  },
  "30251": {
    "id": 30251,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2932,
    "acip": "'DI LA BRJOD PAR BYA STE, GANG DAG RAB RIB CAN LA SOGS PA'I BLO'I YUL DU 'GYUR BA'I SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA DE DAG MA SKYES PAS NA, MA SKYES PAR MO GSHAM GYI BU DANG MTSUNGS PA LA,",
    "wylie": "'di la brjod par bya ste, gang dag rab rib can la sogs pa'i blo'i yul du 'gyur ba'i skra shad la sogs pa de dag ma skyes pas na, ma skyes par mo gsham gyi bu dang mtshungs pa la",
    "english": "To this thought we reply as follows. The mind of a person who possesses cataracts or such sees strands of hair or the like that act as its objects. And if these objects have never started, then in their not starting they are totally equivalent to the child of a barren woman.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"'DI LA BRJOD PAR BYA STE, GANG DAG RAB RIB CAN LA SOGS PA'I BLO'I YUL DU 'GYUR BA'I SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA DE DAG MA SKYES PAS NA, MA SKYES PAR MO GSHAM GYI BU DANG MTSUNGS PA LA,\", \"wylie\": \"'di la brjod par bya ste, gang dag rab rib can la sogs pa'i blo'i yul du 'gyur ba'i skra shad la sogs pa de dag ma skyes pas na, ma skyes par mo gsham gyi bu dang mtshungs pa la\", \"english\": \"To this thought we reply as follows. The mind of a person who possesses cataracts or such sees strands of hair or the like that act as its objects. And if these objects have never started, then in their not starting they are totally equivalent to the child of a barren woman.\"}"
  },
  "30252": {
    "id": 30252,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2933,
    "acip": "CI'I PHYIR KHYED CAG GIS YOD PA MIN PA'I YUL SKRA SHAD SOGS MTHONG LA, MO GSHAM GYI BU MI MTHONG BA CI YIN ZHES,",
    "wylie": "ci'i phyir khyed cag gis yod pa min pa'i yul skra shad sogs mthong la, mo gsham gyi bu mi mthong ba ci yin zhes",
    "english": "And then we would have to ask you a question: Why is it that you would see things like that strand of hair that didn't exist; but not see this child of a barren woman?",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"CI'I PHYIR KHYED CAG GIS YOD PA MIN PA'I YUL SKRA SHAD SOGS MTHONG LA, MO GSHAM GYI BU MI MTHONG BA CI YIN ZHES,\", \"wylie\": \"ci'i phyir khyed cag gis yod pa min pa'i yul skra shad sogs mthong la, mo gsham gyi bu mi mthong ba ci yin zhes\", \"english\": \"And then we would have to ask you a question: Why is it that you would see things like that strand of hair that didn't exist; but not see this child of a barren woman?\"}"
  },
  "30341": {
    "id": 30341,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 3022,
    "acip": "DPER NA MIG SMAN BSKUS PAS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PAR SNANG BA LOG PA NYID RAB RIB CAN LA 'BRAS BUR 'GYUR GYI, SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA RANG BZHIN GZHAN ZHIG TU SONG BA'I SGO NAS DE'I YUL DU 'GYUR BA MIN PA BZHIN NO,,",
    "wylie": "dper na mig sman bskus pas skra shad la sogs par snang ba log pa nyid rab rib can la 'bras bur 'gyur gyi, skra shad la sogs pa rang bzhin gzhan zhig tu song ba'i sgo nas de'i yul du 'gyur ba min pa bzhin no",
    "english": "It's like, for example, when we apply some medicinal drops to our eyes, and this puts a complete stop to the appearance of any strand of hair or the like; this is the kind of result that comes to the person who was afflicted with the cataracts. It is not the case though that some strand of hair or the like has transformed into having some other sort of nature, and then can no longer serve as the object of our perceptions. Fruits from the examination",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DPER NA MIG SMAN BSKUS PAS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PAR SNANG BA LOG PA NYID RAB RIB CAN LA 'BRAS BUR 'GYUR GYI, SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA RANG BZHIN GZHAN ZHIG TU SONG BA'I SGO NAS DE'I YUL DU 'GYUR BA MIN PA BZHIN NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"dper na mig sman bskus pas skra shad la sogs par snang ba log pa nyid rab rib can la 'bras bur 'gyur gyi, skra shad la sogs pa rang bzhin gzhan zhig tu song ba'i sgo nas de'i yul du 'gyur ba min pa bzhin no\", \"english\": \"It's like, for example, when we apply some medicinal drops to our eyes, and this puts a complete stop to the appearance of any strand of hair or the like; this is the kind of result that comes to the person who was afflicted with the cataracts. It is not the case though that some strand of hair or the like has transformed into having some other sort of nature, and then can no longer serve as the object of our perceptions. Fruits from the examination\"}"
  },
  "31012": {
    "id": 31012,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 3693,
    "acip": "DPER NA RAB RIB CAN GYIS DMIGS PA'I SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA RNAMS KYI GCIG NYID DANG, MANG PO NYID DANG, ZLUM PO NYID DANG, NAG PO LA SOGS PA'I PHYOGS SUN 'BYIN PAS,",
    "wylie": "dper na rab rib can gyis dmigs pa'i skra shad la sogs pa rnams kyi gcig nyid dang, mang po nyid dang, zlum po nyid dang, nag po la sogs pa'i phyogs sun 'byin pas",
    "english": "Suppose, for example, that we worked very hard to attack the idea—held by individuals who have cataracts on their eyes—that the strands of hair that they were seeing were either just one alone, or some great number, or somehow rounded, or black, or anything else of the like.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DPER NA RAB RIB CAN GYIS DMIGS PA'I SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA RNAMS KYI GCIG NYID DANG, MANG PO NYID DANG, ZLUM PO NYID DANG, NAG PO LA SOGS PA'I PHYOGS SUN 'BYIN PAS,\", \"wylie\": \"dper na rab rib can gyis dmigs pa'i skra shad la sogs pa rnams kyi gcig nyid dang, mang po nyid dang, zlum po nyid dang, nag po la sogs pa'i phyogs sun 'byin pas\", \"english\": \"Suppose, for example, that we worked very hard to attack the idea—held by individuals who have cataracts on their eyes—that the strands of hair that they were seeing were either just one alone, or some great number, or somehow rounded, or black, or anything else of the like.\"}"
  },
  "32049": {
    "id": 32049,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 4730,
    "acip": "b11) SKRA SHAD MTHONG BA'I DPE DGAG PA, [vv. VI.54, VI.55]",
    "wylie": "b11) skra shad mthong ba'i dpe dgag pa, [vv. vi.54, vi.55]",
    "english": "Denying the metaphor of seeing a strand of hair * * *",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"b11) SKRA SHAD MTHONG BA'I DPE DGAG PA, [vv. VI.54, VI.55]\", \"wylie\": \"b11) skra shad mthong ba'i dpe dgag pa, [vv. vi.54, vi.55]\", \"english\": \"Denying the metaphor of seeing a strand of hair * * *\"}"
  },
  "36841": {
    "id": 36841,
    "course": "SVN",
    "seq": 381,
    "acip": "RAB RIB CAN LA SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR RTOG PA'I GANG ZAG GI MIG LA SKRA SHAD SNANG GA LA DGOS, KUN RDZOB BDEN PAR RTOGS PA'I PHYIR RDZUN PA SGYU MA LTA BUR RTOGS PA YIN GYI, BDEN PAR KHAS BLANGS MI DGOS SO,,",
    "wylie": "rab rib can la skra shad 'dzag par rtog pa'i gang zag gi mig la skra shad snang ga la dgos, kun rdzob bden par rtogs pa'i phyir rdzun pa sgyu ma lta bur rtogs pa yin gyi, bden par khas blangs mi dgos so",
    "english": "Consider then a person who has a scratch on the surface of their eye, and who thus thinks they see a hair lying on the surface of their kitchen sink. How could you say then that a hair was appearing to their eyes? A person can perceive that things are deceptive-that they are like an illusion-because they have understood what deceptive reality is; but that doesn't mean they have to take it as real.",
    "raw": "{\"acip\": \"RAB RIB CAN LA SKRA SHAD 'DZAG PAR RTOG PA'I GANG ZAG GI MIG LA SKRA SHAD SNANG GA LA DGOS, KUN RDZOB BDEN PAR RTOGS PA'I PHYIR RDZUN PA SGYU MA LTA BUR RTOGS PA YIN GYI, BDEN PAR KHAS BLANGS MI DGOS SO,,\", \"wylie\": \"rab rib can la skra shad 'dzag par rtog pa'i gang zag gi mig la skra shad snang ga la dgos, kun rdzob bden par rtogs pa'i phyir rdzun pa sgyu ma lta bur rtogs pa yin gyi, bden par khas blangs mi dgos so\", \"english\": \"Consider then a person who has a scratch on the surface of their eye, and who thus thinks they see a hair lying on the surface of their kitchen sink. How could you say then that a hair was appearing to their eyes? A person can perceive that things are deceptive-that they are like an illusion-because they have understood what deceptive reality is; but that doesn't mean they have to take it as real.\", \"course\": \"SVN\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
  },
  "36852": {
    "id": 36852,
    "course": "SVN",
    "seq": 392,
    "acip": "DE NI RANG BZHIN MED PA RTOGS NA MIG DAG PA LA SKRA SHAD BZHIN DU SNANG BA MTHA' DAG NUB CING MA MTHONG BA'I SGO NAS RTOGS PA DANG,",
    "wylie": "de ni rang bzhin med pa rtogs na mig dag pa la skra shad bzhin du snang ba mtha' dag nub cing ma mthong ba'i sgo nas rtogs pa dang",
    "english": "What this line is actually saying though is that-when we realize that nothing has any nature of its own-it's like that hair on the sink for someone whose eyes are perfectly fine. That is, the appearance of the hair has faded away into nothing; we don't see the hair at all. And it's the same process here, for that realization.",
    "raw": "{\"acip\": \"DE NI RANG BZHIN MED PA RTOGS NA MIG DAG PA LA SKRA SHAD BZHIN DU SNANG BA MTHA' DAG NUB CING MA MTHONG BA'I SGO NAS RTOGS PA DANG,\", \"wylie\": \"de ni rang bzhin med pa rtogs na mig dag pa la skra shad bzhin du snang ba mtha' dag nub cing ma mthong ba'i sgo nas rtogs pa dang\", \"english\": \"What this line is actually saying though is that-when we realize that nothing has any nature of its own-it's like that hair on the sink for someone whose eyes are perfectly fine. That is, the appearance of the hair has faded away into nothing; we don't see the hair at all. And it's the same process here, for that realization.\", \"course\": \"SVN\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
  },
  "37023": {
    "id": 37023,
    "course": "SVN",
    "seq": 563,
    "acip": "RAB RIB MED NA SKRA SHAD LDOG PAR MTHONG BA'I PHYIR RO,,",
    "wylie": "rab rib med na skra shad ldog par mthong ba'i phyir ro",
    "english": "After all, we can see that-when a cataract is removed from our eyes-we stop seeing hairs sticking to our white porcelain sink. No starting, no ending, no staying",
    "raw": "{\"acip\": \"RAB RIB MED NA SKRA SHAD LDOG PAR MTHONG BA'I PHYIR RO,,\", \"wylie\": \"rab rib med na skra shad ldog par mthong ba'i phyir ro\", \"english\": \"After all, we can see that-when a cataract is removed from our eyes-we stop seeing hairs sticking to our white porcelain sink. No starting, no ending, no staying\", \"course\": \"SVN\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
  },
  "37616": {
    "id": 37616,
    "course": "SVN",
    "seq": 1156,
    "acip": "PHYIN CI LOG DANG PHYIN CI MA LOG PA DAG NI THA DAD PA YIN NO, ZHES PA NAS, [DE'I PHYIR, GANG GI TSE RAB RIB CAN GYIS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA LTAR PHYIN CI LOG GIS YOD PA MA YIN PA YOD PA NYID DU 'DZIN PA DE'I TSE NI YOD PAR GYUR PA'I DON CHA TZAM YANG DMIGS PAR GA LA 'GYUR,",
    "wylie": "phyin ci log dang phyin ci ma log pa dag ni tha dad pa yin no, zhes pa nas, [de'i phyir, gang gi tshe rab rib can gyis skra shad la sogs pa ltar phyin ci log gis yod pa ma yin pa yod pa nyid du 'dzin pa de'i tshe ni yod par gyur pa'i don cha tsam yang dmigs par ga la 'gyur",
    "english": "After all, a state of mind which is entirely mistaken is completely separate from one which is not mistaken in any way. We can for example look at a case where a person with cataracts on their eyes believes that there is a strand of hair lying on a white porcelain sink; they are certain that there is something there, when in fact there is nothing-they're just seeing a line on the eyeball itself. We could never say, in this instance, that there was present there even a tiny percentage of any actual object-any actual hair there.",
    "raw": "{\"acip\": \"PHYIN CI LOG DANG PHYIN CI MA LOG PA DAG NI THA DAD PA YIN NO, ZHES PA NAS, [DE'I PHYIR, GANG GI TSE RAB RIB CAN GYIS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA LTAR PHYIN CI LOG GIS YOD PA MA YIN PA YOD PA NYID DU 'DZIN PA DE'I TSE NI YOD PAR GYUR PA'I DON CHA TZAM YANG DMIGS PAR GA LA 'GYUR,\", \"wylie\": \"phyin ci log dang phyin ci ma log pa dag ni tha dad pa yin no, zhes pa nas, [de'i phyir, gang gi tshe rab rib can gyis skra shad la sogs pa ltar phyin ci log gis yod pa ma yin pa yod pa nyid du 'dzin pa de'i tshe ni yod par gyur pa'i don cha tsam yang dmigs par ga la 'gyur\", \"english\": \"After all, a state of mind which is entirely mistaken is completely separate from one which is not mistaken in any way. We can for example look at a case where a person with cataracts on their eyes believes that there is a strand of hair lying on a white porcelain sink; they are certain that there is something there, when in fact there is nothing-they're just seeing a line on the eyeball itself. We could never say, in this instance, that there was present there even a tiny percentage of any actual object-any actual hair there.\", \"course\": \"SVN\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
  },
  "37617": {
    "id": 37617,
    "course": "SVN",
    "seq": 1157,
    "acip": "GANG GI TSE RAB RIB CAN MA YIN PAS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA LTAR PHYIN CI MA LOG PAS YANG DAG PA MA YIN PA SGRO MI 'DOGS PA DE'I TSE NA YANG GANG GIS NA DE'I TSE NA KUN RDZOB TU 'GYUR BA YOD PA MA YIN PAR GYUR PA'I DON CHA TZAM YANG DMIGS PA GA LA YOD,",
    "wylie": "gang gi tshe rab rib can ma yin pas skra shad la sogs pa ltar phyin ci ma log pas yang dag pa ma yin pa sgro mi 'dogs pa de'i tshe na yang gang gis na de'i tshe na kun rdzob tu 'gyur ba yod pa ma yin par gyur pa'i don cha tsam yang dmigs pa ga la yod",
    "english": "And suppose another person comes along who has no cataracts on their eyes. They have none of this tendency for seeing a false object; one which simply isn't there-the hair or whatever it may be. And this is because their state of mind is one which is not mistaken. We could never say, again, that there was present there even a tiny percentage of any object which did not exist in a deceptive manner.",
    "raw": "{\"acip\": \"GANG GI TSE RAB RIB CAN MA YIN PAS SKRA SHAD LA SOGS PA LTAR PHYIN CI MA LOG PAS YANG DAG PA MA YIN PA SGRO MI 'DOGS PA DE'I TSE NA YANG GANG GIS NA DE'I TSE NA KUN RDZOB TU 'GYUR BA YOD PA MA YIN PAR GYUR PA'I DON CHA TZAM YANG DMIGS PA GA LA YOD,\", \"wylie\": \"gang gi tshe rab rib can ma yin pas skra shad la sogs pa ltar phyin ci ma log pas yang dag pa ma yin pa sgro mi 'dogs pa de'i tshe na yang gang gis na de'i tshe na kun rdzob tu 'gyur ba yod pa ma yin par gyur pa'i don cha tsam yang dmigs pa ga la yod\", \"english\": \"And suppose another person comes along who has no cataracts on their eyes. They have none of this tendency for seeing a false object; one which simply isn't there-the hair or whatever it may be. And this is because their state of mind is one which is not mistaken. We could never say, again, that there was present there even a tiny percentage of any object which did not exist in a deceptive manner.\", \"course\": \"SVN\", \"source_wave\": \"gmr-volumes-2026-08\"}"
  },
  "18562": {
    "id": 18562,
    "course": "TCS15",
    "seq": 419,
    "acip": "SNYING GA'I THAD DU 'BAR 'DZAG SKRA SHAD 'DZINGS PA LTA BU LA SEMS GTOD NAS BUM CAN LA NAN TAN BYA,",
    "wylie": "snying ga'i thad du 'bar 'dzag skra shad 'dzings pa lta bu la sems gtod nas bum can la nan tan bya",
    "english": "Focus the mind onto this dripping and blazing tug-of-war at the heart area (which looks like a battle between single strands of hair), and make a concerted effort to hold the vase.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS15\", \"acip\": \"SNYING GA'I THAD DU 'BAR 'DZAG SKRA SHAD 'DZINGS PA LTA BU LA SEMS GTOD NAS BUM CAN LA NAN TAN BYA,\", \"wylie\": \"snying ga'i thad du 'bar 'dzag skra shad 'dzings pa lta bu la sems gtod nas bum can la nan tan bya\", \"english\": \"Focus the mind onto this dripping and blazing tug-of-war at the heart area (which looks like a battle between single strands of hair), and make a concerted effort to hold the vase.\"}"
  },
  "18576": {
    "id": 18576,
    "course": "TCS15",
    "seq": 433,
    "acip": "SMAD KYI ME LCE HAm LA PHOG PAS DE LAS BYANG SEMS DKAR PO SKUD PA LTAR SKYA NAR GYIS BABS PAS SMAD KYI ME RLUNG THUR DU MNAN TE, SNYING GA'I THAD DU 'BAR 'DZAG SKRA SHAD 'DZAGS PA LTA BU LA SEMS GTAD PA DGOS TSUL SOGS GSUNGS,",
    "wylie": "smad kyi me lce haM la phog pas de las byang sems dkar po skud pa ltar skya nar gyis babs pas smad kyi me rlung thur du mnan te, snying ga'i thad du 'bar 'dzag skra shad 'dzags pa lta bu la sems gtad pa dgos tshul sogs gsungs",
    "english": "At the end of this process, the flame of the fire of the lower half strikes the hang letter, and this makes the white element of enlightenment ooze down in a thin, elongated stream, like a long string.  This presses down upon the fire and wind of the lower half.  Our Lama described how then we were to focus our mind at the blazing and dripping at the heart, falling in so fine a stream that it resembles a single strand of human hair.  Other details He spoke as well.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"TCS15\", \"acip\": \"SMAD KYI ME LCE HAm LA PHOG PAS DE LAS BYANG SEMS DKAR PO SKUD PA LTAR SKYA NAR GYIS BABS PAS SMAD KYI ME RLUNG THUR DU MNAN TE, SNYING GA'I THAD DU 'BAR 'DZAG SKRA SHAD 'DZAGS PA LTA BU LA SEMS GTAD PA DGOS TSUL SOGS GSUNGS,\", \"wylie\": \"smad kyi me lce haM la phog pas de las byang sems dkar po skud pa ltar skya nar gyis babs pas smad kyi me rlung thur du mnan te, snying ga'i thad du 'bar 'dzag skra shad 'dzags pa lta bu la sems gtad pa dgos tshul sogs gsungs\", \"english\": \"At the end of this process, the flame of the fire of the lower half strikes the hang letter, and this makes the white element of enlightenment ooze down in a thin, elongated stream, like a long string.  This presses down upon the fire and wind of the lower half.  Our Lama described how then we were to focus our mind at the blazing and dripping at the heart, falling in so fine a stream that it resembles a single strand of human hair.  Other details He spoke as well.\"}"
  },
  "1638": {
    "id": 1638,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 256,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, 'GRO BA RANG RANG GI NGO BOR THAD KA THAD KAR BDEN PAS, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE, YI DVAGS KYI NGO BOR RNAG KHRAG TU BDEN, MI'I NGO BOR CHUR BDEN LHA'I NGO BOR BDUD RTZIR BDEN PA YIN NO, , ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, 'gro ba rang rang gi ngo bor thad ka thad kar bden pas, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de, yi dvags kyi ngo bor rnag khrag tu bden, mi'i ngo bor chur bden lha'i ngo bor bdud rtsir bden pa yin no, , zer na",
    "english": "Someone else might make the following claim: Suppose a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit sit down together and look at a glass filled with water: the thing that we define as \"wet and flowing.\" Since to the perceptions of each different type of being it is real, the glass full of wet and flowing water is in reality pus and blood to the eyes of the craving spirit, and in reality water to the eyes of the human, and in reality ambrosia to the eyes of the pleasure being.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, 'GRO BA RANG RANG GI NGO BOR THAD KA THAD KAR BDEN PAS, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE, YI DVAGS KYI NGO BOR RNAG KHRAG TU BDEN, MI'I NGO BOR CHUR BDEN LHA'I NGO BOR BDUD RTZIR BDEN PA YIN NO, , ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, 'gro ba rang rang gi ngo bor thad ka thad kar bden pas, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de, yi dvags kyi ngo bor rnag khrag tu bden, mi'i ngo bor chur bden lha'i ngo bor bdud rtsir bden pa yin no, , zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone else might make the following claim: Suppose a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit sit down together and look at a glass filled with water: the thing that we define as \\\"wet and flowing.\\\" Since to the perceptions of each different type of being it is real, the glass full of wet and flowing water is in reality pus and blood to the eyes of the craving spirit, and in reality water to the eyes of the human, and in reality ambrosia to the eyes of the pleasure being.\"}"
  },
  "1644": {
    "id": 1644,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 262,
    "acip": "`GNYIS PA RANG LUGS BZHAG PA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LAS CAN GYI LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN PA MA YIN PA DANG , DE'I TSE TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM NGES PAR MI DGOS SHING , BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN DU SKYE BA DANG ,",
    "wylie": "`gnyis pa rang lugs bzhag pa la, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, las can gyi lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, chu phor pa gang po de dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun pa ma yin pa dang , de'i tshe tshad mtshungs gsum nges par mi dgos shing , brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de cha shas gsum ldan du skye ba dang",
    "english": "Here secondly is the section where we establish our own position. Now suppose three different types of beings--a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit, each with their own karma--sit down together and look upon a glass filled with water, the thing we define as \"wet and flowing.\" The glass of water is not at this point one thing which is simultaneously three different objects. Neither is it necessary in this situation for there to be three identical valid perceptions. And when the glass full of wet and flowing water occurs, it occurs with three different, distinct parts to it.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"`GNYIS PA RANG LUGS BZHAG PA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LAS CAN GYI LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN PA MA YIN PA DANG , DE'I TSE TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM NGES PAR MI DGOS SHING , BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN DU SKYE BA DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"`gnyis pa rang lugs bzhag pa la, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, las can gyi lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, chu phor pa gang po de dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun pa ma yin pa dang , de'i tshe tshad mtshungs gsum nges par mi dgos shing , brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de cha shas gsum ldan du skye ba dang\", \"english\": \"Here secondly is the section where we establish our own position. Now suppose three different types of beings--a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit, each with their own karma--sit down together and look upon a glass filled with water, the thing we define as \\\"wet and flowing.\\\" The glass of water is not at this point one thing which is simultaneously three different objects. Neither is it necessary in this situation for there to be three identical valid perceptions. And when the glass full of wet and flowing water occurs, it occurs with three different, distinct parts to it.\"}"
  },
  "1652": {
    "id": 1652,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 270,
    "acip": "BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DNGOS PO TSAD MAS GRUB PA GSUM SKYE BA'I DPE YANG YOD DE, LCAGS GONG DMAR 'BAR BA'I CHA SHAS GCIG GIS NYER LEN DANG LCAGS SNGAGS KYIS LHAN CIG BYED RKYEN BYAS PA LA BRTEN NAS, LCAGS SNGAGS BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA TSA BA'I REG BYA MI MYONG REG BYA GZHAN ZHIG MYONG BA DANG , LCAGS SNGAGS MA BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA, TSA BA'I REG BYA MYONG REG BYA GZHAN MI MYONG BA DANG ,",
    "wylie": "brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na dngos po tshad mas grub pa gsum skye ba'i dpe yang yod de, lcags gong dmar 'bar ba'i cha shas gcig gis nyer len dang lcags sngags kyis lhan cig byed rkyen byas pa la brten nas, lcags sngags btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na tsha ba'i reg bya mi myong reg bya gzhan zhig myong ba dang , lcags sngags ma btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na, tsha ba'i reg bya myong reg bya gzhan mi myong ba dang",
    "english": "There is, moreover, an example we can use for how, when the three different types of beings with their three karmas look all together at a glass full of something wet and flowing, there start to be three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception. Suppose there is a ball of red-hot steel; one piece of this ball provides the material cause, and the \"mantra of steel\" provides a contributing factor. Due to these two, a person who has used the mantra of steel on his hand can touch the ball, but he doesn't undergo any sensation of heat; instead, he feels some other sensation. A person who has not used the mantra on his hand touches the ball and does feel a sensation of heat, and no other kind of sensation.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DNGOS PO TSAD MAS GRUB PA GSUM SKYE BA'I DPE YANG YOD DE, LCAGS GONG DMAR 'BAR BA'I CHA SHAS GCIG GIS NYER LEN DANG LCAGS SNGAGS KYIS LHAN CIG BYED RKYEN BYAS PA LA BRTEN NAS, LCAGS SNGAGS BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA TSA BA'I REG BYA MI MYONG REG BYA GZHAN ZHIG MYONG BA DANG , LCAGS SNGAGS MA BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA, TSA BA'I REG BYA MYONG REG BYA GZHAN MI MYONG BA DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na dngos po tshad mas grub pa gsum skye ba'i dpe yang yod de, lcags gong dmar 'bar ba'i cha shas gcig gis nyer len dang lcags sngags kyis lhan cig byed rkyen byas pa la brten nas, lcags sngags btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na tsha ba'i reg bya mi myong reg bya gzhan zhig myong ba dang , lcags sngags ma btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na, tsha ba'i reg bya myong reg bya gzhan mi myong ba dang\", \"english\": \"There is, moreover, an example we can use for how, when the three different types of beings with their three karmas look all together at a glass full of something wet and flowing, there start to be three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception. Suppose there is a ball of red-hot steel; one piece of this ball provides the material cause, and the \\\"mantra of steel\\\" provides a contributing factor. Due to these two, a person who has used the mantra of steel on his hand can touch the ball, but he doesn't undergo any sensation of heat; instead, he feels some other sensation. A person who has not used the mantra on his hand touches the ball and does feel a sensation of heat, and no other kind of sensation.\"}"
  },
  "1656": {
    "id": 1656,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 274,
    "acip": "GSUM PA DE LA RTZOD PA SPONG BA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO LA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE, CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN GYI CHA CAN DU SKYES KYANG , 'GRO BA GSUM PO DES RANG RANG GI THUN MONG MA YIN PA'I LAS KYI DBANG GIS, 'GRO BA RE RES DNGOS PO GSUM GA MA MTHONG BAR KHAS BLANGS PA LA,",
    "wylie": "gsum pa de la rtsod pa spong ba la, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po la, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de, cha shas gsum ldan gyi cha can du skyes kyang , 'gro ba gsum po des rang rang gi thun mong ma yin pa'i las kyi dbang gis, 'gro ba re res dngos po gsum ga ma mthong bar khas blangs pa la",
    "english": "Here is the third part of our presentation, where we refute the rebuttal of representatives of other views. You will recall that our own position is describing a situation where beings of three different types, each with their own karma, are sitting together and looking at a glass filled with something that is wet and flowing. The glass filled with something wet and flowing exists, at this point, as something with three distinct parts. Nonetheless, no one of the beings is able to see all three things there, for they are each at the mercy of the particular karma that they themselves have collected. Representatives of other viewpoints now come to attack this position.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"GSUM PA DE LA RTZOD PA SPONG BA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO LA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE, CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN GYI CHA CAN DU SKYES KYANG , 'GRO BA GSUM PO DES RANG RANG GI THUN MONG MA YIN PA'I LAS KYI DBANG GIS, 'GRO BA RE RES DNGOS PO GSUM GA MA MTHONG BAR KHAS BLANGS PA LA,\", \"wylie\": \"gsum pa de la rtsod pa spong ba la, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po la, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de, cha shas gsum ldan gyi cha can du skyes kyang , 'gro ba gsum po des rang rang gi thun mong ma yin pa'i las kyi dbang gis, 'gro ba re res dngos po gsum ga ma mthong bar khas blangs pa la\", \"english\": \"Here is the third part of our presentation, where we refute the rebuttal of representatives of other views. You will recall that our own position is describing a situation where beings of three different types, each with their own karma, are sitting together and looking at a glass filled with something that is wet and flowing. The glass filled with something wet and flowing exists, at this point, as something with three distinct parts. Nonetheless, no one of the beings is able to see all three things there, for they are each at the mercy of the particular karma that they themselves have collected. Representatives of other viewpoints now come to attack this position.\"}"
  },
  "1661": {
    "id": 1661,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 279,
    "acip": "GNYIS PA DER THAL, PHYOGS SNGA MAS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE, DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN DU YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, SKYES BU GCIG GI GZUGS MDZES MI MDZES GNYIS KA YIN PAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, YI DVAGS KYIS MIG SHES DANG CHU KLUNG GI BAR DU RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS SKYE BAR KHAS MA BLANGS, YI DVAGS KYI MIG GIS RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS DANG CHU KLUNG GNYIS KA MTHONG BAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, LAS KYI DBANG GIS YI DVAGS KYI CHU KLUNG MA MTHONG BAR KHAS BLANGS PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "gnyis pa der thal, phyogs snga mas brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la, 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de, dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun du yang khas ma blangs, skyes bu gcig gi gzugs mdzes mi mdzes gnyis ka yin par yang khas ma blangs, yi dvags kyis mig shes dang chu klung gi bar du rnag khrag gi las gzugs skye bar khas ma blangs, yi dvags kyi mig gis rnag khrag gi las gzugs dang chu klung gnyis ka mthong bar yang khas ma blangs, las kyi dbang gis yi dvags kyi chu klung ma mthong bar khas blangs pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Our second point [that you have failed to comprehend the position we expressed above] is also quite true. Our original position was describing a situation where three different kinds of beings were sitting together and looking at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It is not our position that the glass full of something wet and flowing is one thing that is three different things. And it is not our position that there is such a thing as the physical appearance of a person which is at once both attractive and ugly. It is furthermore not our position that the blood and pus represent some kind of physical form which results from karma and grows up between the visual consciousness of the craving spirit and the stream of water. And it is not our position that the craving spirit's eyes see both this blood and pus as some kind of physical form resulting from karma, and the river of water at the same time. It is our position that, by force of his karma, the craving spirit is not able to see the stream of water.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"GNYIS PA DER THAL, PHYOGS SNGA MAS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE, DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN DU YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, SKYES BU GCIG GI GZUGS MDZES MI MDZES GNYIS KA YIN PAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, YI DVAGS KYIS MIG SHES DANG CHU KLUNG GI BAR DU RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS SKYE BAR KHAS MA BLANGS, YI DVAGS KYI MIG GIS RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS DANG CHU KLUNG GNYIS KA MTHONG BAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, LAS KYI DBANG GIS YI DVAGS KYI CHU KLUNG MA MTHONG BAR KHAS BLANGS PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"gnyis pa der thal, phyogs snga mas brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la, 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de, dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun du yang khas ma blangs, skyes bu gcig gi gzugs mdzes mi mdzes gnyis ka yin par yang khas ma blangs, yi dvags kyis mig shes dang chu klung gi bar du rnag khrag gi las gzugs skye bar khas ma blangs, yi dvags kyi mig gis rnag khrag gi las gzugs dang chu klung gnyis ka mthong bar yang khas ma blangs, las kyi dbang gis yi dvags kyi chu klung ma mthong bar khas blangs pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Our second point [that you have failed to comprehend the position we expressed above] is also quite true. Our original position was describing a situation where three different kinds of beings were sitting together and looking at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It is not our position that the glass full of something wet and flowing is one thing that is three different things. And it is not our position that there is such a thing as the physical appearance of a person which is at once both attractive and ugly. It is furthermore not our position that the blood and pus represent some kind of physical form which results from karma and grows up between the visual consciousness of the craving spirit and the stream of water. And it is not our position that the craving spirit's eyes see both this blood and pus as some kind of physical form resulting from karma, and the river of water at the same time. It is our position that, by force of his karma, the craving spirit is not able to see the stream of water.\"}"
  },
  "1663": {
    "id": 1663,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 281,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG NA RE, DNGOS GCIG YID NI THA DAD PHYIR, , DON MA GRUB PAR 'DOD PA YIN, , ZHES DNGOS PO GCIG LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BAR GSUNGS PA MI 'THAD PAR THAL, KHYOD LTAR NA DNGOS PO GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig na re, dngos gcig yid ni tha dad phyir, , don ma grub par 'dod pa yin, , zhes dngos po gcig la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung bar gsungs pa mi 'thad par thal, khyod ltar na dngos po gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir, der thal, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, dngos po tshad grub pa gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir, zer na",
    "english": "Someone else might come and make yet another claim: Let's talk about that quotation above, where it said: Insofar as [these different beings] have differing perceptions Of a single thing, we say it has no reality. The idea being expressed here is that a single object can be appearing in three different ways. This is incorrect because, according to you, what's happening is that three different objects are appearing in three different ways. And this certainly is your position; remember, you were describing a situation where three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something that's wet and flowing. You said that there were three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception, and that they were appearing in three different ways.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG NA RE, DNGOS GCIG YID NI THA DAD PHYIR, , DON MA GRUB PAR 'DOD PA YIN, , ZHES DNGOS PO GCIG LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BAR GSUNGS PA MI 'THAD PAR THAL, KHYOD LTAR NA DNGOS PO GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig na re, dngos gcig yid ni tha dad phyir, , don ma grub par 'dod pa yin, , zhes dngos po gcig la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung bar gsungs pa mi 'thad par thal, khyod ltar na dngos po gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir, der thal, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, dngos po tshad grub pa gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir, zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone else might come and make yet another claim: Let's talk about that quotation above, where it said: Insofar as [these different beings] have differing perceptions Of a single thing, we say it has no reality. The idea being expressed here is that a single object can be appearing in three different ways. This is incorrect because, according to you, what's happening is that three different objects are appearing in three different ways. And this certainly is your position; remember, you were describing a situation where three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something that's wet and flowing. You said that there were three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception, and that they were appearing in three different ways.\"}"
  },
  "1667": {
    "id": 1667,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 285,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG NA RE, RNAG KHRAG LA SOGS PA'I DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE PHAN TSUN GO SA 'GOG PA'I DNGOS PO MA YIN PAR THAL, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYI CHU PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM SRID PAR KHAS BLANGS PA LA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig na re, rnag khrag la sogs pa'i dngos po gsum po de phan tshun go sa 'gog pa'i dngos po ma yin par thal, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyi chu phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, dngos po tshad grub pa gsum srid par khas blangs pa la",
    "english": "Someone else may come now and make yet another argument: Let's talk about these three things: the pus and blood, and the other two. Are you implying then that these are not types of objects which would block each other from entering the space that each one occupies? After all, you were talking about a situation where those three types of beings, each with their own karma, sit down together and look at a glass full of water. And you said that your position was that it was possible for there to be three different objects there, each one confirmed by a valid perception.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG NA RE, RNAG KHRAG LA SOGS PA'I DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE PHAN TSUN GO SA 'GOG PA'I DNGOS PO MA YIN PAR THAL, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYI CHU PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM SRID PAR KHAS BLANGS PA LA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig na re, rnag khrag la sogs pa'i dngos po gsum po de phan tshun go sa 'gog pa'i dngos po ma yin par thal, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyi chu phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, dngos po tshad grub pa gsum srid par khas blangs pa la\", \"english\": \"Someone else may come now and make yet another argument: Let's talk about these three things: the pus and blood, and the other two. Are you implying then that these are not types of objects which would block each other from entering the space that each one occupies? After all, you were talking about a situation where those three types of beings, each with their own karma, sit down together and look at a glass full of water. And you said that your position was that it was possible for there to be three different objects there, each one confirmed by a valid perception.\"}"
  },
  "1669": {
    "id": 1669,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 287,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG NA RE, NYI SHU PA'I 'GREL BSHAD SLOB DPON DUL BA LHAS MDZAD PA LAS, DE LA RNAG GI THIGS PA 'GA' YANG MED NA RNAG GIS GANG BA'I KLUNG LTA GA LA YOD, LAS KYI RNAM PAR SMIN PA'I DBANG GIS DE DAG GIS DE MTHONG NGO , , ZHES GSUNGS PA DE MI 'THAD PAR THAL, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES TSAD MAR SONG BA'I RNAG DNGOS GNAS PA YOD PA'I PHYIR ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig na re, nyi shu pa'i 'grel bshad slob dpon dul ba lhas mdzad pa las, de la rnag gi thigs pa 'ga' yang med na rnag gis gang ba'i klung lta ga la yod, las kyi rnam par smin pa'i dbang gis de dag gis de mthong ngo , , zhes gsungs pa de mi 'thad par thal, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, yi dvags kyi mig shes tshad mar song ba'i rnag dngos gnas pa yod pa'i phyir zer na",
    "english": "Someone might make the following claim: In his Commentary to the Twenty Verses, Master Vinitadeva makes this statement-- If there was not a single drop of pus there, then how could there ever be a whole river of pus? They are forced to see it, through the ripening of their karma. According to you, this statement would have to be mistaken, because when the three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing, the visual consciousness of the craving spirit is a valid perception, and the pus is real pus.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG NA RE, NYI SHU PA'I 'GREL BSHAD SLOB DPON DUL BA LHAS MDZAD PA LAS, DE LA RNAG GI THIGS PA 'GA' YANG MED NA RNAG GIS GANG BA'I KLUNG LTA GA LA YOD, LAS KYI RNAM PAR SMIN PA'I DBANG GIS DE DAG GIS DE MTHONG NGO , , ZHES GSUNGS PA DE MI 'THAD PAR THAL, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES TSAD MAR SONG BA'I RNAG DNGOS GNAS PA YOD PA'I PHYIR ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig na re, nyi shu pa'i 'grel bshad slob dpon dul ba lhas mdzad pa las, de la rnag gi thigs pa 'ga' yang med na rnag gis gang ba'i klung lta ga la yod, las kyi rnam par smin pa'i dbang gis de dag gis de mthong ngo , , zhes gsungs pa de mi 'thad par thal, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, yi dvags kyi mig shes tshad mar song ba'i rnag dngos gnas pa yod pa'i phyir zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone might make the following claim: In his Commentary to the Twenty Verses, Master Vinitadeva makes this statement-- If there was not a single drop of pus there, then how could there ever be a whole river of pus? They are forced to see it, through the ripening of their karma. According to you, this statement would have to be mistaken, because when the three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing, the visual consciousness of the craving spirit is a valid perception, and the pus is real pus.\"}"
  },
  "1670": {
    "id": 1670,
    "course": "C05",
    "seq": 288,
    "acip": "SKYON MED DE, DE LTA BU'I BRGAL LAN JI SNYED CIG BYAS PA THAMS CAD PHYI DON 'GOG PA'I DBANG DU BYAS PA'I PHYIR, PHYI DON 'GOG PA'I TSUL YANG YOD DE, SNGON PO PHYI ROL DON DU GRUB NA, TSUR MTHONG GI RGYUD KYI SNGON 'DZIN DBANG PO'I MNGON SUM LA, SNGON PO SNANG BA NA SNGON 'DZIN SNGA MA 'GAGS PA'I TSE, SNGON 'DZIN PHYI MA SKYE BA'I BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA BRTEN NAS, SNANG BA MA YIN PAR SNGON PO PHYI ROL NAS RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG BA YIN NA, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS NAS, DNGOS PO GSUM SNANG BA DE'I TSE NA, RANG RANG GI LAS KYIS BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA MA BLTOS PAR, DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG DGOS,",
    "wylie": "skyon med de, de lta bu'i brgal lan ji snyed cig byas pa thams cad phyi don 'gog pa'i dbang du byas pa'i phyir, phyi don 'gog pa'i tshul yang yod de, sngon po phyi rol don du grub na, tshur mthong gi rgyud kyi sngon 'dzin dbang po'i mngon sum la, sngon po snang ba na sngon 'dzin snga ma 'gags pa'i tshe, sngon 'dzin phyi ma skye ba'i bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la brten nas, snang ba ma yin par sngon po phyi rol nas rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang ba yin na, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas nas, dngos po gsum snang ba de'i tshe na, rang rang gi las kyis bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la ma bltos par, dngos po gsum po de rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang dgos",
    "english": "And yet there is no such problem. No matter how many arguments of this kind you want to present, they are all made from the point of view of denying the existence of external objects. The way these arguments go is as follows. If the color blue were to exist as an external object, then the following would occur when this color appeared directly to a sense perception grasping blue; that is, with such a perception found in the mental stream of one of those who \"only sees this side\" [which is another name for those who have not yet perceived emptiness directly]. When an earlier instance of the perception of blue ends, what actually happens is that it plants a mental seed which eventually grows into a later instance of the same perception of blue, when the seed ripens. Suppose the blue were not just this kind of appearance, but rather an appearance where blue as an outer object were transmitting a likeness of itself and thereby appearing to one's perceptions. Something else then would be happening when the three different beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing. The three different objects would be appearing to them because each of the objects was transmitting a likeness of itself to their perception. All of this would be happening independent of any process where each being's karma planted a mental seed, which later ripened and produced the appearance of the object.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C05\", \"acip\": \"SKYON MED DE, DE LTA BU'I BRGAL LAN JI SNYED CIG BYAS PA THAMS CAD PHYI DON 'GOG PA'I DBANG DU BYAS PA'I PHYIR, PHYI DON 'GOG PA'I TSUL YANG YOD DE, SNGON PO PHYI ROL DON DU GRUB NA, TSUR MTHONG GI RGYUD KYI SNGON 'DZIN DBANG PO'I MNGON SUM LA, SNGON PO SNANG BA NA SNGON 'DZIN SNGA MA 'GAGS PA'I TSE, SNGON 'DZIN PHYI MA SKYE BA'I BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA BRTEN NAS, SNANG BA MA YIN PAR SNGON PO PHYI ROL NAS RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG BA YIN NA, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS NAS, DNGOS PO GSUM SNANG BA DE'I TSE NA, RANG RANG GI LAS KYIS BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA MA BLTOS PAR, DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG DGOS,\", \"wylie\": \"skyon med de, de lta bu'i brgal lan ji snyed cig byas pa thams cad phyi don 'gog pa'i dbang du byas pa'i phyir, phyi don 'gog pa'i tshul yang yod de, sngon po phyi rol don du grub na, tshur mthong gi rgyud kyi sngon 'dzin dbang po'i mngon sum la, sngon po snang ba na sngon 'dzin snga ma 'gags pa'i tshe, sngon 'dzin phyi ma skye ba'i bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la brten nas, snang ba ma yin par sngon po phyi rol nas rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang ba yin na, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas nas, dngos po gsum snang ba de'i tshe na, rang rang gi las kyis bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la ma bltos par, dngos po gsum po de rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang dgos\", \"english\": \"And yet there is no such problem. No matter how many arguments of this kind you want to present, they are all made from the point of view of denying the existence of external objects. The way these arguments go is as follows. If the color blue were to exist as an external object, then the following would occur when this color appeared directly to a sense perception grasping blue; that is, with such a perception found in the mental stream of one of those who \\\"only sees this side\\\" [which is another name for those who have not yet perceived emptiness directly]. When an earlier instance of the perception of blue ends, what actually happens is that it plants a mental seed which eventually grows into a later instance of the same perception of blue, when the seed ripens. Suppose the blue were not just this kind of appearance, but rather an appearance where blue as an outer object were transmitting a likeness of itself and thereby appearing to one's perceptions. Something else then would be happening when the three different beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing. The three different objects would be appearing to them because each of the objects was transmitting a likeness of itself to their perception. All of this would be happening independent of any process where each being's karma planted a mental seed, which later ripened and produced the appearance of the object.\"}"
  },
  "5952": {
    "id": 5952,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 401,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, 'GRO BA RANG RANG GI NGO BOR THAD KA THAD KAR BDEN PAS, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE, YI DVAGS KYI NGO BOR RNAG KHRAG TU BDEN, MI'I NGO BOR CHUR BDEN LHA'I NGO BOR BDUD RTZIR BDEN PA YIN NO, , ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, 'gro ba rang rang gi ngo bor thad ka thad kar bden pas, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de, yi dvags kyi ngo bor rnag khrag tu bden, mi'i ngo bor chur bden lha'i ngo bor bdud rtsir bden pa yin no, , zer na",
    "english": "Someone else might make the following claim: Suppose a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit sit down together and look at a glass filled with water: the thing that we define as \"wet and flowing.\" Since to the perceptions of each different type of being it is real, the glass full of wet and flowing water is in reality pus and blood to the eyes of the craving spirit, and in reality water to the eyes of the human, and in reality ambrosia to the eyes of the pleasure being.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, 'GRO BA RANG RANG GI NGO BOR THAD KA THAD KAR BDEN PAS, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE, YI DVAGS KYI NGO BOR RNAG KHRAG TU BDEN, MI'I NGO BOR CHUR BDEN LHA'I NGO BOR BDUD RTZIR BDEN PA YIN NO, , ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, 'gro ba rang rang gi ngo bor thad ka thad kar bden pas, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de, yi dvags kyi ngo bor rnag khrag tu bden, mi'i ngo bor chur bden lha'i ngo bor bdud rtsir bden pa yin no, , zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone else might make the following claim: Suppose a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit sit down together and look at a glass filled with water: the thing that we define as \\\"wet and flowing.\\\" Since to the perceptions of each different type of being it is real, the glass full of wet and flowing water is in reality pus and blood to the eyes of the craving spirit, and in reality water to the eyes of the human, and in reality ambrosia to the eyes of the pleasure being.\"}"
  },
  "5958": {
    "id": 5958,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 407,
    "acip": "`GNYIS PA RANG LUGS BZHAG PA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LAS CAN GYI LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN PA MA YIN PA DANG , DE'I TSE TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM NGES PAR MI DGOS SHING , BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN DU SKYE BA DANG ,",
    "wylie": "`gnyis pa rang lugs bzhag pa la, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, las can gyi lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, chu phor pa gang po de dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun pa ma yin pa dang , de'i tshe tshad mtshungs gsum nges par mi dgos shing , brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de cha shas gsum ldan du skye ba dang",
    "english": "Here secondly is the section where we establish our own position. Now suppose three different types of beings--a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit, each with their own karma--sit down together and look upon a glass filled with water, the thing we define as \"wet and flowing.\" The glass of water is not at this point one thing which is simultaneously three different objects. Neither is it necessary in this situation for there to be three identical valid perceptions. And when the glass full of wet and flowing water occurs, it occurs with three different, distinct parts to it. [Translator's note: When the phrase \"wet and flowing\" (the definition of the element of water) is used here, it should be understood as emphasizing the more general concept of a liquid, rather than the water which the human perceives.]",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"`GNYIS PA RANG LUGS BZHAG PA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LAS CAN GYI LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN PA MA YIN PA DANG , DE'I TSE TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM NGES PAR MI DGOS SHING , BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN DU SKYE BA DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"`gnyis pa rang lugs bzhag pa la, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, las can gyi lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, chu phor pa gang po de dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun pa ma yin pa dang , de'i tshe tshad mtshungs gsum nges par mi dgos shing , brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de cha shas gsum ldan du skye ba dang\", \"english\": \"Here secondly is the section where we establish our own position. Now suppose three different types of beings--a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit, each with their own karma--sit down together and look upon a glass filled with water, the thing we define as \\\"wet and flowing.\\\" The glass of water is not at this point one thing which is simultaneously three different objects. Neither is it necessary in this situation for there to be three identical valid perceptions. And when the glass full of wet and flowing water occurs, it occurs with three different, distinct parts to it. [Translator's note: When the phrase \\\"wet and flowing\\\" (the definition of the element of water) is used here, it should be understood as emphasizing the more general concept of a liquid, rather than the water which the human perceives.]\"}"
  },
  "5966": {
    "id": 5966,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 415,
    "acip": "BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DNGOS PO TSAD MAS GRUB PA GSUM SKYE BA'I DPE YANG YOD DE, LCAGS GONG DMAR 'BAR BA'I CHA SHAS GCIG GIS NYER LEN DANG LCAGS SNGAGS KYIS LHAN CIG BYED RKYEN BYAS PA LA BRTEN NAS, LCAGS SNGAGS BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA TSA BA'I REG BYA MI MYONG REG BYA GZHAN ZHIG MYONG BA DANG , LCAGS SNGAGS MA BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA, TSA BA'I REG BYA MYONG REG BYA GZHAN MI MYONG BA DANG ,",
    "wylie": "brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na dngos po tshad mas grub pa gsum skye ba'i dpe yang yod de, lcags gong dmar 'bar ba'i cha shas gcig gis nyer len dang lcags sngags kyis lhan cig byed rkyen byas pa la brten nas, lcags sngags btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na tsha ba'i reg bya mi myong reg bya gzhan zhig myong ba dang , lcags sngags ma btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na, tsha ba'i reg bya myong reg bya gzhan mi myong ba dang",
    "english": "There is, moreover, an example we can use for how, when the three different types of beings with their three karmas look all together at a glass full of something wet and flowing, there start to be three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception. Suppose there is a ball of red-hot steel; one piece of this ball provides the material cause, and the \"mantra of steel\" provides a contributing factor. Due to these two, a person who has used the mantra of steel on his hand can touch the ball, but he doesn't undergo any sensation of heat; instead, he feels some other sensation. A person who has not used the mantra on his hand touches the ball and does feel a sensation of heat, and no other kind of sensation.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DNGOS PO TSAD MAS GRUB PA GSUM SKYE BA'I DPE YANG YOD DE, LCAGS GONG DMAR 'BAR BA'I CHA SHAS GCIG GIS NYER LEN DANG LCAGS SNGAGS KYIS LHAN CIG BYED RKYEN BYAS PA LA BRTEN NAS, LCAGS SNGAGS BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA TSA BA'I REG BYA MI MYONG REG BYA GZHAN ZHIG MYONG BA DANG , LCAGS SNGAGS MA BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA, TSA BA'I REG BYA MYONG REG BYA GZHAN MI MYONG BA DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na dngos po tshad mas grub pa gsum skye ba'i dpe yang yod de, lcags gong dmar 'bar ba'i cha shas gcig gis nyer len dang lcags sngags kyis lhan cig byed rkyen byas pa la brten nas, lcags sngags btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na tsha ba'i reg bya mi myong reg bya gzhan zhig myong ba dang , lcags sngags ma btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na, tsha ba'i reg bya myong reg bya gzhan mi myong ba dang\", \"english\": \"There is, moreover, an example we can use for how, when the three different types of beings with their three karmas look all together at a glass full of something wet and flowing, there start to be three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception. Suppose there is a ball of red-hot steel; one piece of this ball provides the material cause, and the \\\"mantra of steel\\\" provides a contributing factor. Due to these two, a person who has used the mantra of steel on his hand can touch the ball, but he doesn't undergo any sensation of heat; instead, he feels some other sensation. A person who has not used the mantra on his hand touches the ball and does feel a sensation of heat, and no other kind of sensation.\"}"
  },
  "5970": {
    "id": 5970,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 419,
    "acip": "GSUM PA DE LA RTZOD PA SPONG BA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO LA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE, CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN GYI CHA CAN DU SKYES KYANG , 'GRO BA GSUM PO DES RANG RANG GI THUN MONG MA YIN PA'I LAS KYI DBANG GIS, 'GRO BA RE RES DNGOS PO GSUM GA MA MTHONG BAR KHAS BLANGS PA LA,",
    "wylie": "gsum pa de la rtsod pa spong ba la, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po la, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de, cha shas gsum ldan gyi cha can du skyes kyang , 'gro ba gsum po des rang rang gi thun mong ma yin pa'i las kyi dbang gis, 'gro ba re res dngos po gsum ga ma mthong bar khas blangs pa la",
    "english": "Here is the third part of our presentation, where we refute the rebuttal of representatives of other views. You will recall that our own position is describing a situation where beings of three different types, each with their own karma, are sitting together and looking at a glass filled with something that is wet and flowing. The glass filled with something wet and flowing exists, at this point, as something with three distinct parts. Nonetheless, no one of the beings is able to see all three things there, for they are each at the mercy of the particular karma that they themselves have collected. Representatives of other viewpoints now come to attack this position.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"GSUM PA DE LA RTZOD PA SPONG BA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO LA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE, CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN GYI CHA CAN DU SKYES KYANG , 'GRO BA GSUM PO DES RANG RANG GI THUN MONG MA YIN PA'I LAS KYI DBANG GIS, 'GRO BA RE RES DNGOS PO GSUM GA MA MTHONG BAR KHAS BLANGS PA LA,\", \"wylie\": \"gsum pa de la rtsod pa spong ba la, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po la, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de, cha shas gsum ldan gyi cha can du skyes kyang , 'gro ba gsum po des rang rang gi thun mong ma yin pa'i las kyi dbang gis, 'gro ba re res dngos po gsum ga ma mthong bar khas blangs pa la\", \"english\": \"Here is the third part of our presentation, where we refute the rebuttal of representatives of other views. You will recall that our own position is describing a situation where beings of three different types, each with their own karma, are sitting together and looking at a glass filled with something that is wet and flowing. The glass filled with something wet and flowing exists, at this point, as something with three distinct parts. Nonetheless, no one of the beings is able to see all three things there, for they are each at the mercy of the particular karma that they themselves have collected. Representatives of other viewpoints now come to attack this position.\"}"
  },
  "5975": {
    "id": 5975,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 424,
    "acip": "GNYIS PA DER THAL, PHYOGS SNGA MAS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE, DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN DU YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, SKYES BU GCIG GI GZUGS MDZES MI MDZES GNYIS KA YIN PAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, YI DVAGS KYIS MIG SHES DANG CHU KLUNG GI BAR DU RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS SKYE BAR KHAS MA BLANGS, YI DVAGS KYI MIG GIS RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS DANG CHU KLUNG GNYIS KA MTHONG BAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, LAS KYI DBANG GIS YI DVAGS KYI CHU KLUNG MA MTHONG BAR KHAS BLANGS PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "gnyis pa der thal, phyogs snga mas brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la, 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de, dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun du yang khas ma blangs, skyes bu gcig gi gzugs mdzes mi mdzes gnyis ka yin par yang khas ma blangs, yi dvags kyis mig shes dang chu klung gi bar du rnag khrag gi las gzugs skye bar khas ma blangs, yi dvags kyi mig gis rnag khrag gi las gzugs dang chu klung gnyis ka mthong bar yang khas ma blangs, las kyi dbang gis yi dvags kyi chu klung ma mthong bar khas blangs pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Our second point [that you have failed to comprehend the position we expressed above] is also quite true. Our original position was describing a situation where three different kinds of beings were sitting together and looking at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It is not our position that the glass full of something wet and flowing is one thing that is three different things. And it is not our position that there is such a thing as the physical appearance of a person which is at once both attractive and ugly. It is furthermore not our position that the blood and pus represent some kind of physical form which results from karma and grows up between the visual consciousness of the craving spirit and the stream of water. And it is not our position that the craving spirit's eyes see both this blood and pus as some kind of physical form resulting from karma, and the river of water at the same time. It is our position that, by force of his karma, the craving spirit is not able to see the stream of water.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"GNYIS PA DER THAL, PHYOGS SNGA MAS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE, DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN DU YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, SKYES BU GCIG GI GZUGS MDZES MI MDZES GNYIS KA YIN PAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, YI DVAGS KYIS MIG SHES DANG CHU KLUNG GI BAR DU RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS SKYE BAR KHAS MA BLANGS, YI DVAGS KYI MIG GIS RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS DANG CHU KLUNG GNYIS KA MTHONG BAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS, LAS KYI DBANG GIS YI DVAGS KYI CHU KLUNG MA MTHONG BAR KHAS BLANGS PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"gnyis pa der thal, phyogs snga mas brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la, 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de, dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun du yang khas ma blangs, skyes bu gcig gi gzugs mdzes mi mdzes gnyis ka yin par yang khas ma blangs, yi dvags kyis mig shes dang chu klung gi bar du rnag khrag gi las gzugs skye bar khas ma blangs, yi dvags kyi mig gis rnag khrag gi las gzugs dang chu klung gnyis ka mthong bar yang khas ma blangs, las kyi dbang gis yi dvags kyi chu klung ma mthong bar khas blangs pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Our second point [that you have failed to comprehend the position we expressed above] is also quite true. Our original position was describing a situation where three different kinds of beings were sitting together and looking at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It is not our position that the glass full of something wet and flowing is one thing that is three different things. And it is not our position that there is such a thing as the physical appearance of a person which is at once both attractive and ugly. It is furthermore not our position that the blood and pus represent some kind of physical form which results from karma and grows up between the visual consciousness of the craving spirit and the stream of water. And it is not our position that the craving spirit's eyes see both this blood and pus as some kind of physical form resulting from karma, and the river of water at the same time. It is our position that, by force of his karma, the craving spirit is not able to see the stream of water.\"}"
  },
  "5977": {
    "id": 5977,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 426,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG NA RE, DNGOS GCIG YID NI THA DAD PHYIR, , DON MA GRUB PAR 'DOD PA YIN, , ZHES DNGOS PO GCIG LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BAR GSUNGS PA MI 'THAD PAR THAL, KHYOD LTAR NA DNGOS PO GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig na re, dngos gcig yid ni tha dad phyir, , don ma grub par 'dod pa yin, , zhes dngos po gcig la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung bar gsungs pa mi 'thad par thal, khyod ltar na dngos po gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir, der thal, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, dngos po tshad grub pa gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir, zer na",
    "english": "Someone else might come and make yet another claim: Let's talk about that quotation above, where it said: Insofar as [these different beings] have differing perceptions Of a single thing, we say it has no reality. The idea being expressed here is that a single object can be appearing in three different ways. This is incorrect because, according to you, what's happening is that three different objects are appearing in three different ways. And this certainly is your position; remember, you were describing a situation where three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something that's wet and flowing. You said that there were three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception, and that they were appearing in three different ways.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG NA RE, DNGOS GCIG YID NI THA DAD PHYIR, , DON MA GRUB PAR 'DOD PA YIN, , ZHES DNGOS PO GCIG LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BAR GSUNGS PA MI 'THAD PAR THAL, KHYOD LTAR NA DNGOS PO GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig na re, dngos gcig yid ni tha dad phyir, , don ma grub par 'dod pa yin, , zhes dngos po gcig la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung bar gsungs pa mi 'thad par thal, khyod ltar na dngos po gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir, der thal, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, dngos po tshad grub pa gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir, zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone else might come and make yet another claim: Let's talk about that quotation above, where it said: Insofar as [these different beings] have differing perceptions Of a single thing, we say it has no reality. The idea being expressed here is that a single object can be appearing in three different ways. This is incorrect because, according to you, what's happening is that three different objects are appearing in three different ways. And this certainly is your position; remember, you were describing a situation where three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something that's wet and flowing. You said that there were three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception, and that they were appearing in three different ways.\"}"
  },
  "5981": {
    "id": 5981,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 430,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG NA RE, RNAG KHRAG LA SOGS PA'I DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE PHAN TSUN GO SA 'GOG PA'I DNGOS PO MA YIN PAR THAL, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYI CHU PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM SRID PAR KHAS BLANGS PA LA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig na re, rnag khrag la sogs pa'i dngos po gsum po de phan tshun go sa 'gog pa'i dngos po ma yin par thal, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyi chu phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, dngos po tshad grub pa gsum srid par khas blangs pa la",
    "english": "Someone else may come now and make yet another argument: Let's talk about these three things: the pus and blood, and the other two. Are you implying then that these are not types of objects which would block each other from entering the space that each one occupies? After all, you were talking about a situation where those three types of beings, each with their own karma, sit down together and look at a glass full of water. And you said that your position was that it was possible for there to be three different objects there, each one confirmed by a valid perception.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG NA RE, RNAG KHRAG LA SOGS PA'I DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE PHAN TSUN GO SA 'GOG PA'I DNGOS PO MA YIN PAR THAL, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYI CHU PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM SRID PAR KHAS BLANGS PA LA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig na re, rnag khrag la sogs pa'i dngos po gsum po de phan tshun go sa 'gog pa'i dngos po ma yin par thal, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyi chu phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, dngos po tshad grub pa gsum srid par khas blangs pa la\", \"english\": \"Someone else may come now and make yet another argument: Let's talk about these three things: the pus and blood, and the other two. Are you implying then that these are not types of objects which would block each other from entering the space that each one occupies? After all, you were talking about a situation where those three types of beings, each with their own karma, sit down together and look at a glass full of water. And you said that your position was that it was possible for there to be three different objects there, each one confirmed by a valid perception.\"}"
  },
  "5983": {
    "id": 5983,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 432,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG NA RE, NYI SHU PA'I 'GREL BSHAD SLOB DPON DUL BA LHAS MDZAD PA LAS, DE LA RNAG GI THIGS PA 'GA' YANG MED NA RNAG GIS GANG BA'I KLUNG LTA GA LA YOD, LAS KYI RNAM PAR SMIN PA'I DBANG GIS DE DAG GIS DE MTHONG NGO , , ZHES GSUNGS PA DE MI 'THAD PAR THAL, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES TSAD MAR SONG BA'I RNAG DNGOS GNAS PA YOD PA'I PHYIR ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig na re, nyi shu pa'i 'grel bshad slob dpon dul ba lhas mdzad pa las, de la rnag gi thigs pa 'ga' yang med na rnag gis gang ba'i klung lta ga la yod, las kyi rnam par smin pa'i dbang gis de dag gis de mthong ngo , , zhes gsungs pa de mi 'thad par thal, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, yi dvags kyi mig shes tshad mar song ba'i rnag dngos gnas pa yod pa'i phyir zer na",
    "english": "Someone might make the following claim: In his Commentary to the Twenty Verses, Master Vinitadeva makes this statement-- If there was not a single drop of pus there, then how could there ever be a whole river of pus? They are forced to see it, through the ripening of their karma. According to you, this statement would have to be mistaken, because when the three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing, the visual consciousness of the craving spirit is a valid perception, and the pus is real pus.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG NA RE, NYI SHU PA'I 'GREL BSHAD SLOB DPON DUL BA LHAS MDZAD PA LAS, DE LA RNAG GI THIGS PA 'GA' YANG MED NA RNAG GIS GANG BA'I KLUNG LTA GA LA YOD, LAS KYI RNAM PAR SMIN PA'I DBANG GIS DE DAG GIS DE MTHONG NGO , , ZHES GSUNGS PA DE MI 'THAD PAR THAL, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, YI DVAGS KYI MIG SHES TSAD MAR SONG BA'I RNAG DNGOS GNAS PA YOD PA'I PHYIR ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig na re, nyi shu pa'i 'grel bshad slob dpon dul ba lhas mdzad pa las, de la rnag gi thigs pa 'ga' yang med na rnag gis gang ba'i klung lta ga la yod, las kyi rnam par smin pa'i dbang gis de dag gis de mthong ngo , , zhes gsungs pa de mi 'thad par thal, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, yi dvags kyi mig shes tshad mar song ba'i rnag dngos gnas pa yod pa'i phyir zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone might make the following claim: In his Commentary to the Twenty Verses, Master Vinitadeva makes this statement-- If there was not a single drop of pus there, then how could there ever be a whole river of pus? They are forced to see it, through the ripening of their karma. According to you, this statement would have to be mistaken, because when the three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing, the visual consciousness of the craving spirit is a valid perception, and the pus is real pus.\"}"
  },
  "5984": {
    "id": 5984,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 433,
    "acip": "SKYON MED DE, DE LTA BU'I BRGAL LAN JI SNYED CIG BYAS PA THAMS CAD PHYI DON 'GOG PA'I DBANG DU BYAS PA'I PHYIR, PHYI DON 'GOG PA'I TSUL YANG YOD DE, SNGON PO PHYI ROL DON DU GRUB NA, TSUR MTHONG GI RGYUD KYI SNGON 'DZIN DBANG PO'I MNGON SUM LA, SNGON PO SNANG BA NA SNGON 'DZIN SNGA MA 'GAGS PA'I TSE, SNGON 'DZIN PHYI MA SKYE BA'I BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA BRTEN NAS, SNANG BA MA YIN PAR SNGON PO PHYI ROL NAS RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG BA YIN NA, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS NAS, DNGOS PO GSUM SNANG BA DE'I TSE NA, RANG RANG GI LAS KYIS BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA MA BLTOS PAR, DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG DGOS,",
    "wylie": "skyon med de, de lta bu'i brgal lan ji snyed cig byas pa thams cad phyi don 'gog pa'i dbang du byas pa'i phyir, phyi don 'gog pa'i tshul yang yod de, sngon po phyi rol don du grub na, tshur mthong gi rgyud kyi sngon 'dzin dbang po'i mngon sum la, sngon po snang ba na sngon 'dzin snga ma 'gags pa'i tshe, sngon 'dzin phyi ma skye ba'i bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la brten nas, snang ba ma yin par sngon po phyi rol nas rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang ba yin na, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas nas, dngos po gsum snang ba de'i tshe na, rang rang gi las kyis bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la ma bltos par, dngos po gsum po de rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang dgos",
    "english": "And yet there is no such problem. No matter how many arguments of this kind you want to present, they are all made from the point of view of denying the existence of external objects. The way these arguments go is as follows. If the color blue were to exist as an external object, then the following would occur when this color appeared directly to a sense perception grasping blue; that is, with such a perception found in the mental stream of one of those who \"only sees this side\" [which is another name for those who have not yet perceived emptiness directly]. When an earlier instance of the perception of blue ends, what actually happens is that it plants a mental seed which eventually grows into a later instance of the same perception of blue, when the seed ripens. Suppose the blue were not just this kind of appearance, but rather an appearance where blue as an outer object were transmitting a likeness of itself and thereby appearing to one's perceptions. Something else then would be happening when the three different beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing. The three different objects would be appearing to them because each of the objects was transmitting a likeness of itself to their perception. All of this would be happening independent of any process where each being's karma planted a mental seed, which later ripened and produced the appearance of the object.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"SKYON MED DE, DE LTA BU'I BRGAL LAN JI SNYED CIG BYAS PA THAMS CAD PHYI DON 'GOG PA'I DBANG DU BYAS PA'I PHYIR, PHYI DON 'GOG PA'I TSUL YANG YOD DE, SNGON PO PHYI ROL DON DU GRUB NA, TSUR MTHONG GI RGYUD KYI SNGON 'DZIN DBANG PO'I MNGON SUM LA, SNGON PO SNANG BA NA SNGON 'DZIN SNGA MA 'GAGS PA'I TSE, SNGON 'DZIN PHYI MA SKYE BA'I BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA BRTEN NAS, SNANG BA MA YIN PAR SNGON PO PHYI ROL NAS RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG BA YIN NA, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS NAS, DNGOS PO GSUM SNANG BA DE'I TSE NA, RANG RANG GI LAS KYIS BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA MA BLTOS PAR, DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG DGOS,\", \"wylie\": \"skyon med de, de lta bu'i brgal lan ji snyed cig byas pa thams cad phyi don 'gog pa'i dbang du byas pa'i phyir, phyi don 'gog pa'i tshul yang yod de, sngon po phyi rol don du grub na, tshur mthong gi rgyud kyi sngon 'dzin dbang po'i mngon sum la, sngon po snang ba na sngon 'dzin snga ma 'gags pa'i tshe, sngon 'dzin phyi ma skye ba'i bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la brten nas, snang ba ma yin par sngon po phyi rol nas rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang ba yin na, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas nas, dngos po gsum snang ba de'i tshe na, rang rang gi las kyis bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la ma bltos par, dngos po gsum po de rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang dgos\", \"english\": \"And yet there is no such problem. No matter how many arguments of this kind you want to present, they are all made from the point of view of denying the existence of external objects. The way these arguments go is as follows. If the color blue were to exist as an external object, then the following would occur when this color appeared directly to a sense perception grasping blue; that is, with such a perception found in the mental stream of one of those who \\\"only sees this side\\\" [which is another name for those who have not yet perceived emptiness directly]. When an earlier instance of the perception of blue ends, what actually happens is that it plants a mental seed which eventually grows into a later instance of the same perception of blue, when the seed ripens. Suppose the blue were not just this kind of appearance, but rather an appearance where blue as an outer object were transmitting a likeness of itself and thereby appearing to one's perceptions. Something else then would be happening when the three different beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing. The three different objects would be appearing to them because each of the objects was transmitting a likeness of itself to their perception. All of this would be happening independent of any process where each being's karma planted a mental seed, which later ripened and produced the appearance of the object.\"}"
  },
  "5993": {
    "id": 5993,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 442,
    "acip": "RNAM PA GCIG TU NA, YI DVAGS KYIS CHU KLUNG LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DE MA BSKAMS KYANG , RANG GI BLTA BYAR GYUR PA'I CHU KLUNG DANG , LONGS SPYOD BYAR GYUR PA'I CHU KLUNG BSKAMS PA YOD DE, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, LAS KYI DBANG GIS RNAG KHRAG SOGS DNGOS PO RNAM PA GSUM CIG CAR DU GRUB PA ZHIG YOD PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "rnam pa gcig tu na, yi dvags kyis chu klung la bltas pa de'i tshe na de ma bskams kyang , rang gi blta byar gyur pa'i chu klung dang , longs spyod byar gyur pa'i chu klung bskams pa yod de, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, las kyi dbang gis rnag khrag sogs dngos po rnam pa gsum cig car du grub pa zhig yod pa'i phyir",
    "english": "From one point of view, the river of water hasn't dried up when the craving spirit looks at it; but there is a river which has dried up, if you're talking about a river of water that the spirit can see, or a river of water that the spirit can drink from. This follows because when the three different types of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing, it is true that, due to the force of karma, three different kinds of objects exist there at the same time: the pus and blood, and the other two.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"RNAM PA GCIG TU NA, YI DVAGS KYIS CHU KLUNG LA BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DE MA BSKAMS KYANG , RANG GI BLTA BYAR GYUR PA'I CHU KLUNG DANG , LONGS SPYOD BYAR GYUR PA'I CHU KLUNG BSKAMS PA YOD DE, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, LAS KYI DBANG GIS RNAG KHRAG SOGS DNGOS PO RNAM PA GSUM CIG CAR DU GRUB PA ZHIG YOD PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"rnam pa gcig tu na, yi dvags kyis chu klung la bltas pa de'i tshe na de ma bskams kyang , rang gi blta byar gyur pa'i chu klung dang , longs spyod byar gyur pa'i chu klung bskams pa yod de, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, las kyi dbang gis rnag khrag sogs dngos po rnam pa gsum cig car du grub pa zhig yod pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"From one point of view, the river of water hasn't dried up when the craving spirit looks at it; but there is a river which has dried up, if you're talking about a river of water that the spirit can see, or a river of water that the spirit can drink from. This follows because when the three different types of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing, it is true that, due to the force of karma, three different kinds of objects exist there at the same time: the pus and blood, and the other two.\"}"
  },
  "5996": {
    "id": 5996,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 445,
    "acip": "BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA LHA MI YI DVAGS DUD 'GRO SOGS KYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, LHA YI BDUD RTZI SNANG BA'I SNANG BA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA DANG , YI DVAGS LA RNAG KHRAG SNANG BA'I SNANG BA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA YOD KYANG , SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG SOGS MED NA, SEMS CAN DMYAL BA'I MIG SHES DES, DMYAL BA'I LCAGS SREG DANG , RAL GRI'I NAGS TSAL DANG , ME TSOGS LA SOGS PA SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM LA TSAD MAR SONG BA YOD KYI, DE DAG DNGOS GNAS PA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA MED PAR THAL, KHYOD KYI DAM BCA' 'THAD PA GANG ZHIG, RGYU MTSAN MTSUNGS PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la lha mi yi dvags dud 'gro sogs kyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, lha yi bdud rtsi snang ba'i snang ba la tshad mar song ba dang , yi dvags la rnag khrag snang ba'i snang ba la tshad mar song ba yod kyang , snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag sogs med na, sems can dmyal ba'i mig shes des, dmyal ba'i lcags sreg dang , ral gri'i nags tshal dang , me tshogs la sogs pa snang ba'i snang ba tsam la tshad mar song ba yod kyi, de dag dngos gnas pa la tshad mar song ba med par thal, khyod kyi dam bca' 'thad pa gang zhig, rgyu mtshan mtshungs pa'i phyir",
    "english": "And consider again this case where a pleasure being and a human and a craving spirit and an animal or the like all sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. According to you, it would have to be genuine when something that just looked like ambrosia appeared to the pleasure being, and it would have to be genuine when something that just looked like pus and blood appeared to the craving spirit, but there couldn't be any pus and blood out there to appear as pus and blood. And if this were the case, then consider the visual consciousness of a being in the hells. It would then have to be a valid perception towards something appearing to it that just looked like the burning steel of the hells, and towards something that just looked like the forest of swords, and towards something that just looked like a mass of fire, and so on. Finally, this person would not have any valid perceptions at all towards any of these things as actual objects. Why would this all have to be so? If your idea were correct, it would have to be, for the logic here is identical to your own.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA LHA MI YI DVAGS DUD 'GRO SOGS KYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, LHA YI BDUD RTZI SNANG BA'I SNANG BA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA DANG , YI DVAGS LA RNAG KHRAG SNANG BA'I SNANG BA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA YOD KYANG , SNANG GZHIR GYUR PA'I RNAG KHRAG SOGS MED NA, SEMS CAN DMYAL BA'I MIG SHES DES, DMYAL BA'I LCAGS SREG DANG , RAL GRI'I NAGS TSAL DANG , ME TSOGS LA SOGS PA SNANG BA'I SNANG BA TZAM LA TSAD MAR SONG BA YOD KYI, DE DAG DNGOS GNAS PA LA TSAD MAR SONG BA MED PAR THAL, KHYOD KYI DAM BCA' 'THAD PA GANG ZHIG, RGYU MTSAN MTSUNGS PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la lha mi yi dvags dud 'gro sogs kyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, lha yi bdud rtsi snang ba'i snang ba la tshad mar song ba dang , yi dvags la rnag khrag snang ba'i snang ba la tshad mar song ba yod kyang , snang gzhir gyur pa'i rnag khrag sogs med na, sems can dmyal ba'i mig shes des, dmyal ba'i lcags sreg dang , ral gri'i nags tshal dang , me tshogs la sogs pa snang ba'i snang ba tsam la tshad mar song ba yod kyi, de dag dngos gnas pa la tshad mar song ba med par thal, khyod kyi dam bca' 'thad pa gang zhig, rgyu mtshan mtshungs pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"And consider again this case where a pleasure being and a human and a craving spirit and an animal or the like all sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. According to you, it would have to be genuine when something that just looked like ambrosia appeared to the pleasure being, and it would have to be genuine when something that just looked like pus and blood appeared to the craving spirit, but there couldn't be any pus and blood out there to appear as pus and blood. And if this were the case, then consider the visual consciousness of a being in the hells. It would then have to be a valid perception towards something appearing to it that just looked like the burning steel of the hells, and towards something that just looked like the forest of swords, and towards something that just looked like a mass of fire, and so on. Finally, this person would not have any valid perceptions at all towards any of these things as actual objects. Why would this all have to be so? If your idea were correct, it would have to be, for the logic here is identical to your own.\"}"
  },
  "5997": {
    "id": 5997,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 446,
    "acip": "'DOD NA, LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PAS, DER SKYES PA'I 'GRO BA'I LUS SREG PA DANG , GTUB PA LA SOGS PA DNGOS GNAS PA ZHIG MED PAR THAL, 'DOD PA DE'I PHYIR, 'DOD NA, DMYAL BA'I SDUG BSNGAL MED PAR THAL, 'DOD PA DE'I PHYIR, GZHAN YANG DE LTAR CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, MI'I MIG SHES TSAD MAR SONG BA'I CHU DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE'I TSE NA RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "'dod na, lcags sreg la sogs pas, der skyes pa'i 'gro ba'i lus sreg pa dang , gtub pa la sogs pa dngos gnas pa zhig med par thal, 'dod pa de'i phyir, 'dod na, dmyal ba'i sdug bsngal med par thal, 'dod pa de'i phyir, gzhan yang de ltar cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, mi'i mig shes tshad mar song ba'i chu dngos gnas pa med par thal, de'i tshe na rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med pa'i phyir",
    "english": "And suppose you agree that this hell being could have no valid perceptions of the type we mentioned. Well then, the burning steel and other objects could never perform any real actions: they could never burn the bodies of the people born there, they could never chop them up, and so on. Why? Well because of what you just agreed to. And suppose you agree to this; that they could never perform any real actions. Well then, the torment of the hells itself then must not even exist, by your own admission. And remember too that case where the beings all sit down and look at the same thing. You must be saying then that the actual water, the thing towards which the visual consciousness of the human is a valid perception, doesn't exist at all. Why? Well because, according to you, no actual pus and blood exists either in the same situation.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"'DOD NA, LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PAS, DER SKYES PA'I 'GRO BA'I LUS SREG PA DANG , GTUB PA LA SOGS PA DNGOS GNAS PA ZHIG MED PAR THAL, 'DOD PA DE'I PHYIR, 'DOD NA, DMYAL BA'I SDUG BSNGAL MED PAR THAL, 'DOD PA DE'I PHYIR, GZHAN YANG DE LTAR CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, MI'I MIG SHES TSAD MAR SONG BA'I CHU DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DE'I TSE NA RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"'dod na, lcags sreg la sogs pas, der skyes pa'i 'gro ba'i lus sreg pa dang , gtub pa la sogs pa dngos gnas pa zhig med par thal, 'dod pa de'i phyir, 'dod na, dmyal ba'i sdug bsngal med par thal, 'dod pa de'i phyir, gzhan yang de ltar cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, mi'i mig shes tshad mar song ba'i chu dngos gnas pa med par thal, de'i tshe na rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"And suppose you agree that this hell being could have no valid perceptions of the type we mentioned. Well then, the burning steel and other objects could never perform any real actions: they could never burn the bodies of the people born there, they could never chop them up, and so on. Why? Well because of what you just agreed to. And suppose you agree to this; that they could never perform any real actions. Well then, the torment of the hells itself then must not even exist, by your own admission. And remember too that case where the beings all sit down and look at the same thing. You must be saying then that the actual water, the thing towards which the visual consciousness of the human is a valid perception, doesn't exist at all. Why? Well because, according to you, no actual pus and blood exists either in the same situation.\"}"
  },
  "6004": {
    "id": 6004,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 453,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DMYAL BA'I LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PA DER SKYES PA'I SEMS CAN GYI SNANG BA TZAM YIN GYI, DNGOS GNAS SU GRUB PA MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DE 'DRA'I BYED PA PO SU YANG MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, SPYOD 'JUG LAS, LCAGS SREG SA GZHI SU YIS BYAS, , ME TSOGS DE DAG CI LAS BYUNG , , DE 'DRA DE DAG THAMS CAD KYANG , SDIG SEMS YIN PAR THUB PAS GSUNGS, , ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par thal, dmyal ba'i lcags sreg la sogs pa der skyes pa'i sems can gyi snang ba tsam yin gyi, dngos gnas su grub pa med pa'i phyir, der thal, de 'dra'i byed pa po su yang med pa'i phyir, der thal, spyod 'jug las, lcags sreg sa gzhi su yis byas, , me tshogs de dag ci las byung , , de 'dra de dag thams cad kyang , sdig sems yin par thub pas gsungs, , zhes gsungs pa'i phyir, zer na",
    "english": "Someone again may come and claim the following: Let's consider once more this situation where three different types of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. Isn't it true that there is no actual pus and blood there? Because isn't it true that the burning steel and so on in the hells is only something that appears to a person who is born there, but that there is nothing there which actually is these objects? Because isn't it true that there is no one at all who went and made all these kinds of things? And isn't this a fact, because doesn't the text of The Bodhisattva's Way of Life say: Who made the burning steel that acts As the floor of the world of hell? Where did all the mass of flames You find there all come from? The Able Ones have spoken that Everything there like this Is nothing at all other than The mind of what's non-virtue.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DMYAL BA'I LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PA DER SKYES PA'I SEMS CAN GYI SNANG BA TZAM YIN GYI, DNGOS GNAS SU GRUB PA MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DE 'DRA'I BYED PA PO SU YANG MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, SPYOD 'JUG LAS, LCAGS SREG SA GZHI SU YIS BYAS, , ME TSOGS DE DAG CI LAS BYUNG , , DE 'DRA DE DAG THAMS CAD KYANG , SDIG SEMS YIN PAR THUB PAS GSUNGS, , ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par thal, dmyal ba'i lcags sreg la sogs pa der skyes pa'i sems can gyi snang ba tsam yin gyi, dngos gnas su grub pa med pa'i phyir, der thal, de 'dra'i byed pa po su yang med pa'i phyir, der thal, spyod 'jug las, lcags sreg sa gzhi su yis byas, , me tshogs de dag ci las byung , , de 'dra de dag thams cad kyang , sdig sems yin par thub pas gsungs, , zhes gsungs pa'i phyir, zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone again may come and claim the following: Let's consider once more this situation where three different types of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. Isn't it true that there is no actual pus and blood there? Because isn't it true that the burning steel and so on in the hells is only something that appears to a person who is born there, but that there is nothing there which actually is these objects? Because isn't it true that there is no one at all who went and made all these kinds of things? And isn't this a fact, because doesn't the text of The Bodhisattva's Way of Life say: Who made the burning steel that acts As the floor of the world of hell? Where did all the mass of flames You find there all come from? The Able Ones have spoken that Everything there like this Is nothing at all other than The mind of what's non-virtue.\"}"
  },
  "6008": {
    "id": 6008,
    "course": "C15",
    "seq": 457,
    "acip": "DES NA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, TSAD MTSUNGS NGES PAR DGOS PA MA YIN TE, DGOS NA, PHOR PA GANG PO DE'I NANG DU GNAS KYI BLOS GNAS SHING , THA MAL GYI MIG SHES KYIS MI BZOD PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , DE'I TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , RGYA MTSO'I NANG NA GNAS PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , RGYA MTSO'I TSAD LA TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , LHA MA YIN 'GA' ZHIG LA, MTSON CHA PHOR GANG SNANG DGOS PA LA SOGS PA'I SKYON YOD PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "des na, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, tshad mtshungs nges par dgos pa ma yin te, dgos na, phor pa gang po de'i nang du gnas kyi blos gnas shing , tha mal gyi mig shes kyis mi bzod pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , de'i tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , rgya mtsho'i nang na gnas pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , rgya mtsho'i tshad la tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , lha ma yin 'ga' zhig la, mtshon cha phor gang snang dgos pa la sogs pa'i skyon yod pa'i phyir",
    "english": "In conclusion now, let us consider again these three kinds of beings, each with their different karma, as they sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It's not necessarily true that they must all have valid perceptions which are identical. If they did, then the three beings looking at the glass of water would have to think of the water as a place to live, in the way that a creature living in water would. The three beings as well would have to see the water in the same way that microscopic organisms living in the water, little beings imperceptible to normal visual consciousness, see it with their own visual consciousness. Then too the visual consciousness of microscopic organisms living in the depths of the ocean would have to be a valid perception towards the entire extent of the sea. And certain kinds of near-gods too would have to see weapons as glasses of water, and on and on; the problems raised would be many.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C15\", \"acip\": \"DES NA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, TSAD MTSUNGS NGES PAR DGOS PA MA YIN TE, DGOS NA, PHOR PA GANG PO DE'I NANG DU GNAS KYI BLOS GNAS SHING , THA MAL GYI MIG SHES KYIS MI BZOD PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , DE'I TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , RGYA MTSO'I NANG NA GNAS PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , RGYA MTSO'I TSAD LA TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , LHA MA YIN 'GA' ZHIG LA, MTSON CHA PHOR GANG SNANG DGOS PA LA SOGS PA'I SKYON YOD PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"des na, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, tshad mtshungs nges par dgos pa ma yin te, dgos na, phor pa gang po de'i nang du gnas kyi blos gnas shing , tha mal gyi mig shes kyis mi bzod pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , de'i tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , rgya mtsho'i nang na gnas pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , rgya mtsho'i tshad la tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , lha ma yin 'ga' zhig la, mtshon cha phor gang snang dgos pa la sogs pa'i skyon yod pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"In conclusion now, let us consider again these three kinds of beings, each with their different karma, as they sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It's not necessarily true that they must all have valid perceptions which are identical. If they did, then the three beings looking at the glass of water would have to think of the water as a place to live, in the way that a creature living in water would. The three beings as well would have to see the water in the same way that microscopic organisms living in the water, little beings imperceptible to normal visual consciousness, see it with their own visual consciousness. Then too the visual consciousness of microscopic organisms living in the depths of the ocean would have to be a valid perception towards the entire extent of the sea. And certain kinds of near-gods too would have to see weapons as glasses of water, and on and on; the problems raised would be many.\"}"
  },
  "6850": {
    "id": 6850,
    "course": "C16",
    "seq": 832,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, 'GRO BA RANG RANG GI NGO BOR THAD KA THAD KAR BDEN PAS, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE, YI DVAGS KYI NGO BOR RNAG KHRAG TU BDEN, MI'I NGO BOR CHUR BDEN LHA'I NGO BOR BDUD RTZIR BDEN PA YIN NO, , ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, 'gro ba rang rang gi ngo bor thad ka thad kar bden pas, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de, yi dvags kyi ngo bor rnag khrag tu bden, mi'i ngo bor chur bden lha'i ngo bor bdud rtsir bden pa yin no, , zer na",
    "english": "Someone else might make the following claim: Suppose a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit sit down together and look at a glass filled with water: the thing that we define as \"wet and flowing.\" Since to the perceptions of each different type of being it is real, the glass full of wet and flowing water is in reality pus and blood to the eyes of the craving spirit, and in reality water to the eyes of the human, and in reality ambrosia to the eyes of the pleasure being.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA, 'GRO BA RANG RANG GI NGO BOR THAD KA THAD KAR BDEN PAS, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE, YI DVAGS KYI NGO BOR RNAG KHRAG TU BDEN, MI'I NGO BOR CHUR BDEN LHA'I NGO BOR BDUD RTZIR BDEN PA YIN NO, , ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na, 'gro ba rang rang gi ngo bor thad ka thad kar bden pas, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de, yi dvags kyi ngo bor rnag khrag tu bden, mi'i ngo bor chur bden lha'i ngo bor bdud rtsir bden pa yin no, , zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone else might make the following claim: Suppose a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit sit down together and look at a glass filled with water: the thing that we define as \\\"wet and flowing.\\\" Since to the perceptions of each different type of being it is real, the glass full of wet and flowing water is in reality pus and blood to the eyes of the craving spirit, and in reality water to the eyes of the human, and in reality ambrosia to the eyes of the pleasure being.\"}"
  },
  "6856": {
    "id": 6856,
    "course": "C16",
    "seq": 838,
    "acip": "`GNYIS PA RANG LUGS BZHAG PA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LAS CAN GYI LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN PA MA YIN PA DANG , DE'I TSE TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM NGES PAR MI DGOS SHING , BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN DU SKYE BA DANG ,",
    "wylie": "`gnyis pa rang lugs bzhag pa la, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, las can gyi lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, chu phor pa gang po de dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun pa ma yin pa dang , de'i tshe tshad mtshungs gsum nges par mi dgos shing , brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de cha shas gsum ldan du skye ba dang",
    "english": "Here secondly is the section where we establish our own position. Now suppose three different types of beings--a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit, each with their own karma--sit down together and look upon a glass filled with water, the thing we define as \"wet and flowing.\" The glass of water is not at this point one thing which is simultaneously three different objects. Neither is it necessary in this situation for there to be three identical valid perceptions. And when the glass full of wet and flowing water occurs, it occurs with three different, distinct parts to it.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"`GNYIS PA RANG LUGS BZHAG PA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LAS CAN GYI LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN PA MA YIN PA DANG , DE'I TSE TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM NGES PAR MI DGOS SHING , BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN DU SKYE BA DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"`gnyis pa rang lugs bzhag pa la, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, las can gyi lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, chu phor pa gang po de dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun pa ma yin pa dang , de'i tshe tshad mtshungs gsum nges par mi dgos shing , brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de cha shas gsum ldan du skye ba dang\", \"english\": \"Here secondly is the section where we establish our own position. Now suppose three different types of beings--a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit, each with their own karma--sit down together and look upon a glass filled with water, the thing we define as \\\"wet and flowing.\\\" The glass of water is not at this point one thing which is simultaneously three different objects. Neither is it necessary in this situation for there to be three identical valid perceptions. And when the glass full of wet and flowing water occurs, it occurs with three different, distinct parts to it.\"}"
  },
  "6863": {
    "id": 6863,
    "course": "C16",
    "seq": 845,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DMYAL BA'I LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PA DER SKYES PA'I SEMS CAN GYI SNANG BA TZAM YIN GYI, DNGOS GNAS SU GRUB PA MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DE 'DRA'I BYED PA PO SU YANG MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, SPYOD 'JUG LAS, LCAGS SREG SA GZHI SU YIS BYAS, , ME TSOGS DE DAG CI LAS BYUNG , , DE 'DRA DE DAG THAMS CAD KYANG , SDIG SEMS YIN PAR THUB PAS GSUNGS, , ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par thal, dmyal ba'i lcags sreg la sogs pa der skyes pa'i sems can gyi snang ba tsam yin gyi, dngos gnas su grub pa med pa'i phyir, der thal, de 'dra'i byed pa po su yang med pa'i phyir, der thal, spyod 'jug las, lcags sreg sa gzhi su yis byas, , me tshogs de dag ci las byung , , de 'dra de dag thams cad kyang , sdig sems yin par thub pas gsungs, , zhes gsungs pa'i phyir, zer na",
    "english": "Someone again may come and claim the following: Let's consider once more this situation where three different types of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. Isn't it true that there is no actual pus and blood there? Because isn't it true that the burning steel and so on in the hells is only something that appears to a person who is born there, but that there is nothing there which actually is these objects? Because isn't it true that there is no one at all who went and made all these kinds of things? And isn't this a fact, because doesn't the text of The Bodhisattva's Way of Life say: Who made the burning steel that acts As the floor of the world of hell? Where did all the mass of flames You find there all come from? The Able Ones have spoken that Everything there like this Is nothing at all other than The mind of what's non-virtue.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG, 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE NA, RNAG KHRAG DNGOS GNAS PA MED PAR THAL, DMYAL BA'I LCAGS SREG LA SOGS PA DER SKYES PA'I SEMS CAN GYI SNANG BA TZAM YIN GYI, DNGOS GNAS SU GRUB PA MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, DE 'DRA'I BYED PA PO SU YANG MED PA'I PHYIR, DER THAL, SPYOD 'JUG LAS, LCAGS SREG SA GZHI SU YIS BYAS, , ME TSOGS DE DAG CI LAS BYUNG , , DE 'DRA DE DAG THAMS CAD KYANG , SDIG SEMS YIN PAR THUB PAS GSUNGS, , ZHES GSUNGS PA'I PHYIR, ZER NA,\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig, 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa'i tshe na, rnag khrag dngos gnas pa med par thal, dmyal ba'i lcags sreg la sogs pa der skyes pa'i sems can gyi snang ba tsam yin gyi, dngos gnas su grub pa med pa'i phyir, der thal, de 'dra'i byed pa po su yang med pa'i phyir, der thal, spyod 'jug las, lcags sreg sa gzhi su yis byas, , me tshogs de dag ci las byung , , de 'dra de dag thams cad kyang , sdig sems yin par thub pas gsungs, , zhes gsungs pa'i phyir, zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone again may come and claim the following: Let's consider once more this situation where three different types of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. Isn't it true that there is no actual pus and blood there? Because isn't it true that the burning steel and so on in the hells is only something that appears to a person who is born there, but that there is nothing there which actually is these objects? Because isn't it true that there is no one at all who went and made all these kinds of things? And isn't this a fact, because doesn't the text of The Bodhisattva's Way of Life say: Who made the burning steel that acts As the floor of the world of hell? Where did all the mass of flames You find there all come from? The Able Ones have spoken that Everything there like this Is nothing at all other than The mind of what's non-virtue.\"}"
  },
  "6867": {
    "id": 6867,
    "course": "C16",
    "seq": 849,
    "acip": "DES NA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, TSAD MTSUNGS NGES PAR DGOS PA MA YIN TE, DGOS NA, PHOR PA GANG PO DE'I NANG DU GNAS KYI BLOS GNAS SHING , THA MAL GYI MIG SHES KYIS MI BZOD PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , DE'I TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , RGYA MTSO'I NANG NA GNAS PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , RGYA MTSO'I TSAD LA TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , LHA MA YIN 'GA' ZHIG LA, MTSON CHA PHOR GANG SNANG DGOS PA LA SOGS PA'I SKYON YOD PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "des na, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, tshad mtshungs nges par dgos pa ma yin te, dgos na, phor pa gang po de'i nang du gnas kyi blos gnas shing , tha mal gyi mig shes kyis mi bzod pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , de'i tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , rgya mtsho'i nang na gnas pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , rgya mtsho'i tshad la tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , lha ma yin 'ga' zhig la, mtshon cha phor gang snang dgos pa la sogs pa'i skyon yod pa'i phyir",
    "english": "In conclusion now, let us consider again these three kinds of beings, each with their different karma, as they sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It's not necessarily true that they must all have valid perceptions which are identical. If they did, then the three beings looking at the glass of water would have to think of the water as a place to live, in the way that a creature living in water would. The three beings as well would have to see the water in the same way that microscopic organisms living in the water, little beings imperceptible to normal visual consciousness, see it with their own visual consciousness. Then too the visual consciousness of microscopic organisms living in the depths of the ocean would have to be a valid perception towards the entire extent of the sea. And certain kinds of near-gods too would have to see weapons as glasses of water, and on and on; the problems raised would be many.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C16\", \"acip\": \"DES NA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, TSAD MTSUNGS NGES PAR DGOS PA MA YIN TE, DGOS NA, PHOR PA GANG PO DE'I NANG DU GNAS KYI BLOS GNAS SHING , THA MAL GYI MIG SHES KYIS MI BZOD PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , DE'I TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , RGYA MTSO'I NANG NA GNAS PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , RGYA MTSO'I TSAD LA TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , LHA MA YIN 'GA' ZHIG LA, MTSON CHA PHOR GANG SNANG DGOS PA LA SOGS PA'I SKYON YOD PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"des na, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, tshad mtshungs nges par dgos pa ma yin te, dgos na, phor pa gang po de'i nang du gnas kyi blos gnas shing , tha mal gyi mig shes kyis mi bzod pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , de'i tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , rgya mtsho'i nang na gnas pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , rgya mtsho'i tshad la tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , lha ma yin 'ga' zhig la, mtshon cha phor gang snang dgos pa la sogs pa'i skyon yod pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"In conclusion now, let us consider again these three kinds of beings, each with their different karma, as they sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It's not necessarily true that they must all have valid perceptions which are identical. If they did, then the three beings looking at the glass of water would have to think of the water as a place to live, in the way that a creature living in water would. The three beings as well would have to see the water in the same way that microscopic organisms living in the water, little beings imperceptible to normal visual consciousness, see it with their own visual consciousness. Then too the visual consciousness of microscopic organisms living in the depths of the ocean would have to be a valid perception towards the entire extent of the sea. And certain kinds of near-gods too would have to see weapons as glasses of water, and on and on; the problems raised would be many.\"}"
  },
  "8916": {
    "id": 8916,
    "course": "C18",
    "seq": 1121,
    "acip": "`GNYIS PA RANG LUGS BZHAG PA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LAS CAN GYI LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN PA MA YIN PA DANG , DE'I TSE TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM NGES PAR MI DGOS SHING , BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN DU SKYE BA DANG ,",
    "wylie": "`gnyis pa rang lugs bzhag pa la, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, las can gyi lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, chu phor pa gang po de dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun pa ma yin pa dang , de'i tshe tshad mtshungs gsum nges par mi dgos shing , brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de cha shas gsum ldan du skye ba dang",
    "english": "Here secondly is the section where we establish our own position. Now suppose three different types of beings--a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit, each with their own karma--sit down together and look upon a glass filled with water, the thing we define as \"wet and flowing.\" The glass of water is not at this point one thing which is simultaneously three different objects. Neither is it necessary in this situation for there to be three identical valid perceptions. And when the glass full of wet and flowing water occurs, it occurs with three different, distinct parts to it. [Translator's note: When the phrase \"wet and flowing\" (the definition of the element of water) is used here, it should be understood as emphasizing the more general concept of a liquid, rather than the water which the human perceives.]",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C18\", \"acip\": \"`GNYIS PA RANG LUGS BZHAG PA LA, BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA, LAS CAN GYI LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN PA MA YIN PA DANG , DE'I TSE TSAD MTSUNGS GSUM NGES PAR MI DGOS SHING , BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE CHA SHAS GSUM LDAN DU SKYE BA DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"`gnyis pa rang lugs bzhag pa la, brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la, las can gyi lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, chu phor pa gang po de dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun pa ma yin pa dang , de'i tshe tshad mtshungs gsum nges par mi dgos shing , brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de cha shas gsum ldan du skye ba dang\", \"english\": \"Here secondly is the section where we establish our own position. Now suppose three different types of beings--a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit, each with their own karma--sit down together and look upon a glass filled with water, the thing we define as \\\"wet and flowing.\\\" The glass of water is not at this point one thing which is simultaneously three different objects. Neither is it necessary in this situation for there to be three identical valid perceptions. And when the glass full of wet and flowing water occurs, it occurs with three different, distinct parts to it. [Translator's note: When the phrase \\\"wet and flowing\\\" (the definition of the element of water) is used here, it should be understood as emphasizing the more general concept of a liquid, rather than the water which the human perceives.]\"}"
  },
  "8924": {
    "id": 8924,
    "course": "C18",
    "seq": 1129,
    "acip": "BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DNGOS PO TSAD MAS GRUB PA GSUM SKYE BA'I DPE YANG YOD DE, LCAGS GONG DMAR 'BAR BA'I CHA SHAS GCIG GIS NYER LEN DANG LCAGS SNGAGS KYIS LHAN CIG BYED RKYEN BYAS PA LA BRTEN NAS, LCAGS SNGAGS BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA TSA BA'I REG BYA MI MYONG REG BYA GZHAN ZHIG MYONG BA DANG , LCAGS SNGAGS MA BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA, TSA BA'I REG BYA MYONG REG BYA GZHAN MI MYONG BA DANG ,",
    "wylie": "brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na dngos po tshad mas grub pa gsum skye ba'i dpe yang yod de, lcags gong dmar 'bar ba'i cha shas gcig gis nyer len dang lcags sngags kyis lhan cig byed rkyen byas pa la brten nas, lcags sngags btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na tsha ba'i reg bya mi myong reg bya gzhan zhig myong ba dang , lcags sngags ma btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na, tsha ba'i reg bya myong reg bya gzhan mi myong ba dang",
    "english": "There is, moreover, an example we can use for how, when the three different types of beings with their three karmas look all together at a glass full of something wet and flowing, there start to be three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception. Suppose there is a ball of red-hot steel; one piece of this ball provides the material cause, and the \"mantra of steel\" provides a contributing factor. Due to these two, a person who has used the mantra of steel on his hand can touch the ball, but he doesn't undergo any sensation of heat; instead, he feels some other sensation. A person who has not used the mantra on his hand touches the ball and does feel a sensation of heat, and no other kind of sensation.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C18\", \"acip\": \"BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DNGOS PO TSAD MAS GRUB PA GSUM SKYE BA'I DPE YANG YOD DE, LCAGS GONG DMAR 'BAR BA'I CHA SHAS GCIG GIS NYER LEN DANG LCAGS SNGAGS KYIS LHAN CIG BYED RKYEN BYAS PA LA BRTEN NAS, LCAGS SNGAGS BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA TSA BA'I REG BYA MI MYONG REG BYA GZHAN ZHIG MYONG BA DANG , LCAGS SNGAGS MA BTAB PA'I LAG PAS REG PA DE'I TSE NA, TSA BA'I REG BYA MYONG REG BYA GZHAN MI MYONG BA DANG ,\", \"wylie\": \"brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na dngos po tshad mas grub pa gsum skye ba'i dpe yang yod de, lcags gong dmar 'bar ba'i cha shas gcig gis nyer len dang lcags sngags kyis lhan cig byed rkyen byas pa la brten nas, lcags sngags btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na tsha ba'i reg bya mi myong reg bya gzhan zhig myong ba dang , lcags sngags ma btab pa'i lag pas reg pa de'i tshe na, tsha ba'i reg bya myong reg bya gzhan mi myong ba dang\", \"english\": \"There is, moreover, an example we can use for how, when the three different types of beings with their three karmas look all together at a glass full of something wet and flowing, there start to be three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception. Suppose there is a ball of red-hot steel; one piece of this ball provides the material cause, and the \\\"mantra of steel\\\" provides a contributing factor. Due to these two, a person who has used the mantra of steel on his hand can touch the ball, but he doesn't undergo any sensation of heat; instead, he feels some other sensation. A person who has not used the mantra on his hand touches the ball and does feel a sensation of heat, and no other kind of sensation.\"}"
  },
  "8928": {
    "id": 8928,
    "course": "C18",
    "seq": 1133,
    "acip": "DES NA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, TSAD MTSUNGS NGES PAR DGOS PA MA YIN TE, DGOS NA, PHOR PA GANG PO DE'I NANG DU GNAS KYI BLOS GNAS SHING , THA MAL GYI MIG SHES KYIS MI BZOD PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , DE'I TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , RGYA MTSO'I NANG NA GNAS PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , RGYA MTSO'I TSAD LA TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , LHA MA YIN 'GA' ZHIG LA, MTSON CHA PHOR GANG SNANG DGOS PA LA SOGS PA'I SKYON YOD PA'I PHYIR,",
    "wylie": "des na, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, tshad mtshungs nges par dgos pa ma yin te, dgos na, phor pa gang po de'i nang du gnas kyi blos gnas shing , tha mal gyi mig shes kyis mi bzod pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , de'i tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , rgya mtsho'i nang na gnas pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , rgya mtsho'i tshad la tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , lha ma yin 'ga' zhig la, mtshon cha phor gang snang dgos pa la sogs pa'i skyon yod pa'i phyir",
    "english": "In conclusion now, let us consider again these three kinds of beings, each with their different karma, as they sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It's not necessarily true that they must all have valid perceptions which are identical. If they did, then the three beings looking at the glass of water would have to think of the water as a place to live, in the way that a creature living in water would. The three beings as well would have to see the water in the same way that microscopic organisms living in the water, little beings imperceptible to normal visual consciousness, see it with their own visual consciousness. Then too the visual consciousness of microscopic organisms living in the depths of the ocean would have to be a valid perception towards the entire extent of the sea. And certain kinds of near-gods too would have to see weapons as glasses of water, and on and on; the problems raised would be many.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"C18\", \"acip\": \"DES NA, LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE, TSAD MTSUNGS NGES PAR DGOS PA MA YIN TE, DGOS NA, PHOR PA GANG PO DE'I NANG DU GNAS KYI BLOS GNAS SHING , THA MAL GYI MIG SHES KYIS MI BZOD PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , DE'I TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , RGYA MTSO'I NANG NA GNAS PA'I SROG CHAGS PHRA MO'I MIG SHES KYIS KYANG , RGYA MTSO'I TSAD LA TSAD MAR 'GRO DGOS PA DANG , LHA MA YIN 'GA' ZHIG LA, MTSON CHA PHOR GANG SNANG DGOS PA LA SOGS PA'I SKYON YOD PA'I PHYIR,\", \"wylie\": \"des na, las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe, tshad mtshungs nges par dgos pa ma yin te, dgos na, phor pa gang po de'i nang du gnas kyi blos gnas shing , tha mal gyi mig shes kyis mi bzod pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , de'i tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , rgya mtsho'i nang na gnas pa'i srog chags phra mo'i mig shes kyis kyang , rgya mtsho'i tshad la tshad mar 'gro dgos pa dang , lha ma yin 'ga' zhig la, mtshon cha phor gang snang dgos pa la sogs pa'i skyon yod pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"In conclusion now, let us consider again these three kinds of beings, each with their different karma, as they sit down together and look at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It's not necessarily true that they must all have valid perceptions which are identical. If they did, then the three beings looking at the glass of water would have to think of the water as a place to live, in the way that a creature living in water would. The three beings as well would have to see the water in the same way that microscopic organisms living in the water, little beings imperceptible to normal visual consciousness, see it with their own visual consciousness. Then too the visual consciousness of microscopic organisms living in the depths of the ocean would have to be a valid perception towards the entire extent of the sea. And certain kinds of near-gods too would have to see weapons as glasses of water, and on and on; the problems raised would be many.\"}"
  },
  "32873": {
    "id": 32873,
    "course": "EM",
    "seq": 165,
    "acip": "GZHAN YANG, MIG SHES LA SNGON PO'I RNAM PA SHAR BA DE PHYI ROL DON GYIS GTAD PA'I RNAM PA MA YIN TE YIN NA, CHU PHOR GANG LA LAS CAN GYI LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE,",
    "wylie": "gzhan yang, mig shes la sngon po'i rnam pa shar ba de phyi rol don gyis gtad pa'i rnam pa ma yin te yin na, chu phor gang la las can gyi lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa'i tshe",
    "english": "We can also argue it this way: When an image of blue appears to our visual perception, it's not as if some outer object is sending the image to our eyes. Because if it were this way, there's a problem in a situation where three beings with different karma are looking at a single cup filled with a liquid. One of them is a worldly god; one of them is a human being; and one is a craving spirit.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"EM\", \"acip\": \"GZHAN YANG, MIG SHES LA SNGON PO'I RNAM PA SHAR BA DE PHYI ROL DON GYIS GTAD PA'I RNAM PA MA YIN TE YIN NA, CHU PHOR GANG LA LAS CAN GYI LHA MI YI DVAGS GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA'I TSE,\", \"wylie\": \"gzhan yang, mig shes la sngon po'i rnam pa shar ba de phyi rol don gyis gtad pa'i rnam pa ma yin te yin na, chu phor gang la las can gyi lha mi yi dvags gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa'i tshe\", \"english\": \"We can also argue it this way: When an image of blue appears to our visual perception, it's not as if some outer object is sending the image to our eyes. Because if it were this way, there's a problem in a situation where three beings with different karma are looking at a single cup filled with a liquid. One of them is a worldly god; one of them is a human being; and one is a craving spirit.\"}"
  },
  "34907": {
    "id": 34907,
    "course": "P7",
    "seq": 18,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA LHA MI YI DWAGS GSUM, GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA 'GRO BA RANG RANG GI NGO BOR THAD KA THAD KAR BDEN PAS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE YI DWAGS KYI NGO BOR RNAG KHRAG TU BDEN MI'I NGO BOR CHUR BDEN LHA'I NGO BOR BDUD RTZIR BDEN PA YIN NO ZER NA",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la lha mi yi dwags gsum, gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na 'gro ba rang rang gi ngo bor thad ka thad kar bden pas brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de yi dwags kyi ngo bor rnag khrag tu bden mi'i ngo bor chur bden lha'i ngo bor bdud rtsir bden pa yin no zer na",
    "english": "Someone else might make the following claim: Suppose a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit sit down together and look at a glass filled with water: the thing that we define as \"wet and flowing.\" Since to the perceptions of each different type of being it is real, the glass full of wet and flowing water is in reality pus and blood to the eyes of the craving spirit, and in reality water to the eyes of the human, and in reality ambrosia to the eyes of the pleasure being.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG LA LHA MI YI DWAGS GSUM, GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA 'GRO BA RANG RANG GI NGO BOR THAD KA THAD KAR BDEN PAS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA CHU PHOR PA GANG PO DE YI DWAGS KYI NGO BOR RNAG KHRAG TU BDEN MI'I NGO BOR CHUR BDEN LHA'I NGO BOR BDUD RTZIR BDEN PA YIN NO ZER NA\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang la lha mi yi dwags gsum, gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na 'gro ba rang rang gi ngo bor thad ka thad kar bden pas brlan zhing gsher ba chu phor pa gang po de yi dwags kyi ngo bor rnag khrag tu bden mi'i ngo bor chur bden lha'i ngo bor bdud rtsir bden pa yin no zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone else might make the following claim: Suppose a pleasure being, a human, and a craving spirit sit down together and look at a glass filled with water: the thing that we define as \\\"wet and flowing.\\\" Since to the perceptions of each different type of being it is real, the glass full of wet and flowing water is in reality pus and blood to the eyes of the craving spirit, and in reality water to the eyes of the human, and in reality ambrosia to the eyes of the pleasure being.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 0.982}"
  },
  "34917": {
    "id": 34917,
    "course": "P7",
    "seq": 28,
    "acip": "GNYIS PA DER THAL PHYOGS SNGA MAS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA 'GRO BA, GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN DU YANG KHAS MA BLANGS SKYES BU GCIG GI GZUGS MDZES MI MDZES GNYIS KA YIN PAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS YI DWAGS KYIS MIG SHES DANG CHU KLUNG GI BAR DU RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS SKYE BAR KHAS MA BLANGS YI DWAGS KYI MIG GIS RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS DANG CHU KLUNG GNYIS KA MTHONG BAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS LAS KYI DBANG GIS YI DWAGS KYI CHU KLUNG MA MTHONG BAR KHAS BLANGS PA'I PHYIR",
    "wylie": "gnyis pa der thal phyogs snga mas brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la 'gro ba, gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun du yang khas ma blangs skyes bu gcig gi gzugs mdzes mi mdzes gnyis ka yin par yang khas ma blangs yi dwags kyis mig shes dang chu klung gi bar du rnag khrag gi las gzugs skye bar khas ma blangs yi dwags kyi mig gis rnag khrag gi las gzugs dang chu klung gnyis ka mthong bar yang khas ma blangs las kyi dbang gis yi dwags kyi chu klung ma mthong bar khas blangs pa'i phyir",
    "english": "Our second point [that you have failed to comprehend the position we expressed above] is also quite true. Our original position was describing a situation where three different kinds of beings were sitting together and looking at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It is not our position that the glass full of something wet and flowing is one thing that is three different things. And it is not our position that there is such a thing as the physical appearance of a person which is at once both attractive and ugly. It is furthermore not our position that the blood and pus represent some kind of physical form which results from karma and grows up between the visual consciousness of the craving spirit and the stream of water. And it is not our position that the craving spirit's eyes see both this blood and pus as some kind of physical form resulting from karma, and the river of water at the same time. It is our position that, by force of his karma, the craving spirit is not able to see the stream of water.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"GNYIS PA DER THAL PHYOGS SNGA MAS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA 'GRO BA, GSUM GYIS CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG PO DE DNGOS PO GSUM GYI GZHI MTHUN DU YANG KHAS MA BLANGS SKYES BU GCIG GI GZUGS MDZES MI MDZES GNYIS KA YIN PAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS YI DWAGS KYIS MIG SHES DANG CHU KLUNG GI BAR DU RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS SKYE BAR KHAS MA BLANGS YI DWAGS KYI MIG GIS RNAG KHRAG GI LAS GZUGS DANG CHU KLUNG GNYIS KA MTHONG BAR YANG KHAS MA BLANGS LAS KYI DBANG GIS YI DWAGS KYI CHU KLUNG MA MTHONG BAR KHAS BLANGS PA'I PHYIR\", \"wylie\": \"gnyis pa der thal phyogs snga mas brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la 'gro ba, gsum gyis cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang po de dngos po gsum gyi gzhi mthun du yang khas ma blangs skyes bu gcig gi gzugs mdzes mi mdzes gnyis ka yin par yang khas ma blangs yi dwags kyis mig shes dang chu klung gi bar du rnag khrag gi las gzugs skye bar khas ma blangs yi dwags kyi mig gis rnag khrag gi las gzugs dang chu klung gnyis ka mthong bar yang khas ma blangs las kyi dbang gis yi dwags kyi chu klung ma mthong bar khas blangs pa'i phyir\", \"english\": \"Our second point [that you have failed to comprehend the position we expressed above] is also quite true. Our original position was describing a situation where three different kinds of beings were sitting together and looking at a glass full of something wet and flowing. It is not our position that the glass full of something wet and flowing is one thing that is three different things. And it is not our position that there is such a thing as the physical appearance of a person which is at once both attractive and ugly. It is furthermore not our position that the blood and pus represent some kind of physical form which results from karma and grows up between the visual consciousness of the craving spirit and the stream of water. And it is not our position that the craving spirit's eyes see both this blood and pus as some kind of physical form resulting from karma, and the river of water at the same time. It is our position that, by force of his karma, the craving spirit is not able to see the stream of water.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
  },
  "34919": {
    "id": 34919,
    "course": "P7",
    "seq": 30,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG NA RE DNGOS GCIG YID NI THA DAD PHYIR DON MA GRUB PAR 'DOD PA, YIN ZHES DNGOS PO GCIG LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BAR GSUNGS PA, MI 'THAD PAR THAL KHYOD LTAR NA DNGOS PO GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR DER THAL 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR ZER NA",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig na re dngos gcig yid ni tha dad phyir don ma grub par 'dod pa, yin zhes dngos po gcig la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung bar gsungs pa, mi 'thad par thal khyod ltar na dngos po gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir der thal 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na dngos po tshad grub pa gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir zer na",
    "english": "Someone else might come and make yet another claim: Let's talk about that quotation above, where it said: Insofar as [these different beings] have Of a single thing, we say it has no reality. The idea being expressed here is that a single object can be appearing in three different ways. This is incorrect because, according to you, what's happening is that three different objects are appearing in three different ways. And this certainly is your position; remember, you were describing a situation where three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something that's wet and flowing. You said that there were three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception, and that they were appearing in three different ways.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG NA RE DNGOS GCIG YID NI THA DAD PHYIR DON MA GRUB PAR 'DOD PA, YIN ZHES DNGOS PO GCIG LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BAR GSUNGS PA, MI 'THAD PAR THAL KHYOD LTAR NA DNGOS PO GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR DER THAL 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM LA SNANG TSUL MI 'DRA BA GSUM 'BYUNG BA'I PHYIR ZER NA\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig na re dngos gcig yid ni tha dad phyir don ma grub par 'dod pa, yin zhes dngos po gcig la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung bar gsungs pa, mi 'thad par thal khyod ltar na dngos po gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir der thal 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na dngos po tshad grub pa gsum la snang tshul mi 'dra ba gsum 'byung ba'i phyir zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone else might come and make yet another claim: Let's talk about that quotation above, where it said: Insofar as [these different beings] have Of a single thing, we say it has no reality. The idea being expressed here is that a single object can be appearing in three different ways. This is incorrect because, according to you, what's happening is that three different objects are appearing in three different ways. And this certainly is your position; remember, you were describing a situation where three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at a glass full of something that's wet and flowing. You said that there were three different objects, each confirmed by a valid perception, and that they were appearing in three different ways.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
  },
  "34920": {
    "id": 34920,
    "course": "P7",
    "seq": 31,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG NA RE RNAG KHRAG LA SOGS PA'I DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE PHAN TSUN GO SA, 'GOG PA'I DNGOS PO MA YIN PAR THAL LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYI CHU PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM SRID PAR KHAS BLANGS PA LA",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig na re rnag khrag la sogs pa'i dngos po gsum po de phan tshun go sa, 'gog pa'i dngos po ma yin par thal las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyi chu phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na dngos po tshad grub pa gsum srid par khas blangs pa la",
    "english": "Someone else may come now and make yet another argument: Let's talk about these three things: the pus and blood, and the other two. Are you implying then that these are not types of objects which would block each other from entering the space that each one occupies? After all, you were talking about a situation where those three types of beings, each with their own karma, sit down together and look at a glass full of water. And you said that your position was that it was possible for there to be three different objects there, each one confirmed by a valid",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG NA RE RNAG KHRAG LA SOGS PA'I DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE PHAN TSUN GO SA, 'GOG PA'I DNGOS PO MA YIN PAR THAL LAS CAN GYI 'GRO BA GSUM GYI CHU PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA DNGOS PO TSAD GRUB PA GSUM SRID PAR KHAS BLANGS PA LA\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig na re rnag khrag la sogs pa'i dngos po gsum po de phan tshun go sa, 'gog pa'i dngos po ma yin par thal las can gyi 'gro ba gsum gyi chu phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na dngos po tshad grub pa gsum srid par khas blangs pa la\", \"english\": \"Someone else may come now and make yet another argument: Let's talk about these three things: the pus and blood, and the other two. Are you implying then that these are not types of objects which would block each other from entering the space that each one occupies? After all, you were talking about a situation where those three types of beings, each with their own karma, sit down together and look at a glass full of water. And you said that your position was that it was possible for there to be three different objects there, each one confirmed by a valid\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
  },
  "34921": {
    "id": 34921,
    "course": "P7",
    "seq": 32,
    "acip": "YANG KHA CIG NA RE NYI SHU PA'I 'GREL BSHAD SLOB DPON DUL BA LHAS MDZAD PA LAS, DE LA RNAG GI THIGS PA 'GA' YANG MED NA RNAG GIS GANG BA'I KLUNG LTA GA LA YOD LAS KYI RNAM PAR SMIN PA'I DBANG GIS DE DAG GIS DE MTHONG NGO ZHES GSUNGS PA DE MI 'THAD PAR THAL 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA YI DWAGS KYI MIG SHES TSAD MAR SONG BA'I RNAG DNGOS GNAS PA YOD PA'I PHYIR ZER NA",
    "wylie": "yang kha cig na re nyi shu pa'i 'grel bshad slob dpon dul ba lhas mdzad pa las, de la rnag gi thigs pa 'ga' yang med na rnag gis gang ba'i klung lta ga la yod las kyi rnam par smin pa'i dbang gis de dag gis de mthong ngo zhes gsungs pa de mi 'thad par thal 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na yi dwags kyi mig shes tshad mar song ba'i rnag dngos gnas pa yod pa'i phyir zer na",
    "english": "Someone might make the following claim: In his Commentary to the Twenty Verses, Master Vinitadeva makes this statement— If there was not a single drop of pus there, then how could there ever be a whole river of pus? They are forced to see it, through the ripening of According to you, this statement would have to be mistaken, because when the three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing, the visual consciousness of the craving spirit is a valid perception, and the pus is real pus.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"YANG KHA CIG NA RE NYI SHU PA'I 'GREL BSHAD SLOB DPON DUL BA LHAS MDZAD PA LAS, DE LA RNAG GI THIGS PA 'GA' YANG MED NA RNAG GIS GANG BA'I KLUNG LTA GA LA YOD LAS KYI RNAM PAR SMIN PA'I DBANG GIS DE DAG GIS DE MTHONG NGO ZHES GSUNGS PA DE MI 'THAD PAR THAL 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS PA DE'I TSE NA YI DWAGS KYI MIG SHES TSAD MAR SONG BA'I RNAG DNGOS GNAS PA YOD PA'I PHYIR ZER NA\", \"wylie\": \"yang kha cig na re nyi shu pa'i 'grel bshad slob dpon dul ba lhas mdzad pa las, de la rnag gi thigs pa 'ga' yang med na rnag gis gang ba'i klung lta ga la yod las kyi rnam par smin pa'i dbang gis de dag gis de mthong ngo zhes gsungs pa de mi 'thad par thal 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas pa de'i tshe na yi dwags kyi mig shes tshad mar song ba'i rnag dngos gnas pa yod pa'i phyir zer na\", \"english\": \"Someone might make the following claim: In his Commentary to the Twenty Verses, Master Vinitadeva makes this statement— If there was not a single drop of pus there, then how could there ever be a whole river of pus? They are forced to see it, through the ripening of According to you, this statement would have to be mistaken, because when the three different kinds of beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing, the visual consciousness of the craving spirit is a valid perception, and the pus is real pus.\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
  },
  "34922": {
    "id": 34922,
    "course": "P7",
    "seq": 33,
    "acip": "SKYON MED DE DE LTA BU'I BRGAL LAN JI SNYED CIG BYAS PA THAMS CAD PHYI DON 'GOG, PA'I DBANG DU BYAS PA'I PHYIR PHYI DON 'GOG PA'I TSUL YANG YOD DE SNGON PO PHYI ROL DON DU GRUB NA TSUR MTHONG GI RGYUD KYI SNGON 'DZIN DBANG PO'I MNGON SUM LA SNGON PO SNANG BA NA SNGON 'DZIN SNGA MA 'GAGS PA'I TSE SNGON 'DZIN PHYI MA SKYE BA'I BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA BRTEN NAS SNANG BA MA YIN PAR SNGON PO PHYI ROL NAS RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG BA YIN NA 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS NAS DNGOS PO GSUM SNANG BA DE'I TSE NA RANG RANG GI LAS KYIS BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA MA BLTOS PAR DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG DGOS",
    "wylie": "skyon med de de lta bu'i brgal lan ji snyed cig byas pa thams cad phyi don 'gog, pa'i dbang du byas pa'i phyir phyi don 'gog pa'i tshul yang yod de sngon po phyi rol don du grub na tshur mthong gi rgyud kyi sngon 'dzin dbang po'i mngon sum la sngon po snang ba na sngon 'dzin snga ma 'gags pa'i tshe sngon 'dzin phyi ma skye ba'i bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la brten nas snang ba ma yin par sngon po phyi rol nas rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang ba yin na 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas nas dngos po gsum snang ba de'i tshe na rang rang gi las kyis bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la ma bltos par dngos po gsum po de rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang dgos",
    "english": "And yet there is no such problem. No matter how many arguments of this kind you want to present, they are all made from the point of view of denying the existence of external objects. The way these arguments go is as follows. If the color blue were to exist as an external object, then the following would occur when this color appeared directly to a sense perception grasping blue; that is, with such a perception found in the mental stream of one of those who \"only sees this side\" [which is another name for those who have not yet perceived emptiness directly]. When an earlier instance of the perception of blue ends, what actually happens is that it plants a mental seed which eventually grows into a later instance of the same perception of blue, when the seed ripens. Suppose the blue were not just this kind of appearance, but rather an appearance where blue as an outer object were transmitting a likeness of itself and thereby appearing to one's perceptions. Something else then would be happening when the three different beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing. The three different objects would be appearing to them because each of the objects was transmitting a likeness of itself to their perception. All of this would be happening independent of any process where each being's karma planted a mental seed, which later ripened and produced the appearance of the",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"P7\", \"acip\": \"SKYON MED DE DE LTA BU'I BRGAL LAN JI SNYED CIG BYAS PA THAMS CAD PHYI DON 'GOG, PA'I DBANG DU BYAS PA'I PHYIR PHYI DON 'GOG PA'I TSUL YANG YOD DE SNGON PO PHYI ROL DON DU GRUB NA TSUR MTHONG GI RGYUD KYI SNGON 'DZIN DBANG PO'I MNGON SUM LA SNGON PO SNANG BA NA SNGON 'DZIN SNGA MA 'GAGS PA'I TSE SNGON 'DZIN PHYI MA SKYE BA'I BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA BRTEN NAS SNANG BA MA YIN PAR SNGON PO PHYI ROL NAS RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG BA YIN NA 'GRO BA GSUM GYIS BRLAN ZHING GSHER BA PHOR PA GANG LA CIG CAR DU BLTAS NAS DNGOS PO GSUM SNANG BA DE'I TSE NA RANG RANG GI LAS KYIS BAG CHAGS BZHAG PA SMIN PA LA MA BLTOS PAR DNGOS PO GSUM PO DE RANG 'DRA'I RNAM PA GTAD PA'I SGO NAS SNANG DGOS\", \"wylie\": \"skyon med de de lta bu'i brgal lan ji snyed cig byas pa thams cad phyi don 'gog, pa'i dbang du byas pa'i phyir phyi don 'gog pa'i tshul yang yod de sngon po phyi rol don du grub na tshur mthong gi rgyud kyi sngon 'dzin dbang po'i mngon sum la sngon po snang ba na sngon 'dzin snga ma 'gags pa'i tshe sngon 'dzin phyi ma skye ba'i bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la brten nas snang ba ma yin par sngon po phyi rol nas rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang ba yin na 'gro ba gsum gyis brlan zhing gsher ba phor pa gang la cig car du bltas nas dngos po gsum snang ba de'i tshe na rang rang gi las kyis bag chags bzhag pa smin pa la ma bltos par dngos po gsum po de rang 'dra'i rnam pa gtad pa'i sgo nas snang dgos\", \"english\": \"And yet there is no such problem. No matter how many arguments of this kind you want to present, they are all made from the point of view of denying the existence of external objects. The way these arguments go is as follows. If the color blue were to exist as an external object, then the following would occur when this color appeared directly to a sense perception grasping blue; that is, with such a perception found in the mental stream of one of those who \\\"only sees this side\\\" [which is another name for those who have not yet perceived emptiness directly]. When an earlier instance of the perception of blue ends, what actually happens is that it plants a mental seed which eventually grows into a later instance of the same perception of blue, when the seed ripens. Suppose the blue were not just this kind of appearance, but rather an appearance where blue as an outer object were transmitting a likeness of itself and thereby appearing to one's perceptions. Something else then would be happening when the three different beings sit down together and look at the glass full of something wet and flowing. The three different objects would be appearing to them because each of the objects was transmitting a likeness of itself to their perception. All of this would be happening independent of any process where each being's karma planted a mental seed, which later ripened and produced the appearance of the\", \"wylie_method\": \"glyph-decode\", \"decode_score\": 1.0}"
  },
  "29638": {
    "id": 29638,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2319,
    "acip": "JI LTA BA DE LTAR DU, MI SDUG PA'I YID CAN GYI RNAL 'BYOR PA LTAR CIG SHOS TE RNAL 'BYOR PA MA YIN PAS, RNAL 'BYOR BAS KENG RUS MTHONG BA'I YUL DER BLO GTAD PA STE KENG RUS LA SOGS PA'I YUL LA LTA 'DOD PA RNAMS LA YANG KENG RUS RTOGS PA STE MTHONG BA SKYE BAR 'GYUR TE, SNGON PO SOGS KYI RNAM SHES BZHIN NO,,",
    "wylie": "ji lta ba de ltar du, mi sdug pa'i yid can gyi rnal 'byor pa ltar cig shos te rnal 'byor pa ma yin pas, rnal 'byor bas keng rus mthong ba'i yul der blo gtad pa ste keng rus la sogs pa'i yul la lta 'dod pa rnams la yang keng rus rtogs pa ste mthong ba skye bar 'gyur te, sngon po sogs kyi rnam shes bzhin no",
    "english": "Just so—in the same way—we can have a case where there is a certain other individual who is not a deep practitioner; which is to say, they are not like the deep practitioner whose mind has taken on an aspect of the repulsive. It should be the case then that if they decide they would like to look upon the skeletons (or whatever else it may be)—if they were to direct their minds towards the same objects where the deep practitioner perceives skeletons—then a perception of the skeletons, that sight, should also arise in them. It would be just the same as the perception of blue and the like.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"JI LTA BA DE LTAR DU, MI SDUG PA'I YID CAN GYI RNAL 'BYOR PA LTAR CIG SHOS TE RNAL 'BYOR PA MA YIN PAS, RNAL 'BYOR BAS KENG RUS MTHONG BA'I YUL DER BLO GTAD PA STE KENG RUS LA SOGS PA'I YUL LA LTA 'DOD PA RNAMS LA YANG KENG RUS RTOGS PA STE MTHONG BA SKYE BAR 'GYUR TE, SNGON PO SOGS KYI RNAM SHES BZHIN NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"ji lta ba de ltar du, mi sdug pa'i yid can gyi rnal 'byor pa ltar cig shos te rnal 'byor pa ma yin pas, rnal 'byor bas keng rus mthong ba'i yul der blo gtad pa ste keng rus la sogs pa'i yul la lta 'dod pa rnams la yang keng rus rtogs pa ste mthong ba skye bar 'gyur te, sngon po sogs kyi rnam shes bzhin no\", \"english\": \"Just so—in the same way—we can have a case where there is a certain other individual who is not a deep practitioner; which is to say, they are not like the deep practitioner whose mind has taken on an aspect of the repulsive. It should be the case then that if they decide they would like to look upon the skeletons (or whatever else it may be)—if they were to direct their minds towards the same objects where the deep practitioner perceives skeletons—then a perception of the skeletons, that sight, should also arise in them. It would be just the same as the perception of blue and the like.\"}"
  },
  "29639": {
    "id": 29639,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2320,
    "acip": "'DIR 'GREL PA DANG BSTUN NA, ,KHYOD KYI DBANG BLO'I YUL GTAD JI LTA BA, ,DE LTAR MI SDUG YID CAN DE BZHIN DU, ,YUL DER BLO GTAD CIG SHOS RNAMS LA YANG, ,SKYE 'GYUR DE NI BRDZUN PAR YANG MI 'GYUR, ZHES PA'I 'GYUR CIG THOB PAR SNANG NGO,,",
    "wylie": "'dir 'grel pa dang bstun na, ,khyod kyi dbang blo'i yul gtad ji lta ba, ,de ltar mi sdug yid can de bzhin du, ,yul der blo gtad cig shos rnams la yang, ,skye 'gyur de ni brdzun par yang mi 'gyur, zhes pa'i 'gyur cig thob par snang ngo",
    "english": "If we were to match these thoughts up to the autocommentary at this point, then it would seem that we could come up with a translation of the same lines as follows— You direct your awareness Of the powers of sense In a certain way, And they take on thus A certain aspect; Just so, When the mind is directed Towards a certain object, Then it should arise For the other ones as well; Neither would this be Deceptive.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"'DIR 'GREL PA DANG BSTUN NA, ,KHYOD KYI DBANG BLO'I YUL GTAD JI LTA BA, ,DE LTAR MI SDUG YID CAN DE BZHIN DU, ,YUL DER BLO GTAD CIG SHOS RNAMS LA YANG, ,SKYE 'GYUR DE NI BRDZUN PAR YANG MI 'GYUR, ZHES PA'I 'GYUR CIG THOB PAR SNANG NGO,,\", \"wylie\": \"'dir 'grel pa dang bstun na, ,khyod kyi dbang blo'i yul gtad ji lta ba, ,de ltar mi sdug yid can de bzhin du, ,yul der blo gtad cig shos rnams la yang, ,skye 'gyur de ni brdzun par yang mi 'gyur, zhes pa'i 'gyur cig thob par snang ngo\", \"english\": \"If we were to match these thoughts up to the autocommentary at this point, then it would seem that we could come up with a translation of the same lines as follows— You direct your awareness Of the powers of sense In a certain way, And they take on thus A certain aspect; Just so, When the mind is directed Towards a certain object, Then it should arise For the other ones as well; Neither would this be Deceptive.\"}"
  },
  "29640": {
    "id": 29640,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2321,
    "acip": "TING NGE 'DZIN 'DI BRDZUN PAR TE LOG PA'I DON YID LA BYED PAR YANG MI 'GYUR RO,,",
    "wylie": "ting nge 'dzin 'di brdzun par te log pa'i don yid la byed par yang mi 'gyur ro",
    "english": "Neither is it the case that this meditative concentration would be deceptive; which is to say, you would not be directing your mind towards something mistaken.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"TING NGE 'DZIN 'DI BRDZUN PAR TE LOG PA'I DON YID LA BYED PAR YANG MI 'GYUR RO,,\", \"wylie\": \"ting nge 'dzin 'di brdzun par te log pa'i don yid la byed par yang mi 'gyur ro\", \"english\": \"Neither is it the case that this meditative concentration would be deceptive; which is to say, you would not be directing your mind towards something mistaken.\"}"
  },
  "29641": {
    "id": 29641,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2322,
    "acip": "RNAL 'BYOR PA YIN MIN MGO MTSUNGS MDZAD PA YANG, SHES PA RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PA YIN NA KENG RUS KYI MAN NGAG SGOM PA LA LTOS PA 'GOG NUS PA'I GNAD KYIS YIN NO,,",
    "wylie": "rnal 'byor pa yin min mgo mtshungs mdzad pa yang, shes pa rang bzhin gyis grub pa yin na keng rus kyi man ngag sgom pa la ltos pa 'gog nus pa'i gnad kyis yin no",
    "english": "This is true because of a crucial point here. Even if we were to say that the two observers were the same—in either being a deep practitioner, or not—in a case where their states of mind existed through some nature of their own, we would be able to deny that they were relying on a meditation upon the advices for the practice of the skeletons.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"RNAL 'BYOR PA YIN MIN MGO MTSUNGS MDZAD PA YANG, SHES PA RANG BZHIN GYIS GRUB PA YIN NA KENG RUS KYI MAN NGAG SGOM PA LA LTOS PA 'GOG NUS PA'I GNAD KYIS YIN NO,,\", \"wylie\": \"rnal 'byor pa yin min mgo mtshungs mdzad pa yang, shes pa rang bzhin gyis grub pa yin na keng rus kyi man ngag sgom pa la ltos pa 'gog nus pa'i gnad kyis yin no\", \"english\": \"This is true because of a crucial point here. Even if we were to say that the two observers were the same—in either being a deep practitioner, or not—in a case where their states of mind existed through some nature of their own, we would be able to deny that they were relying on a meditation upon the advices for the practice of the skeletons.\"}"
  },
  "29643": {
    "id": 29643,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2324,
    "acip": "DE BZHIN DU RAB RIB DANG LDAN PA'I DBANG PO CAN DANG MTSUNGS PA'I SGYU MA DANG GZUGS BRNYAN SOGS DPER 'GOD PA LA YANG LAN GDAB TSUL SHES PAR BYA LA, CHU 'BAB PA'I KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAMS LA RNAG KHRAG GI BLO SKYE BA LA YANG SNGA MA BZHIN DU SHES PAR BYA'O,,",
    "wylie": "de bzhin du rab rib dang ldan pa'i dbang po can dang mtshungs pa'i sgyu ma dang gzugs brnyan sogs dper 'god pa la yang lan gdab tshul shes par bya la, chu 'bab pa'i klung la yi dvags rnams la rnag khrag gi blo skye ba la yang snga ma bzhin du shes par bya'o",
    "english": "You can understand, in the same way, how to reply as well to cases where someone sets forth—as an example—an illusion, or a reflection in a mirror, as being equivalent to a state of mind with a sense power that has cataracts. And you should also understand, in the same way, the illustration of when a state of mind arises in craving spirits wherein they perceive a stream of fresh water as being pus & blood.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE BZHIN DU RAB RIB DANG LDAN PA'I DBANG PO CAN DANG MTSUNGS PA'I SGYU MA DANG GZUGS BRNYAN SOGS DPER 'GOD PA LA YANG LAN GDAB TSUL SHES PAR BYA LA, CHU 'BAB PA'I KLUNG LA YI DVAGS RNAMS LA RNAG KHRAG GI BLO SKYE BA LA YANG SNGA MA BZHIN DU SHES PAR BYA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"de bzhin du rab rib dang ldan pa'i dbang po can dang mtshungs pa'i sgyu ma dang gzugs brnyan sogs dper 'god pa la yang lan gdab tshul shes par bya la, chu 'bab pa'i klung la yi dvags rnams la rnag khrag gi blo skye ba la yang snga ma bzhin du shes par bya'o\", \"english\": \"You can understand, in the same way, how to reply as well to cases where someone sets forth—as an example—an illusion, or a reflection in a mirror, as being equivalent to a state of mind with a sense power that has cataracts. And you should also understand, in the same way, the illustration of when a state of mind arises in craving spirits wherein they perceive a stream of fresh water as being pus & blood.\"}"
  },
  "29644": {
    "id": 29644,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2325,
    "acip": "DE LA CHOS KYI SKYE MCHED KYI GZUGS LNGA NI DNGOS POR SMRA BA'I GRUB MTHAS BTAGS PA MIN GYI, MDO LAS GSUNGS SHING RANG NYID KYANG DE LTAR DU BZHED DO,,",
    "wylie": "de la chos kyi skye mched kyi gzugs lnga ni dngos por smra ba'i grub mthas btags pa min gyi, mdo las gsungs shing rang nyid kyang de ltar du bzhed do",
    "english": "Now the five types of form relating to the sense door of all things are not something simply imputed by the school of thought known as the Functionalists. Rather, they have been stated in sutra, and we also accept them as being such.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE LA CHOS KYI SKYE MCHED KYI GZUGS LNGA NI DNGOS POR SMRA BA'I GRUB MTHAS BTAGS PA MIN GYI, MDO LAS GSUNGS SHING RANG NYID KYANG DE LTAR DU BZHED DO,,\", \"wylie\": \"de la chos kyi skye mched kyi gzugs lnga ni dngos por smra ba'i grub mthas btags pa min gyi, mdo las gsungs shing rang nyid kyang de ltar du bzhed do\", \"english\": \"Now the five types of form relating to the sense door of all things are not something simply imputed by the school of thought known as the Functionalists. Rather, they have been stated in sutra, and we also accept them as being such.\"}"
  },
  "29645": {
    "id": 29645,
    "course": "ILL",
    "seq": 2326,
    "acip": "DE'I PHYIR KENG RUS MED KYANG DE GSAL BAR SNANG BA NI, GZUGS BRNYAN DANG 'DRA BAR GZUGS CAN DU 'DOD DGOS LA, 'DI YID SHES KHO NA LA SNANG BA YIN PAS, GZUGS BRNYAN BZHIN DU GZUGS KYI SKYE MCHED MIN LA, SKYE MCHED GZUGS CAN LHAG MA DGU YANG MIN PAS CHOS KYI SKYE MCHED KYI GZUGS KUN BRTAGS PA ZHES 'BYUNG BA'O,,",
    "wylie": "de'i phyir keng rus med kyang de gsal bar snang ba ni, gzugs brnyan dang 'dra bar gzugs can du 'dod dgos la, 'di yid shes kho na la snang ba yin pas, gzugs brnyan bzhin du gzugs kyi skye mched min la, skye mched gzugs can lhag ma dgu yang min pas chos kyi skye mched kyi gzugs kun brtags pa zhes 'byung ba'o",
    "english": "As such, even though the skeletons don't really exist, we do have to accept that the vivid aspect of them does exist; and that it involves form, in the same way that a reflection in the mirror does. And so despite the fact that the image of the skeletons is not, like the reflection in a mirror, something possessed of form, neither does it belong to any of the remaining nine doors of sense of form. And this means that it constitutes form within the sense door of all things, and can be referred to as an imaginary form.",
    "raw": "{\"course\": \"ILL\", \"acip\": \"DE'I PHYIR KENG RUS MED KYANG DE GSAL BAR SNANG BA NI, GZUGS BRNYAN DANG 'DRA BAR GZUGS CAN DU 'DOD DGOS LA, 'DI YID SHES KHO NA LA SNANG BA YIN PAS, GZUGS BRNYAN BZHIN DU GZUGS KYI SKYE MCHED MIN LA, SKYE MCHED GZUGS CAN LHAG MA DGU YANG MIN PAS CHOS KYI SKYE MCHED KYI GZUGS KUN BRTAGS PA ZHES 'BYUNG BA'O,,\", \"wylie\": \"de'i phyir keng rus med kyang de gsal bar snang ba ni, gzugs brnyan dang 'dra bar gzugs can du 'dod dgos la, 'di yid shes kho na la snang ba yin pas, gzugs brnyan bzhin du gzugs kyi skye mched min la, skye mched gzugs can lhag ma dgu yang min pas chos kyi skye mched kyi gzugs kun brtags pa zhes 'byung ba'o\", \"english\": \"As such, even though the skeletons don't really exist, we do have to accept that the vivid aspect of them does exist; and that it involves form, in the same way that a reflection in the mirror does. And so despite the fact that the image of the skeletons is not, like the reflection in a mirror, something possessed of form, neither does it belong to any of the remaining nine doors of sense of form. And this means that it constitutes form within the sense door of all things, and can be referred to as an imaginary form.\"}"
  }
}
